2.2 Orthogonal Projection
The coefficient used in the proof of Cauchy–Schwarz has a geometric meaning. It selects the point on the line through \(\by\) that is closest to \(\bx\).






NoteDefinition: Projection onto a Nonzero Vector



For \(\by\neq\bzero\), the orthogonal projection of \(\bx\) onto the line spanned by \(\by\) is \[
\begin{align}
\operatorname{proj}_{\by}(\bx)
  =\frac{\langle\bx,\by\rangle}{\langle\by,\by\rangle}\by.
\end{align}
\] The residual \(\bx-\operatorname{proj}_{\by}(\bx)\) is orthogonal to \(\by\).








NoteTheorem: Closest Point on a Line



Let \(\by\neq\bzero\) and let \[
\begin{align}
\alpha=\frac{\langle\bx,\by\rangle}{\langle\by,\by\rangle}.
\end{align}
\] Then \(\alpha\by\) is the unique closest point to \(\bx\) among all scalar multiples of \(\by\): \[
\begin{align}
\|\bx-\alpha\by\|\leq\|\bx-\beta\by\|
  \qquad\text{for every }\beta\in\fR.
\end{align}
\]








TipProof





Let \(\alpha=\langle\bx,\by\rangle/\langle\by,\by\rangle\) and set \(\br=\bx-\alpha\by\). The assumption \(\by\neq\bzero\) makes the denominator positive.

Compute \[
\begin{align}
\langle\br,\by\rangle
  &=\langle\bx,\by\rangle-\alpha\langle\by,\by\rangle=0.
\end{align}
\] Thus \(\alpha\by\) has the required perpendicular residual.
For any real \(\beta\), \[
\begin{align}
\bx-\beta\by=\br+(\alpha-\beta)\by.
\end{align}
\] Expand the squared norm. The cross term is zero by the preceding calculation: \[
\begin{align}
\|\bx-\beta\by\|^2
  &=\|\br\|^2+2(\alpha-\beta)\langle\br,\by\rangle
       +(\alpha-\beta)^2\|\by\|^2\\
  &=\|\br\|^2+(\alpha-\beta)^2\|\by\|^2\geq\|\br\|^2.
\end{align}
\]
Since \(\|\by\|^2&gt;0\), equality holds only when \(\beta=\alpha\). Therefore \(\alpha\by\) is the unique closest point on the line.










NoteDefinition: Orthogonal Complement



For a subspace \(S\subseteq\fR^n\), its orthogonal complement is \[
\begin{align}
S^\perp=\{\bz\in\fR^n:\langle\bz,\bs\rangle=0\text{ for every }\bs\in S\}.
\end{align}
\]








NoteTheorem: Projection onto a Subspace with an Orthonormal Basis



Let the columns of \(\bQ\in\fR^{n\times k}\) be an orthonormal basis for a subspace \(S\). Then \[
\begin{align}
\bP=\bQ\bQ^T
\end{align}
\] is the orthogonal projector onto \(S\). Every vector has the unique decomposition \[
\begin{align}
\bx=\bP\bx+(\bI-\bP)\bx,
  \qquad
  \bP\bx\in S,
  \qquad
  (\bI-\bP)\bx\in S^\perp.
\end{align}
\] Moreover, \(\bP\bx\) is the unique point in \(S\) closest to \(\bx\).








TipProof





Let \(\bQ\) contain the orthonormal basis vectors of \(S\), so \(\bQ^T\bQ=\bI\), and let \(\bP=\bQ\bQ^T\).

The vector \(\bQ^T\bx\) is a list of coefficients. Therefore \(\bP\bx=\bQ(\bQ^T\bx)\) is a linear combination of the columns of \(\bQ\) and lies in \(S\).
First calculate \[
\begin{align}
\bQ^T(\bx-\bP\bx)
  &=\bQ^T\bx-\bQ^T\bQ\bQ^T\bx=\bzero.
\end{align}
\] Every \(\bs\in S\) has the form \(\bs=\bQ\bc\). Hence \[
\begin{align}
\bs^T(\bx-\bP\bx)=\bc^T\bQ^T(\bx-\bP\bx)=0.
\end{align}
\]
For any \(\bs\in S\), write \[
\begin{align}
\bx-\bs=(\bx-\bP\bx)+(\bP\bx-\bs).
\end{align}
\] The first term is perpendicular to \(S\), and the second belongs to \(S\). Pythagoras gives \[
\begin{align}
\|\bx-\bs\|_2^2=\|\bx-\bP\bx\|_2^2+\|\bP\bx-\bs\|_2^2.
\end{align}
\] The second summand is nonnegative and vanishes only when \(\bs=\bP\bx\). This proves both minimality and uniqueness.










WarningExercise



Orthogonal versus oblique projection. Let \[
\begin{align}
\bP=\begin{pmatrix}1&-2\\0&0\end{pmatrix}.
\end{align}
\] Verify that \(\bP^2=\bP\) but \(\bP^T\neq\bP\). Find its range and null space. Give a vector for which \(\bP\bx\) is not the closest point in the range of \(\bP\).








WarningExercise



Projection and uniqueness. Let \(S\) have orthonormal basis matrix \(\bQ\). Prove directly that \(\bP=\bQ\bQ^T\) satisfies \(\bP^T=\bP\) and \(\bP^2=\bP\). Then prove that these two identities characterize orthogonal projectors.








TipRemark



For an orthonormal basis, the projection coefficients are simply the inner products \(\bQ^T\bx\). If the basis columns are stored in a general full-rank matrix \(\bV\), the closest vector \(\bV\bc\) instead satisfies \[
\begin{align}
\bV^T\bV\bc=\bV^T\bx.
\end{align}
\] The matrix \(\bV^T\bV\) records all pairwise inner products between the basis vectors. Orthogonality reduces this matrix to the identity. This is the first reason orthogonal bases simplify computation.

3.4 Matrix Condition Numbers
An invertible matrix can stretch some directions and compress others. A solve with that matrix must undo both effects. The condition number measures the total distortion.






NoteDefinition: Condition Number



For a nonsingular square matrix \(\bA\) and an induced norm, \[
\begin{align}
\kappa(\bA)=\|\bA\|\,\|\bA^{-1}\|.
\end{align}
\]








NoteTheorem: Two-Sided Stretching Bound



For every nonzero vector \(\bx\), \[
\begin{align}
\frac{1}{\|\bA^{-1}\|}
  \leq
  \frac{\|\bA\bx\|}{\|\bx\|}
  \leq
  \|\bA\|.
\end{align}
\] The ratio between the largest possible upper stretch and the guaranteed lower stretch is \(\kappa(\bA)\).








TipProof





The assumption that \(\bA\) is nonsingular is needed for the lower estimate. Fix \(\bx\neq\bzero\).

By the definition of the induced norm, the ratio for this particular input cannot exceed the largest possible ratio: \[
\begin{align}
\frac{\|\bA\bx\|}{\|\bx\|}\leq
  \sup_{\bz\neq\bzero}\frac{\|\bA\bz\|}{\|\bz\|}=\|\bA\|.
\end{align}
\]
Undo the action of \(\bA\) and apply consistency to its inverse: \[
\begin{align}
\bx&=\bA^{-1}(\bA\bx),\\
  \|\bx\|&\leq\|\bA^{-1}\|\|\bA\bx\|.
\end{align}
\] Both \(\|\bx\|\) and \(\|\bA^{-1}\|\) are positive. Dividing by their product gives \[
\begin{align}
\frac1{\|\bA^{-1}\|}\leq\frac{\|\bA\bx\|}{\|\bx\|}.
\end{align}
\]
The upper stretch is \(\|\bA\|\) and the lower stretch is \(1/\|\bA^{-1}\|\). Dividing the former by the latter gives \(\|\bA\|\|\bA^{-1}\|=\kappa(\bA)\).










WarningExercise



Conditioning as a thin ellipse. Let \(\bA=\operatorname{diag}(1,\varepsilon)\) with \(0&lt;\varepsilon&lt;1\). Describe the image of the Euclidean unit circle, compute \(\|\bA\|_2\), \(\|\bA^{-1}\|_2\), and \(\kappa_2(\bA)\) directly from the definition, and explain which component of a solution is sensitive to perturbations.


If \(\kappa(\bA)\) is near one, the matrix treats every direction comparably. If it is large, at least one direction is compressed relative to another. Recovering a component in a strongly compressed direction requires amplification by \(\bA^{-1}\). Consequently, ill-conditioned linear systems can turn small data errors into large solution errors. the referenced result makes this statement quantitative.

6.2 Invertibility and the Four Fundamental Subspaces
The solvability and uniqueness of solutions are governed by four fundamental subspaces associated with \(\bA\).






NoteDefinition: The Four Fundamental Subspaces



Let \(\bA \in \fR^{m \times n}\).

The Column Space (Range) \(\mathcal{R}(\bA) = \{ \bA\bx : \bx \in \fR^n \} \subseteq \fR^m\).
The Null Space (Kernel) \(\mathcal{N}(\bA) = \{ \bx \in \fR^n : \bA\bx = \bzero \} \subseteq \fR^n\).
The Row Space \(\mathcal{R}(\bA^T) = \{ \bA^T\by : \by \in \fR^m \} \subseteq \fR^n\).
The Left Null Space \(\mathcal{N}(\bA^T) = \{ \by \in \fR^m : \bA^T\by = \bzero \} \subseteq \fR^m\).

The rank \(r = \operatorname{rank}(\bA)\) is the dimension of the column space \(\mathcal{R}(\bA)\).








NoteTheorem: The Rank-Nullity Theorem



Let \(\bA \in \fR^{m \times n}\) with rank \(r = \dim \mathcal{R}(\bA)\). Then \[
\begin{align}
\operatorname{rank}(\bA) + \dim \mathcal{N}(\bA) = n.
\end{align}
\]








TipProof





Let \(k = \dim \mathcal{N}(\bA)\), and choose a basis \(\{\bv_1, ..., \bv_k\}\) for the null space. Extend this list to a basis \(\{\bv_1, ..., \bv_n\}\) of \(\fR^n\). The basis-extension theorem permits this choice because the null space is a subspace of \(\fR^n\). The first \(k\) basis vectors are precisely those on which the matrix vanishes. We will show that the images of the other \(n-k\) vectors form a basis of the range.
For any \(\bx \in \fR^n\), expand \(\bx = \sum_{i=1}^n c_i \bv_i\). Linearity gives \[
\begin{align}
\bA\bx = \sum_{i=1}^k c_i \bA\bv_i + \sum_{i=k+1}^n c_i \bA\bv_i = \bzero + \sum_{i=k+1}^n c_i \bA\bv_i = \sum_{i=k+1}^n c_i \bA\bv_i.
\end{align}
\] Thus the vectors \(\{\bA\bv_{k+1}, ..., \bA\bv_n\}\) span \(\mathcal{R}(\bA)\). Spanning alone would only bound the rank from above. We must also rule out dependence among these output vectors before concluding that their number equals the rank.
To establish linear independence, suppose \(\sum_{i=k+1}^n d_i \bA\bv_i = \bzero\). Linearity yields \[
\begin{align}
\bA\left(\sum_{i=k+1}^n d_i \bv_i\right) = \bzero \quad \Longrightarrow \quad \bw = \sum_{i=k+1}^n d_i \bv_i \in \mathcal{N}(\bA).
\end{align}
\] Because \(\{\bv_1, ..., \bv_k\}\) is a basis for \(\mathcal{N}(\bA)\), there exist coefficients \(a_1, ..., a_k\) such that \(\bw = \sum_{j=1}^k a_j \bv_j\). Equating the two expressions gives \[
\begin{align}
\sum_{j=1}^k a_j \bv_j - \sum_{i=k+1}^n d_i \bv_i = \bzero.
\end{align}
\] Since the full set \(\{\bv_1, ..., \bv_n\}\) is linearly independent, every coefficient vanishes: \[
\begin{align}
a_1 = ... = a_k = d_{k+1} = ... = d_n = 0.
\end{align}
\] Therefore \(\{\bA\bv_{k+1}, ..., \bA\bv_n\}\) is a basis for \(\mathcal{R}(\bA)\), containing exactly \(n - k\) vectors. Hence \[
\begin{align}
r = n - k = n - \dim\mathcal{N}(\bA) \quad \Longrightarrow \quad \operatorname{rank}(\bA) + \dim \mathcal{N}(\bA) = n.
\end{align}
\]









NoteTheorem: Fundamental Theorem of Linear Algebra and Orthogonal Decompositions



For any matrix \(\bA \in \fR^{m \times n}\):

The row space and null space are orthogonal complements in \(\fR^n\): \[
\begin{align}
\mathcal{R}(\bA^T) \perp \mathcal{N}(\bA) \qquad \text{and} \qquad \fR^n = \mathcal{R}(\bA^T) \oplus \mathcal{N}(\bA).
\end{align}
\]
The column space and left null space are orthogonal complements in \(\fR^m\): \[
\begin{align}
\mathcal{R}(\bA) \perp \mathcal{N}(\bA^T) \qquad \text{and} \qquad \fR^m = \mathcal{R}(\bA) \oplus \mathcal{N}(\bA^T).
\end{align}
\]

Consequently, \(\dim \mathcal{R}(\bA^T) = \dim \mathcal{R}(\bA) = \operatorname{rank}(\bA)\).








TipProof





Let the rows of \(\bA\) be \(\ba_1^T,...,\ba_m^T\). The equation \(\bA\bx=\bzero\) is equivalent to the \(m\) scalar equations \(\ba_i^T\bx=0\). Thus a vector belongs to the null space exactly when it is perpendicular to every row.
A vector perpendicular to each row is perpendicular to every linear combination of rows, because \[
\begin{align}
\left(\sum_i c_i\ba_i\right)^T\bx=\sum_i c_i\ba_i^T\bx=0.
\end{align}
\] Conversely, being perpendicular to the row span implies being perpendicular to each individual row. Since this span is \(\mathcal R(\bA^T)\), \[
\begin{align}
\mathcal N(\bA)=\mathcal R(\bA^T)^\perp.
\end{align}
\] In a finite-dimensional inner-product space, \(S^{\perp\perp}=S\): extend an orthonormal basis of \(S\) to one of the entire space, and the remaining basis vectors span \(S^\perp\). Taking the complement again returns the original basis vectors. Hence \[
\begin{align}
\mathcal N(\bA)^\perp=\mathcal R(\bA^T).
\end{align}
\] The same calculation applied to the matrix \(\bA^T\) gives \(\mathcal N(\bA^T)=\mathcal R(\bA)^\perp\) and its equivalent reversed-complement identity.
The orthonormal-basis construction also proves the direct-sum decompositions and the dimension rule \(\dim S+\dim S^\perp=n\) in \(\fR^n\). Therefore \[
\begin{align}
\dim\mathcal R(\bA^T)
=n-\dim\mathcal N(\bA)
=\operatorname{rank}(\bA),
\end{align}
\] where the last equality is rank–nullity. Thus row rank equals column rank.









NoteTheorem: Solvability and the Left Null Space



The linear system \(\bA\bx = \bb\) has a solution if and only if \(\bb \in \mathcal{R}(\bA)\). Equivalently, \(\bA\bx = \bb\) is solvable if and only if \(\bb\) is orthogonal to every vector in the left null space of \(\bA\): \[
\begin{align}
\bA\bx = \bb \text{ has a solution} \iff \by^T\bb = 0 \quad \text{for all } \by \text{ such that } \bA^T\by = \bzero.
\end{align}
\] If a solution exists and \(\bx_p\) is one particular solution, the complete set of solutions is the affine space \[
\begin{align}
\bx = \bx_p + \bz, \qquad \bz \in \mathcal{N}(\bA).
\end{align}
\]








TipProof





Solvability means that \(\bb\) can be produced as an output \(\bA\bx\). By the definition of the range, this is exactly \(\bb\in\mathcal R(\bA)\). The preceding theorem identifies that range with \(\mathcal N(\bA^T)^\perp\). Consequently \[
\begin{align}
\bA\bx=\bb\text{ is solvable}
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
\by^T\bb=0\text{ for every }\by\text{ with }\bA^T\by=\bzero.
\end{align}
\] One direction can also be checked directly: if \(\bb=\bA\bx\), then \(\by^T\bb=(\bA^T\by)^T\bx=0\). The subspace theorem supplies the converse, which is not obtained by this calculation alone.
Now suppose a solution \(\bx_p\) has been found. For any \(\bz\in\mathcal N(\bA)\), \[
\begin{align}
\bA(\bx_p+\bz)=\bA\bx_p+\bA\bz=\bb+\bzero=\bb.
\end{align}
\] Thus every vector in \(\bx_p+\mathcal N(\bA)\) is a solution.
To prove that these are all the solutions, take any \(\bx\) satisfying the equations and subtract the equations for \(\bx_p\): \[
\begin{align}
\bA(\bx-\bx_p)=\bA\bx-\bA\bx_p=\bb-\bb=\bzero.
\end{align}
\] Hence \(\bz=\bx-\bx_p\) belongs to the null space and \(\bx=\bx_p+\bz\). A trivial null space therefore gives uniqueness. A nonzero null-space vector gives distinct solutions by adding its scalar multiples.









WarningExercise



The Fredholm alternative and inconsistent systems. Consider the \(3 \times 2\) system \[
\begin{align}
\bA = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 \\ 1 & 2 \\ 2 & 3 \end{pmatrix}, \qquad \bb = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \\ b_3 \end{pmatrix}.
\end{align}
\]

Compute a basis for the left null space \(\mathcal{N}(\bA^T)\).
Apply the referenced result to find the exact value of \(b_3\) that makes the system consistent.
For \(b_3 = 5\), find the orthogonal projection of \(\bb\) onto \(\mathcal{R}(\bA)\) and compute the minimum residual norm \(\min_{\bx} \|\bA\bx - \bb\|_2\).









TipRemark



This compatibility condition is often called the finite-dimensional Fredholm alternative. Each left-null vector describes one obstruction that a solvable right-hand side must avoid.

NoteDefinition: Forward Error, Residual, and Backward Error



For a computed approximation \(\hat{\bx}\):

The forward error is the vector difference \(\Delta\bx = \hat{\bx} - \bx^*\), with relative measure \(\frac{\|\hat{\bx} - \bx^*\|}{\|\bx^*\|}\).
The residual is the vector defect \(\br = \bb - \bA\hat{\bx}\), with relative measure \(\frac{\|\br\|}{\|\bA\|\|\hat{\bx}\| + \|\bb\|}\).
The backward error is the smallest perturbation \((\delta\bA, \delta\bb)\) such that \(\hat{\bx}\) is the exact solution to the perturbed system \((\bA + \delta\bA)\hat{\bx} = \bb + \delta\bb\).










NoteTheorem: The Fundamental Residual-Error Bound



Let \(\bA \in \fR^{n \times n}\) be nonsingular, let \(\bb \neq \bzero\), and let \(\bx^*\) satisfy \(\bA\bx^* = \bb\). For any vector \(\hat{\bx}\) with residual \(\br = \bb - \bA\hat{\bx}\), the relative forward error satisfies \[
\begin{align}
\frac{1}{\kappa(\bA)}\frac{\|\br\|}{\|\bb\|}
    \leq
    \frac{\|\hat{\bx} - \bx^*\|}{\|\bx^*\|}
    \leq
    \kappa(\bA) \frac{\|\br\|}{\|\bb\|},
\end{align}
\] where \(\kappa(\bA) = \|\bA\|\|\bA^{-1}\|\) is the condition number of \(\bA\).








TipProof





Let \(\be=\hat{\bx}-\bx^*\). Subtract the exact and approximate matrix products: \[
\begin{align}
\br=\bb-\bA\hat{\bx}
=\bA\bx^*-\bA\hat{\bx}=-\bA\be.
\end{align}
\] Thus \(\be=-\bA^{-1}\br\). The sign has no effect on a norm.
For the upper error bound, consistency applied to this last identity gives \(\|\be\|\leq\|\bA^{-1}\|\|\br\|\). We need to normalize by the unknown exact solution norm. Since \(\bb=\bA\bx^*\), \[
\begin{align}
\|\bb\|\leq\|\bA\|\|\bx^*\|,
\qquad \frac1{\|\bx^*\|}\leq\frac{\|\bA\|}{\|\bb\|}.
\end{align}
\] The divisions are legitimate because \(\bb\neq\bzero\) and invertibility makes \(\bx^*\neq\bzero\). Multiply the two estimates: \[
\begin{align}
\frac{\|\be\|}{\|\bx^*\|}
\leq\|\bA^{-1}\|\|\br\|\frac{\|\bA\|}{\|\bb\|}
=\kappa(\bA)\frac{\|\br\|}{\|\bb\|}.
\end{align}
\] For the lower bound, use \(\br=-\bA\be\) to obtain \(\|\be\|\geq\|\br\|/\|\bA\|\). Also \(\bx^*=\bA^{-1}\bb\) gives \(\|\bx^*\|\leq\|\bA^{-1}\|\|\bb\|\). A lower bound for the numerator and an upper bound for its positive denominator give \[
\begin{align}
\frac{\|\be\|}{\|\bx^*\|}
\geq\frac{\|\br\|/\|\bA\|}{\|\bA^{-1}\|\|\bb\|}
=\frac1{\kappa(\bA)}\frac{\|\br\|}{\|\bb\|}.
\end{align}
\] Combining the upper and lower inequalities proves the statement. They are separate estimates, rather than one being obtained by reversing the other.









NoteExample



(A small residual does not guarantee a small error) Consider the diagonal system \[
\begin{align}
\bA = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 10^{-8} \end{pmatrix}, \qquad \bb = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 10^{-8} \end{pmatrix}.
\end{align}
\] The exact solution is \(\bx^* = (1, 1)^T\). Suppose an algorithm produces the approximation \(\hat{\bx} = (1, 0)^T\). The residual evaluates to \[
\begin{align}
\br = \bb - \bA\hat{\bx} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 10^{-8} \end{pmatrix} - \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 10^{-8} \end{pmatrix},
\end{align}
\] which satisfies \(\|\br\|_2 / \|\bb\|_2 \approx 10^{-8}\). However, the forward error is \(\hat{\bx} - \bx^* = (0, -1)^T\), yielding a relative forward error of \(\|\hat{\bx} - \bx^*\|_2 / \|\bx^*\|_2 = 1/\sqrt{2} \approx 0.707\) (a seventy percent error). The matrix has condition number \(\kappa_2(\bA) = 10^8\), which amplifies the small residual into an \(O(1)\) error in the solution.

7.2 Sensitivity to Perturbations in the Data
the referenced result measures the error of a given approximation through its residual. A related question asks how far the exact solution moves when the data \(\bA\) and \(\bb\) are perturbed. The answer is again governed by the condition number \(\kappa(\bA)=\|\bA\|\|\bA^{-1}\|\) of the referenced result. Perturbing \(\bA\) requires one preliminary fact, namely that a sufficiently small perturbation of a nonsingular matrix remains nonsingular.






NoteLemma: Perturbation of the Identity



Let \(\|\cdot\|\) be an induced matrix norm and let \(\bM\) be a square matrix with \(\|\bM\|&lt;1\). Then \(\bI+\bM\) is nonsingular and \[
\begin{align}
\|(\bI+\bM)^{-1}\|\leq\frac{1}{1-\|\bM\|}.
\end{align}
\]








TipProof





Put \(q=\|\bM\|\), so \(0\leq q&lt;1\).

If \((\bI+\bM)\bz=\bzero\), then \(\bz=-\bM\bz\). Taking norms gives \(\|\bz\|\leq q\|\bz\|\), or \((1-q)\|\bz\|\leq0\). Since \(1-q&gt;0\), we obtain \(\bz=\bzero\). A square matrix with trivial null space is nonsingular.
Given any \(\by\), let \(\bz=(\bI+\bM)^{-1}\by\). Rearrange \(\by=\bz+\bM\bz\) to obtain \(\bz=\by-\bM\bz\). Therefore \[
\begin{align}
\|\bz\|\leq\|\by\|+q\|\bz\|,
  \qquad (1-q)\|\bz\|\leq\|\by\|.
\end{align}
\] For nonzero \(\by\), division gives \[
\begin{align}
\frac{\|(\bI+\bM)^{-1}\by\|}{\|\by\|}\leq\frac1{1-q}.
\end{align}
\] Taking the supremum over inputs proves the inverse-norm bound.










NoteTheorem: Perturbation Bound for Linear Systems



Let \(\bA\) be nonsingular, let \(\bb\neq\bzero\), and let \(\bA\bx=\bb\). Suppose the perturbations \(\delta\bA\) and \(\delta\bb\) satisfy \(\kappa(\bA)\|\delta\bA\|/\|\bA\|&lt;1\). Then \(\bA+\delta\bA\) is nonsingular, and the solution of \((\bA+\delta\bA)(\bx+\delta\bx)=\bb+\delta\bb\) satisfies \[
\begin{align}
\frac{\|\delta\bx\|}{\|\bx\|}
    \leq
    \frac{\kappa(\bA)}{1-\kappa(\bA)\|\delta\bA\|/\|\bA\|}
    \left(\frac{\|\delta\bA\|}{\|\bA\|}+\frac{\|\delta\bb\|}{\|\bb\|}\right).
\end{align}
\]








TipProof





Write \(q=\|\bA^{-1}\|\|\delta\bA\|\). The hypothesis is \(q&lt;1\), because \(q=\kappa(\bA)\|\delta\bA\|/\|\bA\|\).
Factor the perturbed matrix: \[
\begin{align}
\bA+\delta\bA=\bA(\bI+\bA^{-1}\delta\bA).
\end{align}
\] Consistency gives \(\|\bA^{-1}\delta\bA\|\leq q&lt;1\). The inverse lemma makes the second factor nonsingular and bounds its inverse by \(1/(1-q)\). The first factor is nonsingular by assumption, so the product is nonsingular as well.
Expand the perturbed equation before subtracting \(\bA\bx=\bb\): \[
\begin{align}
\bA\bx+\bA\delta\bx+\delta\bA\bx+\delta\bA\delta\bx
&=\bb+\delta\bb,\\
(\bA+\delta\bA)\delta\bx&=\delta\bb-\delta\bA\bx.
\end{align}
\] Use the factorization of the inverse to solve this equation: \[
\begin{align}
\delta\bx=(\bI+\bA^{-1}\delta\bA)^{-1}
\bA^{-1}(\delta\bb-\delta\bA\bx).
\end{align}
\] Taking norms, applying consistency, and then the triangle inequality yields \[
\begin{align}
\|\delta\bx\|
\leq\frac{\|\bA^{-1}\|}{1-q}
       (\|\delta\bb\|+\|\delta\bA\|\|\bx\|).
\end{align}
\] Since \(\bb\neq\bzero\), its exact solution is nonzero. Divide by \(\|\bx\|\). From \(\|\bb\|=\|\bA\bx\|\leq\|\bA\|\|\bx\|\) we obtain \[
\begin{align}
\frac{\|\delta\bb\|}{\|\bx\|}
\leq\|\bA\|\frac{\|\delta\bb\|}{\|\bb\|}.
\end{align}
\] Substitute this estimate, factor out \(\|\bA^{-1}\|\|\bA\|=\kappa(\bA)\), and replace \(q\) by its relative expression. This produces precisely the bound in the statement. Every denominator used is positive by the hypotheses.



To first order, relative perturbations of size \(\varepsilon\) in \(\bA\) and in \(\bb\) change the solution by a relative amount of at most about \(2\kappa(\bA)\varepsilon\). The hypothesis \(\kappa(\bA)\|\delta\bA\|/\|\bA\|&lt;1\) cannot be dropped, because a larger perturbation may make \(\bA+\delta\bA\) singular. In the Euclidean norm, the smallest perturbation that does so has \(\|\delta\bA\|_2=\sigma_{\min}(\bA)=\|\bA\|_2/\kappa_2(\bA)\) by the referenced result. The reciprocal \(1/\kappa_2(\bA)\) is therefore the relative distance from \(\bA\) to the nearest singular matrix.

7.3 Backward Error and Backward Stability
The perturbation bound describes the problem and makes no reference to an algorithm. To apply it to a computed solution \(\hat{\bx}\), we need perturbations \(\delta\bA\) and \(\delta\bb\) for which \(\hat{\bx}\) is the exact solution. The smallest such perturbations can be computed from the residual.






NoteTheorem: Rigal–Gaches Theorem on Normwise Backward Error



In the Euclidean vector norm and its induced matrix norm, let \(\hat{\bx}\neq\bzero\) have residual \(\br = \bb - \bA\hat{\bx}\). The smallest relative perturbation size satisfying \((\bA + \delta\bA)\hat{\bx} = \bb + \delta\bb\) with \(\|\delta\bA\|_2 \leq \eta \|\bA\|_2\) and \(\|\delta\bb\|_2 \leq \eta \|\bb\|_2\) is \[
\begin{align}
\eta(\hat{\bx}) = \frac{\|\br\|_2}{\|\bA\|_2\|\hat{\bx}\|_2 + \|\bb\|_2}.
\end{align}
\] An algorithm is backward stable if, for every nonsingular \(\bA\) and every \(\bb\), the computed solution satisfies \(\eta(\hat{\bx})\leq c_n u\), where \(u\) is the unit roundoff and \(c_n\) grows at most polynomially with \(n\).


Rigal and Gaches proved this formula for the normwise backward error (Rigal and Gaches 1967). The proof below constructs the minimizing perturbations explicitly.






TipProof





Let \(d=\|\bA\|_2\|\hat{\bx}\|_2+\|\bb\|_2&gt;0\). We first bound any admissible perturbations, then construct ones attaining the bound.
Rearrange the perturbed equation to get \[
\begin{align}
\br=\bb-\bA\hat{\bx}=\delta\bA\hat{\bx}-\delta\bb.
\end{align}
\] If both relative perturbations are bounded by \(\eta\), then \[
\begin{align}
\|\br\|_2
&\leq\|\delta\bA\|_2\|\hat{\bx}\|_2+\|\delta\bb\|_2\\
&\leq\eta\|\bA\|_2\|\hat{\bx}\|_2+\eta\|\bb\|_2=\eta d.
\end{align}
\] Thus every admissible value satisfies \(\eta\geq\|\br\|_2/d\).
To attain this value, define the nonnegative weights \[
\begin{align}
a=\frac{\|\bA\|_2\|\hat{\bx}\|_2}{d},
\qquad b=\frac{\|\bb\|_2}{d},\qquad a+b=1,
\end{align}
\] and choose \[
\begin{align}
\delta\bA=a\frac{\br\hat{\bx}^T}{\|\hat{\bx}\|_2^2},
\qquad \delta\bb=-b\br.
\end{align}
\] The assumption \(\hat{\bx}\neq\bzero\) makes this matrix well defined. Multiplying it by \(\hat{\bx}\) cancels the squared norm in its denominator, so \(\delta\bA\hat{\bx}=a\br\). Consequently \[
\begin{align}
\delta\bA\hat{\bx}-\delta\bb=(a+b)\br=\br.
\end{align}
\] This is exactly the rearranged perturbed equation. Thus the constructed perturbations really make \(\hat{\bx}\) an exact solution.
For a rank-one matrix \(\bu\bv^T\), consistency and Cauchy–Schwarz give norm at most \(\|\bu\|_2\|\bv\|_2\), and a unit input along \(\bv\) attains it when both vectors are nonzero. Therefore \[
\begin{align}
\|\delta\bA\|_2
&=a\frac{\|\br\|_2}{\|\hat{\bx}\|_2}
=\frac{\|\br\|_2}{d}\|\bA\|_2,\\
\|\delta\bb\|_2
&=b\|\br\|_2=\frac{\|\br\|_2}{d}\|\bb\|_2.
\end{align}
\] The same equalities hold when the residual is zero, by taking zero perturbations. Both perturbation bounds are attained at the lower bound for \(\eta\), proving minimality. This is the normwise backward-error formula of Rigal and Gaches (1967).









NoteCorollary: Forward Error of a Backward Stable Solution



In the Euclidean norm, let \(\hat{\bx}\) have backward error \(\eta=\eta(\hat{\bx})\) as in the referenced result, and suppose \(\kappa_2(\bA)\eta&lt;1\). Then \[
\begin{align}
\frac{\|\hat{\bx}-\bx\|_2}{\|\bx\|_2}\leq\frac{2\kappa_2(\bA)\eta}{1-\kappa_2(\bA)\eta}.
\end{align}
\]








TipProof





The backward-error theorem provides perturbations satisfying \[
\begin{align}
(\bA+\delta\bA)\hat{\bx}&=\bb+\delta\bb,\\
\frac{\|\delta\bA\|_2}{\|\bA\|_2}&\leq\eta,
&\frac{\|\delta\bb\|_2}{\|\bb\|_2}&\leq\eta.
\end{align}
\] Set \(\delta\bx=\hat{\bx}-\bx\). Then \(\bx+\delta\bx=\hat{\bx}\), so these are precisely the perturbed data and solution in the perturbation theorem.
Let \(\kappa=\kappa_2(\bA)\). Its smallness hypothesis holds because \[
\begin{align}
\kappa\frac{\|\delta\bA\|_2}{\|\bA\|_2}\leq\kappa\eta&lt;1.
\end{align}
\] Apply that theorem and then replace each perturbation ratio by its upper bound: \[
\begin{align}
\frac{\|\hat{\bx}-\bx\|_2}{\|\bx\|_2}
&\leq\frac{\kappa}{1-\kappa\|\delta\bA\|_2/\|\bA\|_2}
\left(\frac{\|\delta\bA\|_2}{\|\bA\|_2}
+\frac{\|\delta\bb\|_2}{\|\bb\|_2}\right)\\
&\leq\frac{\kappa}{1-\kappa\eta}(\eta+\eta)
=\frac{2\kappa\eta}{1-\kappa\eta}.
\end{align}
\] The second step uses a lower bound for the positive denominator and upper bounds for the nonnegative numerator terms. This explains the direction of the inequality.









WarningExercise




Let \(\bA = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 \\ 1 & 1 + 10^{-10} \end{pmatrix}\). Compute \(\bA^{-1}\) explicitly and evaluate \(\kappa_\infty(\bA)\).
Suppose \(\bA\bx = \bb\) is solved using a backward stable algorithm in arithmetic with unit roundoff \(u \approx 10^{-16}\). If \(\kappa_2(\bA) = 10^{11}\), estimate the number of correct decimal digits in the computed solution.
Prove that for any two nonsingular matrices \(\bA\) and \(\bB\), \(\kappa(\bA\bB) \leq \kappa(\bA)\kappa(\bB)\).
Show that for an orthogonal matrix \(\bQ\), \(\kappa_2(\bQ\bA) = \kappa_2(\bA)\) for any square matrix \(\bA\).



For a backward stable algorithm, \(\eta\) is a modest multiple of \(u\), and the referenced result bounds the relative forward error by about \(2\kappa_2(\bA)u\). In double precision, with \(u\approx1.1\times10^{-16}\), a system with \(\kappa_2(\bA)=10^{10}\) can therefore be solved to about five correct digits by a stable method, and this is a worst-case accuracy estimate, not a limit attained for every right-hand side. A large forward error with a small backward error indicates an ill-conditioned problem, not an unstable algorithm.




Rigal, J. L., and J. Gaches. 1967. “On the Compatibility of a Given Solution with the Data of a Linear System.” Journal of the ACM 14 (3): 543–48.

NoteExample



Consider the coefficient matrix \[
\begin{align}
\bA=\begin{pmatrix}2&1&1\\4&3&3\\2&2&3\end{pmatrix}.
\end{align}
\] Subtracting twice the first row from the second and once the first row from the third produces \[
\begin{align}
\begin{pmatrix}2&1&1\\0&1&1\\0&1&2\end{pmatrix}.
\end{align}
\] The first column now has the zeros needed for an upper triangular matrix. One more row subtraction completes elimination. To solve a system, we apply each subtraction to the right-hand side as well. To reuse the calculation, we store the multipliers instead.








WarningExercise



Complete this elimination. Collect the multipliers in the positions below the diagonal of a matrix \(\bL\) with diagonal entries one, and call the final upper triangular matrix \(\bU\). Multiply the two factors to verify \(\bA=\bL\bU\). Why must \(\bL\) record the multipliers with their original signs rather than the minus signs used in the row subtractions?








NoteDefinition: LU Factorization



Let \(\bA \in \fR^{n \times n}\). An LU factorization of \(\bA\) is a decomposition \[
\begin{align}
\bA = \bL\bU,
\end{align}
\] where \(\bL \in \fR^{n \times n}\) is unit lower triangular (\(\ell_{ii} = 1\) and \(\ell_{ij} = 0\) for \(j &gt; i\)) and \(\bU \in \fR^{n \times n}\) is upper triangular (\(u_{ij} = 0\) for \(i &gt; j\)).








NoteLemma: Algebra of Unit Lower Triangular Matrices



The set of \(n \times n\) unit lower triangular matrices forms a group under matrix multiplication. In particular:

The product of two unit lower triangular matrices is unit lower triangular.
The inverse of a unit lower triangular matrix is unit lower triangular.
The determinant of every unit lower triangular matrix equals one.









WarningExercise



For \(i&lt;j\), show that every term in \((\bL_1\bL_2)_{ij}\) vanishes. Compute the diagonal. Next solve \(\bL\bx_j=\be_j\) by forward substitution and show that \(x_{ij}=0\) for \(i&lt;j\) and \(x_{jj}=1\). Deduce the assertions about the inverse and determinant.








NoteTheorem: Elimination Matrices and the Structure of L



Let \(\bA^{(0)} = \bA\). At step \(k\) of Gaussian elimination, assuming the pivot entry \(a_{kk}^{(k-1)} \neq 0\), the subdiagonal entries in column \(k\) are annihilated by applying the elementary elimination matrix \[
\begin{align}
\bM_k = \bI - \boldsymbol{\ell}_k \be_k^T,
\end{align}
\] where \(\boldsymbol{\ell}_k = (0, ..., 0, \ell_{k+1,k}, ..., \ell_{n,k})^T\) contains the multipliers \(\ell_{ik} = a_{ik}^{(k-1)} / a_{kk}^{(k-1)}\). After \(n-1\) elimination steps, the upper triangular factor is \(\bU = \bM_{n-1} ... \bM_1 \bA\). The lower triangular factor is the inverse of the accumulated elimination operations: \[
\begin{align}
\bL = \bM_1^{-1} \bM_2^{-1} ... \bM_{n-1}^{-1} = \bI + \sum_{k=1}^{n-1} \boldsymbol{\ell}_k \be_k^T.
\end{align}
\] Consequently, the entries of \(\bL\) strictly below the main diagonal are precisely the multipliers computed during Gaussian elimination.








TipProof





The inverse of an elementary elimination matrix \(\bM_k = \bI - \boldsymbol{\ell}_k \be_k^T\) is \(\bM_k^{-1} = \bI + \boldsymbol{\ell}_k \be_k^T\). This identity follows from the fact that the first \(k\) entries of \(\boldsymbol{\ell}_k\) are zero, which implies \(\be_k^T \boldsymbol{\ell}_k = 0\) and therefore \[
\begin{align}
(\bI - \boldsymbol{\ell}_k \be_k^T)(\bI + \boldsymbol{\ell}_k \be_k^T) = \bI - \boldsymbol{\ell}_k \be_k^T + \boldsymbol{\ell}_k \be_k^T - \boldsymbol{\ell}_k (\be_k^T \boldsymbol{\ell}_k) \be_k^T = \bI.
\end{align}
\] Writing \(\bN_k=\boldsymbol\ell_k\be_k^T\), the condition \(\be_k^T\boldsymbol\ell_k=0\) says \(\bN_k^2=\bzero\). Hence both \((\bI-\bN_k)(\bI+\bN_k)\) and the product in the reverse order equal the identity. This verifies both sides of the proposed inverse.
Next, consider the product of two successive inverse matrices \(\bM_j^{-1} \bM_k^{-1}\) with \(j &lt; k\). Because \(\be_j^T \boldsymbol{\ell}_k = 0\), the cross term vanishes: \[
\begin{align}
(\bI + \boldsymbol{\ell}_j \be_j^T)(\bI + \boldsymbol{\ell}_k \be_k^T) = \bI + \boldsymbol{\ell}_j \be_j^T + \boldsymbol{\ell}_k \be_k^T + \boldsymbol{\ell}_j (\be_j^T \boldsymbol{\ell}_k) \be_k^T = \bI + \boldsymbol{\ell}_j \be_j^T + \boldsymbol{\ell}_k \be_k^T.
\end{align}
\] By induction, the product \(\bM_1^{-1} ... \bM_{n-1}^{-1}\) simply places each column of multipliers into column \(k\) of the identity matrix without modifying any previously placed multipliers. Multiplying \(\bU = \bM_{n-1} ... \bM_1 \bA\) on the left by \(\bL\) yields \(\bA = \bL\bU\).
The ordering matters. Multiplying the final upper matrix on the left successively by \(\bM_{n-1}^{-1},...,\bM_1^{-1}\) undoes the row operations in reverse order. Grouping the inverses as \(\bL=\bM_1^{-1}\cdots\bM_{n-1}^{-1}\) therefore gives \(\bL\bU=\bA\). The vanishing cross terms show that these inverse operations record precisely the multipliers in the lower triangle.









NoteTheorem: Existence and Uniqueness of the LU Factorization



A matrix \(\bA \in \fR^{n \times n}\) has a unique LU factorization with unit lower triangular \(\bL\) and nonsingular upper triangular \(\bU\) if and only if every leading principal submatrix \(\bA_k = \bA[1:k, 1:k]\) is nonsingular for each \(k = 1, ..., n\).








TipProof





We proceed by induction on the matrix dimension \(n\). For \(n = 1\), \(\bA = (a_{11})\). Because \(\det(\bA_1) = a_{11} \neq 0\), the unique factorization is \(\bL = (1)\) and \(\bU = (a_{11})\). Assume the result holds for matrices of dimension \(n-1\). Partition the \(n \times n\) matrix \(\bA\) into blocks: \[
\begin{align}
\bA = \begin{pmatrix} \bA_{n-1} & \bc \\ \bd^T & a_{nn} \end{pmatrix},
\end{align}
\] where \(\bA_{n-1} \in \fR^{(n-1) \times (n-1)}\), \(\bc, \bd \in \fR^{n-1}\), and \(a_{nn} \in \fR\). Every leading principal block of \(\bA_{n-1}\) is also a leading principal block of \(\bA\), so it satisfies the theorem’s hypotheses. This verifies the condition needed to invoke the induction hypothesis on the smaller matrix.
By the induction hypothesis, \(\bA_{n-1}\) has a unique factorization \(\bA_{n-1} = \bL_{n-1} \bU_{n-1}\) with \(\bL_{n-1}\) unit lower triangular and \(\bU_{n-1}\) nonsingular. We seek block factors \[
\begin{align}
\bL = \begin{pmatrix} \bL_{n-1} & \bzero \\ \boldsymbol{\ell}^T & 1 \end{pmatrix}, \qquad
    \bU = \begin{pmatrix} \bU_{n-1} & \bu \\ \bzero^T & u_{nn} \end{pmatrix}.
\end{align}
\] The zero lower-left block of \(\bU\) and zero upper-right block of \(\bL\) are required by triangularity. The diagonal one in \(\bL\) is required by the unit-diagonal convention. Only the new vectors and the final scalar remain undetermined.
Multiplying \(\bL\) and \(\bU\) and equating block by block gives: \[
\begin{align}
\bL_{n-1}\bu = \bc, \qquad \boldsymbol{\ell}^T \bU_{n-1} = \bd^T, \qquad \boldsymbol{\ell}^T\bu + u_{nn} = a_{nn}.
\end{align}
\] Because \(\bL_{n-1}\) is unit lower triangular (hence invertible), \(\bu = \bL_{n-1}^{-1}\bc\) is uniquely determined. Because \(\bU_{n-1}\) is nonsingular, \(\boldsymbol{\ell}^T = \bd^T \bU_{n-1}^{-1}\) is uniquely determined. Finally, the scalar diagonal entry is uniquely determined by \(u_{nn} = a_{nn} - \boldsymbol{\ell}^T\bu\). Taking determinants of the block decomposition gives \[
\begin{align}
\det(\bA) = \det(\bL)\det(\bU) = (1 \cdot \det(\bL_{n-1})) (\det(\bU_{n-1}) \cdot u_{nn}) = \det(\bA_{n-1}) u_{nn}.
\end{align}
\] Because \(\det(\bA) \neq 0\) and \(\det(\bA_{n-1}) \neq 0\), we have \(u_{nn} = \det(\bA)/\det(\bA_{n-1}) \neq 0\), which ensures that \(\bU\) is nonsingular. This completes the induction.
For necessity, if \(\bA=\bL\bU\) with \(\bU\) nonsingular, its leading block satisfies \(\bA_k=\bL_k\bU_k\). Both triangular factors are nonsingular, so every \(\bA_k\) is nonsingular. This proves the converse as well.
For the converse, triangularity ensures that the first \(k\) rows and columns of the product involve only the leading factors: no omitted trailing index contributes. The diagonal of \(\bU\) is nonzero because its determinant is their product and \(\bU\) is nonsingular. Therefore every leading triangular block \(\bU_k\) is nonsingular. The unit lower block \(\bL_k\) is nonsingular as well, proving every leading block of \(\bA\) is nonsingular.

9.2 Cholesky Factorization






NoteTheorem: Cholesky Factorization



Every SPD matrix \(\bA\in\fR^{n\times n}\) has a unique factorization \[
\begin{align}
\bA=\bL\bL^T,
\end{align}
\] where \(\bL\) is lower triangular with positive diagonal entries.








TipProof





We construct the factor one block at a time. In addition to existence, the positive choice of diagonal will determine each new block uniquely.
We proceed by induction on \(n\). For \(n=1\), \(\bA = (\alpha)\) with \(\alpha &gt; 0\), so \(\bL = (\sqrt{\alpha})\) is the unique positive square root. For the induction step, partition \(\bA \in \fR^{n \times n}\) as \[
\begin{align}
\bA = \begin{pmatrix} \alpha & \br^T \\ \br & \bB \end{pmatrix}.
\end{align}
\] Since \(\bA\) is SPD, \(\alpha = \be_1^T\bA\be_1 &gt; 0\). Set \(\ell_{11} = \sqrt{\alpha}\) and \(\boldsymbol{\ell} = \br / \ell_{11}\). The first diagonal entry \(\alpha\) is positive by testing the quadratic form with the first coordinate vector. Thus its positive square root and all divisions by it are defined.
Form the Schur complement \[
\begin{align}
\bS = \bB - \boldsymbol{\ell}\boldsymbol{\ell}^T = \bB - \frac{\br\br^T}{\alpha}.
\end{align}
\] For any nonzero \(\by \in \fR^{n-1}\), choose \(\bx = (-\boldsymbol{\ell}^T\by / \ell_{11}, \by^T)^T \in \fR^n\). Expanding the quadratic form: \[
\begin{align}
\bx^T\bA\bx
    &= \begin{pmatrix} -\frac{\boldsymbol{\ell}^T\by}{\ell_{11}} \\ \by \end{pmatrix}^T
       \begin{pmatrix} \alpha & \br^T \\ \br & \bB \end{pmatrix}
       \begin{pmatrix} -\frac{\boldsymbol{\ell}^T\by}{\ell_{11}} \\ \by \end{pmatrix} \\
    &= \alpha\left(\frac{\boldsymbol{\ell}^T\by}{\ell_{11}}\right)^2 - 2\frac{(\boldsymbol{\ell}^T\by)(\br^T\by)}{\ell_{11}} + \by^T\bB\by \\
    &= (\boldsymbol{\ell}^T\by)^2 - 2(\boldsymbol{\ell}^T\by)^2 + \by^T\bB\by \\
    &= \by^T(\bB - \boldsymbol{\ell}\boldsymbol{\ell}^T)\by = \by^T\bS\by.
\end{align}
\] Since \(\by \neq \bzero\), the vector \(\bx\) is nonzero, so \(\by^T\bS\by = \bx^T\bA\bx &gt; 0\). Hence \(\bS\) is SPD. By induction, \(\bS = \bK\bK^T\) for a unique lower triangular \(\bK\) with positive diagonal entries. Block multiplication gives \[
\begin{align}
\bA = \begin{pmatrix} \ell_{11} & \bzero^T \\ \boldsymbol{\ell} & \bK \end{pmatrix}
          \begin{pmatrix} \ell_{11} & \boldsymbol{\ell}^T \\ \bzero & \bK^T \end{pmatrix}
        = \begin{pmatrix} \ell_{11}^2 & \ell_{11}\boldsymbol{\ell}^T \\ \ell_{11}\boldsymbol{\ell} & \boldsymbol{\ell}\boldsymbol{\ell}^T + \bK\bK^T \end{pmatrix}
        = \begin{pmatrix} \alpha & \br^T \\ \br & \bB \end{pmatrix}.
\end{align}
\] The positivity of the diagonal requires \(\ell_{11} = \sqrt{\alpha}\), which uniquely fixes \(\boldsymbol{\ell} = \br / \ell_{11}\), and uniqueness of \(\bK\) makes the entire factor \(\bL\) unique.









WarningExercise



Complete the block calculation in the proof of the referenced result: verify exactly that \(\bx^T\bA\bx=\by^T\bS\by\). Then prove the converse statement that if \(\alpha&gt;0\) and \(\bS\) is SPD, the block matrix \(\bA\) is SPD.








NoteTheorem: Equivalent Characterizations of Positive Definiteness



For a real symmetric matrix \(\bA\), the following statements are equivalent.

\(\bx^T\bA\bx&gt;0\) for every nonzero \(\bx\).
\(\bA=\bL\bL^T\) for a lower triangular \(\bL\) with positive diagonal.
\(\bA=\bW^T\bW\) for a nonsingular matrix \(\bW\).
Every leading principal minor is positive.









TipProof





We prove the first three equivalences, then connect them to the leading principal minors.
If \(\bA\) is SPD, the Cholesky theorem supplies \(\bA=\bL\bL^T\) with positive diagonal entries. A triangular matrix with nonzero diagonal is nonsingular. Taking \(\bW=\bL^T\) gives the third representation, \(\bA=\bW^T\bW\).
Conversely, suppose \(\bA=\bW^T\bW\) with \(\bW\) nonsingular. A nonzero \(\bx\) cannot have \(\bW\bx=\bzero\). Therefore \[
\begin{align}
\bx^T\bA\bx=(\bW\bx)^T(\bW\bx)=\|\bW\bx\|_2^2&gt;0.
\end{align}
\] This proves positive definiteness and completes the equivalence of statements (1), (2), and (3).
Next suppose \(\bA\) is SPD. Every leading principal block \(\bA_j\) is SPD by the referenced result. Its Cholesky factor has positive diagonal, so \[
\begin{align}
\det(\bA_j)=\det(\bL_j)\det(\bL_j^T)
=\prod_{i=1}^j\ell_{ii}^2&gt;0.
\end{align}
\] Thus (1) implies (4).
For the converse, assume every leading principal minor is positive. We use induction on the dimension. For dimension one, this is exactly the positive-definiteness condition. In a larger dimension, write \[
\begin{align}
\bA=\begin{pmatrix}\alpha&\br^T\\\br&\bB\end{pmatrix},
\qquad \bS=\bB-\br\br^T/\alpha.
\end{align}
\] The first leading minor is \(\alpha&gt;0\). For a leading block of size \(j\) in \(\bS\), subtracting \(r_i/\alpha\) times the first row from each lower row of \(\bA_{j+1}\) gives a block upper triangular matrix with diagonal blocks \(\alpha\) and \(\bS_j\). These row subtractions leave the determinant unchanged, so \[
\begin{align}
\det(\bS_j)=\frac{\det(\bA_{j+1})}{\alpha}&gt;0.
\end{align}
\] The Schur complement is symmetric and has positive leading minors. The induction hypothesis makes it SPD.
Finally complete the square for an arbitrary vector \((s,\by^T)^T\): \[
\begin{align}
\begin{pmatrix}s\\\by\end{pmatrix}^T\bA\begin{pmatrix}s\\\by\end{pmatrix}
&=\alpha s^2+2s\br^T\by+\by^T\bB\by\\
&=\alpha\left(s+\frac{\br^T\by}{\alpha}\right)^2+\by^T\bS\by.
\end{align}
\] If \(\by\neq\bzero\), the second term is positive. If \(\by=\bzero\) and the full vector is nonzero, then \(s\neq0\) and the first term is positive. Thus \(\bA\) is SPD, proving (4) implies (1). This is Sylvester’s criterion (Meyer 2000; Horn and Johnson 2012).









WarningExercise



Let \(\bX\in\fR^{m\times n}\) with \(m\geq n\). Prove that \(\bX^T\bX\) is SPD exactly when the columns of \(\bX\) are linearly independent. State what remains true when they are dependent.








TipRemark



After the spectral theorem is available, one more characterization follows directly. A real symmetric matrix is SPD exactly when all its eigenvalues are positive. The Cholesky argument does not need this fact, so we postpone it and keep the present argument self-contained.

9.3 The Cholesky Algorithm






NoteTheorem: The Cholesky Algorithm



The entries of the factor in \(\bA=\bL\bL^T\) are computed column by column: \[
\begin{align}
\ell_{jj}
    &=\sqrt{a_{jj}-\sum_{k=1}^{j-1}\ell_{jk}^2},\\
    \ell_{ij}
    &=\frac{a_{ij}-\sum_{k=1}^{j-1}\ell_{ik}\ell_{jk}}{\ell_{jj}},
    \qquad i=j+1,...,n.
\end{align}
\] For an SPD matrix every expression under a square root is positive. A nonpositive pivot signals that the input is not positive definite, apart from the effects of rounding.








TipProof





We derive one column at a time from the identity \(\bA=\bL\bL^T\).

For \(i\geq j\), triangularity gives \[
\begin{align}
a_{ij}=(\bL\bL^T)_{ij}
  =\sum_{k=1}^n\ell_{ik}\ell_{jk}
  =\sum_{k=1}^{j}\ell_{ik}\ell_{jk}.
\end{align}
\] Terms with \(k&gt;j\) vanish because \(\ell_{jk}=0\).
Put \(i=j\) and separate the last term: \[
\begin{align}
a_{jj}=\sum_{k=1}^{j-1}\ell_{jk}^2+\ell_{jj}^2.
\end{align}
\] The Cholesky convention chooses the positive square root, so \[
\begin{align}
\ell_{jj}=\sqrt{a_{jj}-\sum_{k=1}^{j-1}\ell_{jk}^2}.
\end{align}
\] Existence of the Cholesky factor for an SPD matrix ensures that the radicand is strictly positive in exact arithmetic.
For \(i&gt;j\), move the known terms to the other side: \[
\begin{align}
a_{ij}-\sum_{k=1}^{j-1}\ell_{ik}\ell_{jk}=\ell_{ij}\ell_{jj}.
\end{align}
\] Division by \(\ell_{jj}&gt;0\) gives the stated update. All quantities on its right side come from preceding columns or from the diagonal entry just computed.










NoteExample



For \[
\begin{align}
\bA=\begin{pmatrix}4&2&-2\\2&10&2\\-2&2&6\end{pmatrix},
\end{align}
\] the algorithm gives \[
\begin{align}
\ell_{11}=2,&\quad \ell_{21}=1,\quad \ell_{31}=-1,\\
    \ell_{22}=3,&\quad \ell_{32}=1,\quad \ell_{33}=2.
\end{align}
\] Thus \[
\begin{align}
\bL=\begin{pmatrix}2&0&0\\1&3&0\\-1&1&2\end{pmatrix},
    \qquad \bL\bL^T=\bA.
\end{align}
\] To solve \(\bA\bx=\bb\), first solve \(\bL\by=\bb\) and then \(\bL^T\bx=\by\).

NoteTheorem: Sherman–Morrison Formula



Let \(\bA\in\fR^{n\times n}\) be nonsingular, and let \(\bu,\bv\in\fR^{n}\). The updated matrix \(\bA+\bu\bv^T\) is nonsingular if and only if \[
\begin{align}
1+\bv^T\bA^{-1}\bu\neq 0.
\end{align}
\] When this condition holds, \[
\begin{align}
(\bA+\bu\bv^T)^{-1}
    =\bA^{-1}-\frac{\bA^{-1}\bu\bv^T\bA^{-1}}
    {1+\bv^T\bA^{-1}\bu}.
\end{align}
\]








TipProof





Set \(\bz=\bA^{-1}\bu\) and \(\alpha=1+\bv^T\bz\). We must handle the singular and nonsingular cases separately.
Suppose \(\alpha=0\). If \(\bu\) were zero, then \(\bz\) would be zero and \(\alpha\) would be one. Hence \(\bu\neq\bzero\), and invertibility of \(\bA\) gives \(\bz\neq\bzero\). Now calculate \[
\begin{align}
(\bA+\bu\bv^T)\bz
&=\bA\bz+\bu(\bv^T\bz)\\
&=\bu+\bu(\bv^T\bz)
=\bu(1+\bv^T\bz)=\bzero.
\end{align}
\] The updated matrix has a nonzero null vector, so it is singular.
Suppose instead that \(\alpha\neq0\) and define \(\bB=\bA^{-1}-\bz\bv^T\bA^{-1}/\alpha\). The first product needed is \[
\begin{align}
(\bA+\bu\bv^T)\bA^{-1}=\bI+\bu\bv^T\bA^{-1}.
\end{align}
\] The calculation in the first case, now without setting \(\alpha\) to zero, gives \((\bA+\bu\bv^T)\bz=\alpha\bu\). Therefore \[
\begin{align}
(\bA+\bu\bv^T)\bB
&=\bI+\bu\bv^T\bA^{-1}
   -\frac{\alpha\bu\bv^T\bA^{-1}}{\alpha}
=\bI.
\end{align}
\] A square matrix possessing a right inverse is nonsingular, so \(\bB\) is its inverse. Replacing \(\bz\) by \(\bA^{-1}\bu\) gives the stated formula. This identity is due to Sherman and Morrison (1950).









WarningExercise



A nearly singular correction. Let \(\bA=\bI\), \(\bu=-(1-\delta)\be_1\), and \(\bv=\be_1\). Compute the Sherman–Morrison denominator and the updated inverse. Explain why the correction amplifies roundoff as \(\delta\to0\), even though solving with the base matrix is perfectly conditioned.








NoteDefinition: Sherman–Morrison Solve



To solve \((\bA+\bu\bv^T)\bx=\bb\) from stored factors of \(\bA\):

Solve \(\bA\bz=\bu\) once for the update.
Compute \(\alpha=1+\bv^T\bz\). Refactor the updated matrix if \(\alpha\) is zero or so small that division by it would magnify roundoff.
For each right-hand side, solve \(\bA\by=\bb\).
Compute \[
\begin{align}
\bx=\by-\bz\frac{\bv^T\by}{\alpha}.
\end{align}
\]

The algorithm never forms \(\bA^{-1}\).








WarningExercise



Sherman–Morrison by hand. Repeat the worked example with \(\bb=(0,1)^T\). Compute the corrected solution from the referenced result, and verify it by multiplying with \(\bA+\bu\bv^T\).








NoteExample



Let \[
\begin{align}
\bA=\begin{pmatrix}2&0\\0&3\end{pmatrix},\qquad
    \bu=\begin{pmatrix}1\\1\end{pmatrix},\qquad
    \bv=\begin{pmatrix}1\\-1\end{pmatrix},\qquad
    \bb=\begin{pmatrix}1\\0\end{pmatrix}.
\end{align}
\] The two solves with \(\bA\) give \(\bz=(1/2,1/3)^T\) and \(\by=(1/2,0)^T\). Since \(\alpha=1+1/2-1/3=7/6\), \[
\begin{align}
\bx=\by-\bz\frac{1/2}{7/6}
       =\begin{pmatrix}2/7\\-1/7\end{pmatrix}.
\end{align}
\] Multiplication by \(\bA+\bu\bv^T=\begin{pmatrix}3&-1\\1&2\end{pmatrix}\) verifies the solution.








TipRemark



(Cost of a rank-one change) With a dense LU factorization, preparing the correction requires one \(O(n^2)\) solve. Each new right-hand side requires one further solve and \(O(n)\) correction work. A fresh \(O(n^3)\) factorization is avoided. For a sparse factorization, the same comparison uses the measured cost of a triangular solve rather than the dense estimate.

10.5 The Symmetric Positive Definite Case
When the block-tridiagonal matrix is symmetric positive definite, block Cholesky removes the need for pivoting and stores only one off-diagonal side. The key fact is that every diagonal Schur complement remains positive definite.






NoteTheorem: Positive Definite Schur Complement



Let \[
\begin{align}
\bA=\begin{pmatrix}\bD&\bB^T\\\bB&\bC\end{pmatrix}
\end{align}
\] be symmetric positive definite. Then \(\bD\) is positive definite, and the Schur complement \[
\begin{align}
\bC-\bB\bD^{-1}\bB^T
\end{align}
\] is positive definite.








TipProof





For every nonzero \(\bx\), testing with \((\bx,\bzero)^T\) gives \[
\begin{align}
\begin{pmatrix}\bx\\\bzero\end{pmatrix}^T \bA \begin{pmatrix}\bx\\\bzero\end{pmatrix} = \bx^T\bD\bx &gt; 0,
\end{align}
\] so \(\bD\) is positive definite and \(\bD^{-1}\) exists. The leading block \(\bD\) is symmetric because it is a principal block of the symmetric matrix. Thus \(\bD^{-1}\) is symmetric too, by transposing \(\bD\bD^{-1}=\bI\). This fact is used when transposing the chosen vector in the next expansion.
Now let \(\by \neq \bzero\), and choose \(\bx = -\bD^{-1}\bB^T\by\). Expanding the block quadratic form: \[
\begin{align}
\begin{pmatrix}\bx\\\by\end{pmatrix}^T
    \begin{pmatrix}\bD&\bB^T\\\bB&\bC\end{pmatrix}
    \begin{pmatrix}\bx\\\by\end{pmatrix}
    &= \bx^T\bD\bx + \bx^T\bB^T\by + \by^T\bB\bx + \by^T\bC\by \\
    &= (-\bD^{-1}\bB^T\by)^T\bD(-\bD^{-1}\bB^T\by) + 2(-\bD^{-1}\bB^T\by)^T\bB^T\by + \by^T\bC\by \\
    &= \by^T\bB\bD^{-1}\bB^T\by - 2\by^T\bB\bD^{-1}\bB^T\by + \by^T\bC\by \\
    &= \by^T(\bC - \bB\bD^{-1}\bB^T)\by.
\end{align}
\] Because \(\by \neq \bzero\), the compound vector \((\bx,\by)^T\) is nonzero. The positive definiteness of \(\bA\) implies that this quadratic form is strictly positive: \[
\begin{align}
\by^T(\bC - \bB\bD^{-1}\bB^T)\by &gt; 0.
\end{align}
\] The Schur complement \(\bC - \bB\bD^{-1}\bB^T\) is therefore positive definite.
The Schur complement is symmetric because both \(\bC\) and \(\bB\bD^{-1}\bB^T\) are symmetric. The quadratic-form calculation proves strict positivity for every nonzero \(\by\). These two facts together prove that it is SPD and justify repeating the same elimination step on the trailing block.



Block Cholesky follows the same order as block LU. Factor \(\bS_k=\bF_k\bF_k^T\), solve \[
\begin{align}
\bC_k\bF_k^T=\bL_k,
\end{align}
\] and update \[
\begin{align}
\bS_{k+1}=\bD_{k+1}-\bC_k\bC_k^T.
\end{align}
\] The theorem above shows that every \(\bS_k\) remains positive definite. The stored factors form a block lower-bidiagonal Cholesky factor of \(\bA\).

11.5 Orthogonal Projectors






NoteDefinition: Orthogonal Projector



Let \(S \subset \fR^m\) be a subspace with orthonormal basis columns stored in \(\bQ_1 \in \fR^{m \times n}\). The orthogonal projector onto \(S\) is the matrix \[
\begin{align}
\bP = \bQ_1 \bQ_1^T.
\end{align}
\] Every orthogonal projector is symmetric (\(\bP^T = \bP\)) and idempotent (\(\bP^2 = \bP\)). The complementary projector \(\bP^\perp = \bI - \bP\) projects onto the orthogonal complement \(S^\perp\).








NoteTheorem: Symmetric Idempotents are Orthogonal Projectors



A square matrix \(\bP\) is the orthogonal projector onto its range if and only if \[
\begin{align}
\bP^T=\bP,
    \qquad
    \bP^2=\bP.
\end{align}
\] For such a matrix, every vector has the orthogonal decomposition \[
\begin{align}
\bx=\bP\bx+(\bI-\bP)\bx,
\end{align}
\] with the first term in \(\mathcal{R}(\bP)\) and the second in \(\mathcal{N}(\bP)\).








TipProof





We prove both directions.

Assume \(\bP^T=\bP\) and \(\bP^2=\bP\). For any \(\bx\), \[
\begin{align}
\bx=\bP\bx+(\bI-\bP)\bx.
\end{align}
\] The first term belongs to the range by definition. The second belongs to the null space because \[
\begin{align}
\bP(\bI-\bP)\bx=(\bP-\bP^2)\bx=\bzero.
\end{align}
\] If \(\by=\bP\bu\) is any range vector and \(\bz\) is any null-space vector, then \[
\begin{align}
\by^T\bz=\bu^T\bP^T\bz=\bu^T\bP\bz=0.
\end{align}
\] Symmetry was needed to replace \(\bP^T\) by \(\bP\). Thus the two components are perpendicular.
For any \(\bs\) in the range, \(\bP\bx-\bs\) also lies in the range. Consequently \[
\begin{align}
\|\bx-\bs\|_2^2
  =\|(\bI-\bP)\bx\|_2^2+\|\bP\bx-\bs\|_2^2.
\end{align}
\] The unique minimum occurs at \(\bs=\bP\bx\).
If \(\bP\) is the orthogonal projector onto a subspace with orthonormal basis \(\bQ_1\), then \(\bP=\bQ_1\bQ_1^T\). Direct multiplication gives \[
\begin{align}
\bP^T&=(\bQ_1\bQ_1^T)^T=\bQ_1\bQ_1^T=\bP,\\
  \bP^2&=\bQ_1(\bQ_1^T\bQ_1)\bQ_1^T=\bQ_1\bQ_1^T=\bP.
\end{align}
\]










WarningExercise



Let \(\bQ\) and \(\widehat{\bQ}\) have orthonormal columns spanning the same subspace. Prove that \(\bQ\bQ^T=\widehat{\bQ}\widehat{\bQ}^T\). Thus projection depends on the subspace, not on the chosen orthonormal basis.

13.2 Multiplicity and Schur Form






NoteDefinition: Algebraic and Geometric Multiplicity



Let \(\lambda\) be an eigenvalue of \(\bA\).

The algebraic multiplicity \(\mu_a(\lambda)\) is the multiplicity of \(\lambda\) as a root of \(\det(\bA - z\bI) = 0\).
The geometric multiplicity \(\mu_g(\lambda) = \dim \mathcal{N}(\bA - \lambda\bI)\) is the dimension of the corresponding eigenspace.

Every eigenvalue satisfies \(1 \leq \mu_g(\lambda) \leq \mu_a(\lambda)\). When \(\mu_g(\lambda) &lt; \mu_a(\lambda)\) for any eigenvalue, \(\bA\) is defective and does not possess a complete basis of eigenvectors.








WarningExercise



(A defective shear) For \(\bA=\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\0&1\end{pmatrix}\), find the algebraic and geometric multiplicities of the eigenvalue. Compute \(\bA^k\) and explain how its growth is invisible if one records only the eigenvalue \(1\).


For complex vectors, \(\bQ^*\) denotes the conjugate transpose: transpose the matrix and conjugate its entries. The inner product is \(\bx^*\by\), and a unitary matrix satisfies \(\bQ^*\bQ=\bI\). These extend the real inner product and orthogonal matrices. The conjugation is necessary because \(\bx^*\bx=\sum_i|x_i|^2\) must be positive for nonzero \(\bx\).






WarningExercise



Prove that eigenvectors associated with distinct eigenvalues are linearly independent. For an induction, apply \(\bA-\lambda_k\bI\) to a proposed dependence among \(k\) eigenvectors. Conclude that a matrix with \(n\) distinct eigenvalues has an eigenvector basis over \(\fC\).


Repeated eigenvalues can prevent diagonalization, as the shear shows. We can still begin a basis with one eigenvector. The entries below the first diagonal position then vanish. Repeating the construction on the remaining block produces a triangular matrix, without requiring the remaining basis vectors to be eigenvectors of the original matrix.






NoteTheorem: Schur Triangularization Decomposition



Every square matrix \(\bA \in \fC^{n \times n}\) is unitarily similar to an upper triangular matrix: \[
\begin{align}
\bA = \bQ\bT\bQ^*,
\end{align}
\] where \(\bQ \in \fC^{n \times n}\) is unitary (\(\bQ^*\bQ = \bI\)) and \(\bT \in \fC^{n \times n}\) is upper triangular. The diagonal entries of \(\bT\) are the eigenvalues of \(\bA\).








TipProof





We proceed by induction on the matrix dimension \(n\). For \(n = 1\), the statement holds trivially with \(\bQ = [1]\) and \(\bT = \bA\).
The induction will isolate one eigenvector and then apply the result only to a matrix of order \(n-1\). We do not assume that \(\bA\) has a complete eigenvector basis.
Assume the theorem holds for all square matrices of order \(n - 1\). By the Fundamental Theorem of Algebra, the characteristic polynomial \(\det(\bA - z\bI) = 0\) possesses at least one complex root \(\lambda_1\). Let \(\bv_1 \in \fC^n\) be an eigenvector associated with \(\lambda_1\), normalized so that \(\|\bv_1\|_2 = 1\). Using the Gram–Schmidt process or Householder reflectors, construct an orthonormal basis for \(\fC^n\) whose first column is \(\bv_1\), forming a unitary matrix \(\bU_1 = [\bv_1, \bW] \in \fC^{n \times n}\) with \(\bW \in \fC^{n \times (n-1)}\).
The completed basis satisfies \(\bv_1^*\bv_1=1\), \(\bW^*\bv_1=\bzero\), and \(\bW^*\bW=\bI_{n-1}\). These identities will determine the first column of the transformed matrix.
Applying this unitary similarity transformation to \(\bA\) gives \[
\begin{align}
\bU_1^* \bA \bU_1
    =
    \begin{pmatrix} \bv_1^* \\ \bW^* \end{pmatrix} \bA \begin{pmatrix} \bv_1 & \bW \end{pmatrix}
    =
    \begin{pmatrix} \bv_1^* \bA \bv_1 & \bv_1^* \bA \bW \\ \bW^* \bA \bv_1 & \bW^* \bA \bW \end{pmatrix}.
\end{align}
\] Because \(\bA\bv_1 = \lambda_1 \bv_1\), the leading entry is \(\bv_1^*(\lambda_1\bv_1) = \lambda_1 \|\bv_1\|_2^2 = \lambda_1\). The lower-left block is \(\bW^*(\lambda_1\bv_1) = \lambda_1(\bW^*\bv_1) = \bzero\), because the columns of \(\bW\) are orthogonal to \(\bv_1\). Thus \[
\begin{align}
\bU_1^* \bA \bU_1 = \begin{pmatrix} \lambda_1 & \bw^* \\ \bzero & \bA_1 \end{pmatrix},
\end{align}
\] where \(\bA_1 = \bW^* \bA \bW \in \fC^{(n-1) \times (n-1)}\) and \(\bw^* = \bv_1^* \bA \bW\).
By the induction hypothesis, there exists a unitary matrix \(\bQ_1 \in \fC^{(n-1) \times (n-1)}\) such that \(\bQ_1^* \bA_1 \bQ_1 = \bT_1\) is upper triangular. Define the \(n \times n\) block-diagonal unitary matrix \[
\begin{align}
\bV = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & \bzero \\ \bzero & \bQ_1 \end{pmatrix}.
\end{align}
\] Setting \(\bQ = \bU_1 \bV\), which is unitary as the product of unitary matrices, we obtain \[
\begin{align}
\bQ^*\bA\bQ
    &=\bV^*(\bU_1^*\bA\bU_1)\bV\\
    &=\begin{pmatrix}\lambda_1&\bw^*\bQ_1\\\bzero&\bQ_1^*\bA_1\bQ_1\end{pmatrix}
      =\begin{pmatrix}\lambda_1&\bw^*\bQ_1\\\bzero&\bT_1\end{pmatrix}.
\end{align}
\] To verify that the new matrix is unitary, multiply \[
\begin{align}
\bQ^*\bQ=\bV^*\bU_1^*\bU_1\bV=\bV^*\bV=\bI.
\end{align}
\] The block \(\bQ_1\) acts only on the trailing coordinates, so it cannot introduce entries below the zero lower-left block.
Because \(\bT_1\) is upper triangular, the entire block matrix \(\bT = \bQ^*\bA\bQ\) is upper triangular. The diagonal entries of \(\bT\) are \(\lambda_1\) and the eigenvalues of \(\bA_1\), which collectively form the eigenvalues of \(\bA\).
Finally, similarity preserves the characteristic polynomial, and the determinant of a triangular matrix is the product of its diagonal entries. Therefore \(\det(\bT-z\bI)=\prod_i(t_{ii}-z)\), which proves that the diagonal entries list all eigenvalues with algebraic multiplicities. See Horn and Johnson (2012) for Schur form.









NoteTheorem: Spectral Theorem for Real Symmetric Matrices



If \(\bA \in \fR^{n \times n}\) is real symmetric (\(\bA = \bA^T\)), then all eigenvalues of \(\bA\) are real, and \(\bA\) is orthogonally diagonalizable: \[
\begin{align}
\bA = \bQ \boldsymbol{\Lambda} \bQ^T = \sum_{i=1}^n \lambda_i \bq_i \bq_i^T,
\end{align}
\] where \(\bQ \in \fR^{n \times n}\) is real orthogonal (\(\bQ^T\bQ = \bI\)) and \(\boldsymbol{\Lambda} = \operatorname{diag}(\lambda_1, ..., \lambda_n)\) is real diagonal.








TipProof





First, let \(\lambda \in \fC\) and \(\bv \in \fC^n \setminus \{\bzero\}\) satisfy \(\bA\bv = \lambda\bv\). Taking the inner product with \(\bv\), \[
\begin{align}
\bv^*\bA\bv = \bv^*(\lambda\bv) = \lambda\|\bv\|_2^2.
\end{align}
\] Taking the conjugate transpose of this scalar and using \(\bA = \bA^T = \bA^*\), \[
\begin{align}
\bv^*\bA\bv = (\bv^*\bA\bv)^* = (\lambda\|\bv\|_2^2)^* = \bar{\lambda}\|\bv\|_2^2.
\end{align}
\] Subtracting these two scalar expressions gives \[
\begin{align}
(\lambda - \bar{\lambda})\|\bv\|_2^2 = 0.
\end{align}
\] Because \(\bv \neq \bzero\), \(\|\bv\|_2^2 &gt; 0\), forcing \(\lambda = \bar{\lambda} \in \fR\). Thus all eigenvalues are real.
Real eigenvalues alone do not yet give an orthogonal basis. We now construct that basis by reducing the dimension, retaining symmetry at every reduction.
Because \(\lambda_1 \in \fR\) and \(\bA\) is real, writing \(\bv = \bx + i\by\) gives \(\bA\bx = \lambda_1\bx\) and \(\bA\by = \lambda_1\by\). At least one of \(\bx,\by\) is nonzero, yielding a real unit eigenvector \(\bq_1\). Extend \(\bq_1\) to an orthogonal matrix \(\bQ_1 = [\bq_1, \bW] \in \fR^{n \times n}\) with \(\bW \in \fR^{n \times (n-1)}\). Because \(\bA\bq_1 = \lambda_1\bq_1\) and \(\bW^T\bq_1 = \bzero\): \[
\begin{align}
\bQ_1^T\bA\bQ_1
    =
    \begin{pmatrix} \bq_1^T \\ \bW^T \end{pmatrix} \bA \begin{pmatrix} \bq_1 & \bW \end{pmatrix}
    =
    \begin{pmatrix} \bq_1^T\bA\bq_1 & \bq_1^T\bA\bW \\ \bW^T\bA\bq_1 & \bW^T\bA\bW \end{pmatrix}
    =
    \begin{pmatrix} \lambda_1 & (\bW^T\bA\bq_1)^T \\ \bzero & \bA_1 \end{pmatrix}
    =
    \begin{pmatrix} \lambda_1 & \bzero^T \\ \bzero & \bA_1 \end{pmatrix},
\end{align}
\] The upper-right block vanishes for a specific reason: \(\bq_1^T\bA\bW=(\bW^T\bA\bq_1)^T\), and the lower-left block is zero. This equality uses symmetry. Without it, Schur form would leave a possibly nonzero upper-right block.
where \(\bA_1 = \bW^T\bA\bW \in \fR^{(n-1)\times(n-1)}\) is symmetric. Indeed, \(\bA_1^T=\bW^T\bA^T\bW=\bA_1\). For dimension one the theorem is immediate. At each larger dimension we may therefore apply the induction hypothesis to this real symmetric trailing block.
By induction on dimension, there exists an orthogonal \(\bQ_2 \in \fR^{(n-1)\times(n-1)}\) such that \(\bQ_2^T\bA_1\bQ_2 = \operatorname{diag}(\lambda_2, ..., \lambda_n)\). The matrix \[
\begin{align}
\bQ = \bQ_1 \begin{pmatrix} 1 & \bzero \\ \bzero & \bQ_2 \end{pmatrix}
\end{align}
\] is orthogonal and satisfies \(\bQ^T\bA\bQ = \boldsymbol{\Lambda}\), so \(\bA = \bQ\boldsymbol{\Lambda}\bQ^T\).
Reading the columns of \(\bA\bQ=\bQ\boldsymbol\Lambda\) shows \(\bA\bq_i=\lambda_i\bq_i\) for every column. Orthogonality of \(\bQ\) makes these vectors an orthonormal basis. This verifies the eigenvector assertion as well as the displayed factorization.



For a nonzero vector, define its Rayleigh quotient by \(R(\bx)=\bx^T\bA\bx/(\bx^T\bx)\). In an orthonormal eigenvector basis, \(\bx=\sum_i c_i\bq_i\) gives \[
\begin{align}
R(\bx)=\frac{\sum_i\lambda_i c_i^2}{\sum_i c_i^2}.
\end{align}
\] This is a weighted average of the eigenvalues, so its minimum and maximum are the smallest and largest eigenvalues. To isolate an intermediate eigenvalue, we restrict the quotient to subspaces of a specified dimension.






WarningExercise



For \(\bA=\operatorname{diag}(1,3,7)\), find the extreme Rayleigh quotients on \(\operatorname{span}\{\be_1,\be_2\}\) and on \(\operatorname{span}\{\be_2,\be_3\}\). Explain why every plane through the origin intersects \(\operatorname{span}\{\be_2,\be_3\}\) in a nonzero vector. Use that fact to bound the largest quotient on any plane from below by \(3\).








NoteTheorem: Courant–Fischer Minimax Theorem



Let \(\bA \in \fR^{n \times n}\) be real symmetric with eigenvalues ordered as \(\lambda_1 \leq \lambda_2 \leq ... \leq \lambda_n\). Then for each \(k \in \{1, ..., n\}\), \[
\begin{align}
\lambda_k = \min_{\dim(S) = k} \max_{\bx \in S,\, \bx \neq \bzero} \frac{\bx^T \bA \bx}{\bx^T \bx}
    =
    \max_{\dim(S) = n - k + 1} \min_{\bx \in S,\, \bx \neq \bzero} \frac{\bx^T \bA \bx}{\bx^T \bx}.
\end{align}
\]








TipProof





Let \(\bq_1, ..., \bq_n\) be an orthonormal basis of eigenvectors of \(\bA\) with \(\bA\bq_i = \lambda_i \bq_i\). For any nonzero \(\bx = \sum_{i=1}^n c_i \bq_i\), the Rayleigh quotient satisfies \[
\begin{align}
R(\bx) = \frac{\bx^T\bA\bx}{\bx^T\bx} = \frac{\sum_{i=1}^n \lambda_i c_i^2}{\sum_{i=1}^n c_i^2}.
\end{align}
\] The quotient is unchanged under rescaling, so each maximum can equivalently be taken on the unit sphere of its subspace. That sphere is compact, which ensures the stated extrema are attained.
Define the subspace \(V_k = \operatorname{span}\{\bq_1, ..., \bq_k\}\), which has dimension \(k\). For any nonzero \(\bx \in V_k\), \(c_i = 0\) for all \(i &gt; k\), so \[
\begin{align}
R(\bx) = \frac{\sum_{i=1}^k \lambda_i c_i^2}{\sum_{i=1}^k c_i^2} \leq \lambda_k \frac{\sum_{i=1}^k c_i^2}{\sum_{i=1}^k c_i^2} = \lambda_k.
\end{align}
\] Because \(\bq_k \in V_k\) achieves \(R(\bq_k) = \lambda_k\), we have \(\max_{\bx \in V_k,\, \bx \neq \bzero} R(\bx) = \lambda_k\).
Now let \(S\) be any arbitrary subspace of \(\fR^n\) with \(\dim(S) = k\). Define \(W_{k-1} = \operatorname{span}\{\bq_k, ..., \bq_n\}\), which has dimension \(n - k + 1\). By the dimension formula, \[
\begin{align}
\dim(S \cap W_{k-1}) = \dim(S) + \dim(W_{k-1}) - \dim(S + W_{k-1}) \geq k + (n - k + 1) - n = 1.
\end{align}
\] Therefore, there exists a nonzero vector \(\by \in S \cap W_{k-1}\). Because \(\by \in W_{k-1}\), its expansion \(\by = \sum_{i=k}^n d_i \bq_i\) contains only modes \(i \geq k\), so \[
\begin{align}
R(\by) = \frac{\sum_{i=k}^n \lambda_i d_i^2}{\sum_{i=k}^n d_i^2} \geq \lambda_k \frac{\sum_{i=k}^n d_i^2}{\sum_{i=k}^n d_i^2} = \lambda_k.
\end{align}
\] Because \(\by \in S\), the maximum over \(S\) satisfies \(\max_{\bx \in S,\, \bx \neq \bzero} R(\bx) \geq R(\by) \geq \lambda_k\). Taking the minimum over all \(k\)-dimensional subspaces \(S\) proves the first equality. For the second characterization, the eigenvalues of \(-\bA\) in increasing order are \(-\lambda_n,...,-\lambda_1\). Apply the first characterization to its index \(n-k+1\): \[
\begin{align}
-\lambda_k
=\min_{\dim S=n-k+1}\max_{\bx\in S\setminus\{0\}}[-R(\bx)].
\end{align}
\] For each fixed \(S\), \(\max(-R)=-\min R\). Negating the outer minimum changes it into a maximum. Thus \[
\begin{align}
\lambda_k=\max_{\dim S=n-k+1}\min_{\bx\in S\setminus\{0\}}R(\bx),
\end{align}
\] which proves the second equality without leaving an additional argument implicit.









NoteTheorem: Cauchy Interlacing Theorem



Let \(\bA\in\fR^{n\times n}\) be symmetric, and let \(\bB\in\fR^{(n-1)\times(n-1)}\) be a principal submatrix. If \[
\begin{align}
\lambda_1\leq\cdots\leq\lambda_n,
    \qquad
    \mu_1\leq\cdots\leq\mu_{n-1}
\end{align}
\] are the eigenvalues of \(\bA\) and \(\bB\), respectively, then \[
\begin{align}
\lambda_k\leq\mu_k\leq\lambda_{k+1},
    \qquad k=1,...,n-1.
\end{align}
\]








TipProof





After a simultaneous permutation of rows and columns, we may assume that \(\bB\) is obtained by deleting the last row and column. Identify \(\fR^{n-1}\) with the coordinate hyperplane \[
\begin{align}
H=\{\bx\in\fR^n:x_n=0\}.
\end{align}
\] For \(\by\in\fR^{n-1}\), insert a final zero to get \(\bx=(\by,0)^T\). Block multiplication gives \(\bx^T\bA\bx=\by^T\bB\by\) and \(\bx^T\bx=\by^T\by\). Thus the identification preserves both the dimension of a subspace and its Rayleigh quotient.
For every nonzero \(\bx\in H\), the Rayleigh quotients of \(\bA\) and \(\bB\) agree.
Apply the first Courant–Fischer characterization from the referenced result. The minimization defining \(\mu_k\) ranges only over \(k\)-dimensional subspaces of \(H\), whereas the minimization defining \(\lambda_k\) ranges over all \(k\)-dimensional subspaces of \(\fR^n\). Restricting the admissible set cannot decrease the minimum, so \(\lambda_k\leq\mu_k\).
For the other inequality, use the dual characterization. Since \(\dim(H)=n-1\), \[
\begin{align}
\mu_k
    &=\max_{\substack{S\subset H\\ \dim(S)=n-k}}
      \min_{\bx\in S,\,\bx\neq\bzero}R_{\bA}(\bx)
    \leq\max_{\substack{S\subset\fR^n\\ \dim(S)=n-k}}
      \min_{\bx\in S,\,\bx\neq\bzero}R_{\bA}(\bx)
      =\lambda_{k+1}.
\end{align}
\] Together the two bounds give the interlacing inequalities.
In the dual formula, the subspaces for \(\bB\) have dimension \((n-1)-k+1=n-k\). The same dimension in the formula for \(\bA\) corresponds to its eigenvalue with index \(k+1\). This index calculation explains the one-step shift in the upper bound.









WarningExercise



(Cauchy interlacing on a concrete matrix) For \[
\begin{align}
\bA=\begin{pmatrix}2&1&0\\1&2&1\\0&1&2\end{pmatrix},
\end{align}
\] compute the eigenvalues of \(\bA\) and of the leading \(2\times2\) principal submatrix. Verify each inequality in the referenced result. Repeat after deleting the middle row and column.

13.3 Sensitivity of Eigenvalues
Every eigenvalue algorithm computes the eigenvalues of a matrix that differs from \(\bA\) by rounding errors, and every iterative eigensolver stops with an approximate eigenpair whose residual is small but nonzero. We therefore need to know how far eigenvalues move when the matrix is perturbed. For symmetric matrices the answer follows from the referenced result, and eigenvalues move no farther than the norm of the perturbation. For a general diagonalizable matrix the answer also depends on the condition number of the eigenvector basis.






NoteTheorem: Weyl’s Perturbation Theorem



Let \(\bA\) and \(\bE\) be real symmetric \(n\times n\) matrices. Let \(\lambda_1\leq\cdots\leq\lambda_n\) be the eigenvalues of \(\bA\) and \(\mu_1\leq\cdots\leq\mu_n\) the eigenvalues of \(\bA+\bE\). Then \[
\begin{align}
|\mu_k-\lambda_k|\leq\|\bE\|_2,\qquad k=1,...,n.
\end{align}
\]








TipProof





Write \(R_{\bA}(\bx)=\bx^T\bA\bx/(\bx^T\bx)\) for a nonzero vector. Subtract the two quotients: \[
\begin{align}
R_{\bA+\bE}(\bx)-R_{\bA}(\bx)=\frac{\bx^T\bE\bx}{\|\bx\|_2^2}.
\end{align}
\] Cauchy–Schwarz and consistency bound the numerator: \[
\begin{align}
|\bx^T\bE\bx|\leq\|\bx\|_2\|\bE\bx\|_2
\leq\|\bE\|_2\|\bx\|_2^2.
\end{align}
\] After division, this gives the pointwise inequalities \[
\begin{align}
R_{\bA}(\bx)-\|\bE\|_2
\leq R_{\bA+\bE}(\bx)
\leq R_{\bA}(\bx)+\|\bE\|_2.
\end{align}
\] Fix a subspace \(S\) of dimension \(k\). Each inequality holds for every nonzero vector of \(S\), so it also holds after taking the maximum over those vectors. A constant independent of the vector passes unchanged through the maximum.
Now take the minimum over all such subspaces. The same constant is independent of \(S\), so it again passes unchanged through this operation. Courant–Fischer identifies the resulting middle value with \(\mu_k\) and the other two with \(\lambda_k\pm\|\bE\|_2\). Both matrices are symmetric, as required to use that theorem. Hence \[
\begin{align}
\lambda_k-\|\bE\|_2\leq\mu_k\leq\lambda_k+\|\bE\|_2,
\end{align}
\] which is equivalent to \(|\mu_k-\lambda_k|\leq\|\bE\|_2\). This classical perturbation theorem is treated in Horn and Johnson (2012).









NoteCorollary: Residual Bounds for Symmetric Matrices



Let \(\bA\) be real symmetric, let \(\bx\) be a unit vector, let \(\theta\) be real, and let \(\br=\bA\bx-\theta\bx\).

Some eigenvalue \(\lambda\) of \(\bA\) satisfies \(|\lambda-\theta|\leq\|\br\|_2\).
Suppose \(\theta=\bx^T\bA\bx\), let \(\lambda\) be an eigenvalue of \(\bA\) nearest to \(\theta\), and let \(\delta&gt;0\) be the distance from \(\theta\) to the remaining eigenvalues. Then \[
\begin{align}
|\lambda-\theta|\leq\frac{\|\br\|_2^2}{\delta}.
\end{align}
\]









TipProof





Expand \(\bx=\sum_ic_i\bq_i\) in an orthonormal eigenvector basis, so that \(\sum_ic_i^2=1\) and \(\br=\sum_i(\lambda_i-\theta)c_i\bq_i\). Then \[
\begin{align}
\|\br\|_2^2=\sum_{i=1}^n(\lambda_i-\theta)^2c_i^2\geq\min_i(\lambda_i-\theta)^2,
\end{align}
\] because each squared distance is at least its minimum and the nonnegative weights \(c_i^2\) sum to one. Taking square roots gives \(\min_i|\lambda_i-\theta|\leq\|\br\|_2\). The minimum is attained at some eigenvalue because there are finitely many of them.
For the second claim, the Rayleigh quotient condition is \(\sum_i(\lambda_i-\theta)c_i^2=0\). Suppose \(\lambda\leq\theta\), since the case \(\lambda\geq\theta\) follows by applying the argument to \(-\bA\). Let \(\beta=\theta+\delta\).
An eigenvalue \(\lambda_i\neq\lambda\) cannot lie in \((\lambda,\theta]\), because it would then be closer to \(\theta\) than \(\lambda\). Such an eigenvalue also cannot lie in \((\theta,\beta)\), by the definition of \(\delta\). Hence every such eigenvalue satisfies \(\lambda_i&lt;\lambda\) or \(\lambda_i\geq\beta\), and in both cases \((\lambda_i-\lambda)(\lambda_i-\beta)\geq0\). The product vanishes when \(\lambda_i=\lambda\).
Writing \(\lambda_i-\lambda=(\lambda_i-\theta)+(\theta-\lambda)\) and \(\lambda_i-\beta=(\lambda_i-\theta)-\delta\) and expanding gives \[
\begin{align}
0\leq\sum_{i=1}^n(\lambda_i-\lambda)(\lambda_i-\beta)c_i^2
    =\|\br\|_2^2+(\theta-\lambda-\delta)\sum_{i=1}^n(\lambda_i-\theta)c_i^2-(\theta-\lambda)\delta
    =\|\br\|_2^2-(\theta-\lambda)\delta.
\end{align}
\] For clarity, the expansion in the last display uses \(a=\lambda_i-\theta\) and \(d=\theta-\lambda\): \[
\begin{align}
(a+d)(a-\delta)=a^2+(d-\delta)a-d\delta.
\end{align}
\] Summing the first term gives the squared residual norm. The middle term vanishes because \(\theta=\bx^T\bA\bx\). The last term is \(-d\delta\) because the weights sum to one. These are the three contributions in the displayed inequality.
Therefore \((\theta-\lambda)\delta\leq\|\br\|_2^2\).



The first bound states that a small residual locates an eigenvalue of a symmetric matrix to within the residual norm. The second bound shows that the Rayleigh quotient is much more accurate than its residual when the eigenvalue is well separated from the rest of the spectrum. An eigenvector residual of \(10^{-4}\) and a gap of order one, for example, give an eigenvalue error of order \(10^{-8}\).






NoteTheorem: Bauer–Fike Theorem



Let \(\bA=\bX\boldsymbol{\Lambda}\bX^{-1}\) be diagonalizable with eigenvalues \(\lambda_1,...,\lambda_n\), and let \(\bE\) be any \(n\times n\) matrix. Every eigenvalue \(\mu\) of \(\bA+\bE\) satisfies \[
\begin{align}
\min_{1\leq i\leq n}|\lambda_i-\mu|\leq\kappa_2(\bX)\|\bE\|_2,
\end{align}
\] where \(\kappa_2(\bX)=\|\bX\|_2\|\bX^{-1}\|_2\) is computed in the Euclidean norm on \(\fC^n\).








TipProof





Let \(\mu\) be an eigenvalue of \(\bA+\bE\) and choose a nonzero eigenvector \(\by\). If \(\mu\) is already an eigenvalue of \(\bA\), its distance to that spectrum is zero and the estimate follows. Assume it is not.
Write \(\bA=\bX\boldsymbol\Lambda\bX^{-1}\) and put \(\bz=\bX^{-1}\by\). This vector is nonzero because \(\bX\) is invertible. Multiply \((\bA+\bE)\by=\mu\by\) by \(\bX^{-1}\) and substitute \(\by=\bX\bz\): \[
\begin{align}
(\boldsymbol\Lambda-\mu\bI)\bz=-\bX^{-1}\bE\bX\bz.
\end{align}
\] Every diagonal entry \(\lambda_i-\mu\) is nonzero, so the diagonal matrix is invertible. Therefore \[
\begin{align}
\bz=-(\boldsymbol\Lambda-\mu\bI)^{-1}\bX^{-1}\bE\bX\bz.
\end{align}
\] Take Euclidean norms and divide by \(\|\bz\|_2&gt;0\): \[
\begin{align}
1\leq\|(\boldsymbol\Lambda-\mu\bI)^{-1}\|_2
       \|\bX^{-1}\|_2\|\bE\|_2\|\bX\|_2.
\end{align}
\] For a diagonal matrix the Euclidean norm is the largest diagonal magnitude. Here it is \[
\begin{align}
\max_i\frac1{|\lambda_i-\mu|}
=\frac1{\min_i|\lambda_i-\mu|}.
\end{align}
\] Multiply the norm inequality by the positive denominator. The result is \(\min_i|\lambda_i-\mu|\leq\kappa_2(\bX)\|\bE\|_2\). This is the Bauer–Fike theorem (Horn and Johnson 2012; Golub and Van Loan 2013).









NoteCorollary: Residual Bound for Diagonalizable Matrices



Let \(\bA=\bX\boldsymbol{\Lambda}\bX^{-1}\), let \(\bx\) be a unit vector, let \(\theta\in\fC\), and let \(\br=\bA\bx-\theta\bx\). Then some eigenvalue \(\lambda\) of \(\bA\) satisfies \(|\lambda-\theta|\leq\kappa_2(\bX)\|\br\|_2\).








TipProof





The unit-norm hypothesis gives \(\bx^*\bx=1\), where the star denotes conjugate transpose. Define the rank-one perturbation \(\bE=-\br\bx^*\). Its action on this particular vector is \[
\begin{align}
\bE\bx=-\br(\bx^*\bx)=-\br.
\end{align}
\] Since \(\br=\bA\bx-\theta\bx\), we obtain \[
\begin{align}
(\bA+\bE)\bx=\bA\bx-\br=\theta\bx.
\end{align}
\] The vector is nonzero, so \(\theta\) is an exact eigenvalue of the perturbed matrix.
For any unit vector \(\by\), Cauchy–Schwarz gives \[
\begin{align}
\|\bE\by\|_2=\|\br\|_2|\bx^*\by|\leq\|\br\|_2.
\end{align}
\] Equality is attained at \(\by=\bx\), because \(|\bx^*\bx|=1\). Thus \(\|\bE\|_2=\|\br\|_2\). Applying Bauer–Fike to this explicitly constructed perturbation gives the stated residual bound. The argument concerns eigenvalue location, not the distance to a particular eigenvector.



For a symmetric matrix, the referenced result provides an orthogonal \(\bX\) with \(\kappa_2(\bX)=1\), and the referenced result reduces to the first part of the referenced result. For a nonnormal matrix, \(\kappa_2(\bX)\) can be large, and a small residual then locates an eigenvalue only loosely. Near a defective matrix the eigenvalues can move much farther than the perturbation, as the following example shows.






NoteExample



(Eigenvalues of a nearly defective matrix) The matrix \[
\begin{align}
\bA_\varepsilon=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\ \varepsilon&0\end{pmatrix}
\end{align}
\] differs from the defective matrix \(\bA_0\) by a perturbation of norm \(\varepsilon\), but its eigenvalues are \(\pm\sqrt{\varepsilon}\). For \(\varepsilon=10^{-10}\), a perturbation at the level of \(10^{-10}\) moves the double eigenvalue zero by \(10^{-5}\). The eigenvectors \((1,\pm\sqrt{\varepsilon})^T\) of \(\bA_\varepsilon\) become parallel as \(\varepsilon\to0\), so \(\kappa_2(\bX)\) grows without bound, in agreement with the referenced result.

14.1 Power Iteration and Shifted Inverse Iteration
To compute an eigenpair approximately, we seek a unit vector \(\bv\) and scalar \(\theta\) for which \(\bA\bv-\theta\bv\) is small. The residual bounds of the preceding section explain what such a calculation establishes for symmetric matrices.
We first multiply a vector repeatedly by the matrix, then replace multiplication by the solution of a shifted linear system to select other eigenvalues. These methods approximate one eigenpair at a time. The QR algorithm instead changes the whole matrix by orthogonal similarity transformations to expose all its eigenvalues.
The convergence claim comes from expanding the starting vector in an orthonormal eigenbasis. Write \(\bv^{(0)}=\sum_i c_i\bq_i\) with \(c_1\neq0\). Then \[
\begin{align}
\bA^k\bv^{(0)}
=\lambda_1^k\left(c_1\bq_1+\sum_{i=2}^nc_i(\lambda_i/\lambda_1)^k\bq_i\right).
\end{align}
\] Each unwanted component decreases relative to the first at least as fast as \(|\lambda_2/\lambda_1|^k\). Normalizing removes the common scale. A negative dominant eigenvalue can alternate the sign of the vector, so convergence is to a direction.
The vectors used in the shift formula must have unit norm. Before solving the next shifted system, test the eigenpair residual. At an exact eigenpair, the shift makes the matrix singular and the calculation should stop. A singular shift can also occur away from an eigenvector, so the formula alone is not a globally defined iteration. The local theorem below concerns steps for which the shifted solve exists, with exact convergence treated as termination.

NoteDefinition: Power Iteration



Let \(\bA\) be real symmetric. Given a unit vector \(\bv^{(0)}\), power iteration constructs \[
\begin{align}
\bw^{(k)} = \bA \bv^{(k-1)}, \qquad \bv^{(k)} = \frac{\bw^{(k)}}{\|\bw^{(k)}\|_2}, \qquad \lambda^{(k)} = (\bv^{(k)})^T \bA \bv^{(k)}.
\end{align}
\] Order the eigenvalues by magnitude. If \(|\lambda_1|&gt;|\lambda_2|\geq\cdots\geq|\lambda_n|\) and \(\bq_1^T\bv^{(0)}\neq0\), the direction of the iterate converges linearly: \[
\begin{align}
\|\bv^{(k)} - (\pm \bq_1)\|_2 = O\left( \left| \frac{\lambda_2}{\lambda_1} \right|^k \right).
\end{align}
\] For a general nonnormal matrix, an eigenvector basis may be poorly conditioned or may not exist, and this simple orthogonal expansion no longer justifies the claim.









WarningExercise



For \(\bA=\operatorname{diag}(4,2,1)\), write the normalized power iterate explicitly when \(\bv^{(0)}=(1,1,1)^T/\sqrt3\). Bound the angle to \(\be_1\). What changes if the starting vector is \((0,1,1)^T/\sqrt2\)? What fails if the first diagonal entry is replaced by \(-2\)?








NoteDefinition: Shifted Inverse Iteration



For a real symmetric matrix, an eigenvalue near a target \(\sigma\) can be isolated by applying power iteration implicitly to \((\bA-\sigma\bI)^{-1}\): \[
\begin{align}
(\bA - \sigma \bI) \bw^{(k)} = \bv^{(k-1)}, \qquad \bv^{(k)} = \frac{\bw^{(k)}}{\|\bw^{(k)}\|_2}.
\end{align}
\] The eigenvalues of \((\bA - \sigma \bI)^{-1}\) are \((\lambda_i - \sigma)^{-1}\). If exactly one eigenvalue is closest to \(\sigma\), the shift is not an eigenvalue, and the starting vector has a component in its eigenspace, that eigenspace dominates the iteration. The matrix inverse is never formed. Each step solves a shifted linear system instead.








NoteDefinition: Rayleigh Quotient Iteration (RQI)



For symmetric matrices, updating the shift dynamically to the current Rayleigh estimate \(\sigma_k = (\bv^{(k)})^T \bA \bv^{(k)}\) defines Rayleigh Quotient Iteration: \[
\begin{align}
(\bA - \sigma_k \bI) \bw^{(k+1)} = \bv^{(k)}, \qquad \bv^{(k+1)} = \frac{\bw^{(k+1)}}{\|\bw^{(k+1)}\|_2}, \qquad \sigma_{k+1} = (\bv^{(k+1)})^T \bA \bv^{(k+1)}.
\end{align}
\]









NoteTheorem: Local Cubic Convergence of Rayleigh Quotient Iteration



Let \(\bA\) be real symmetric, and let \(\lambda_*\) be a simple eigenvalue with unit eigenvector \(\bq_*\). If a unit RQI iterate \(\bv^{(k)}\) is sufficiently close to \(\bq_*\), then \[
\begin{align}
\sin\angle(\bv^{(k+1)},\bq_*)
    \leq C\sin^3\angle(\bv^{(k)},\bq_*).
\end{align}
\] If \(\lambda_*\) is an extremal eigenvalue, the Rayleigh shifts also satisfy \[
\begin{align}
|\sigma_{k+1}-\lambda_*|
    \leq C'|\sigma_k-\lambda_*|^3
\end{align}
\] for iterates sufficiently close to \(\bq_*\).








TipProof





Write the current unit vector as \[
\begin{align}
\bv=\cos\theta\,\bq_*+\sin\theta\,\bz,
    \qquad
    \bz\perp\bq_*,
    \qquad
    \|\bz\|_2=1.
\end{align}
\] Its Rayleigh quotient \(\sigma=\bv^T\bA\bv\) obeys \[
\begin{align}
\sigma
    &= (\cos\theta\,\bq_*+\sin\theta\,\bz)^T\bA(\cos\theta\,\bq_*+\sin\theta\,\bz) \\
    &= \cos^2\theta\,\lambda_* + 2\cos\theta\sin\theta\,\bq_*^T\bA\bz + \sin^2\theta\,\bz^T\bA\bz.
\end{align}
\] Because \(\bA\bq_* = \lambda_*\bq_*\) and \(\bq_*^T\bz = 0\), the cross-term vanishes: \(\bq_*^T\bA\bz = \lambda_*\bq_*^T\bz = 0\). Substituting \(\cos^2\theta = 1-\sin^2\theta\), \[
\begin{align}
\sigma-\lambda_*
    = (1-\sin^2\theta)\lambda_* + \sin^2\theta\,\bz^T\bA\bz - \lambda_*
    = \sin^2\theta\,(\bz^T\bA\bz-\lambda_*),
\end{align}
\] so \(|\sigma-\lambda_*|\leq C_0\sin^2\theta\).
For example, the first constant can be taken as \(C_0=\|\bA-\lambda_*\bI\|_2\), because \(|\bz^T(\bA-\lambda_*\bI)\bz|\leq C_0\) for unit \(\bz\). Thus it does not depend on the current angle.
Let \(\gamma=\min_{\lambda_j\neq\lambda_*}|\lambda_j-\lambda_*|&gt;0\). Once \(\theta\) is small enough, \(|\lambda_j-\sigma|\geq\gamma/2\) for every other eigenvalue. Expanding the inverse-iteration vector \(\bw=(\bA-\sigma\bI)^{-1}\bv\) in an orthonormal eigenbasis, its component along \(\bq_*\) has magnitude \[
\begin{align}
\frac{|\cos\theta|}{|\lambda_*-\sigma|},
\end{align}
\] whereas the norm of its orthogonal component is at most \(2|\sin\theta|/\gamma\). More explicitly, expand \(\bz=\sum_{j\neq *}a_j\bq_j\) with \(\sum a_j^2=1\). The squared perpendicular-component norm is \[
\begin{align}
\sin^2\theta\sum_{j\neq *}\frac{a_j^2}{|\lambda_j-\sigma|^2}
\leq\frac{4\sin^2\theta}{\gamma^2}.
\end{align}
\] Normalization does not change the ratio of perpendicular and parallel component lengths, which is the tangent of the acute angle. Therefore, \[
\begin{align}
\tan\angle(\bv^+,\bq_*)
    \leq
    \frac{2|\lambda_*-\sigma|}{\gamma|\cos\theta|}|\sin\theta|
    \leq C_1|\sin\theta|^3.
\end{align}
\] For small angles, \(\sin\theta\) and \(\tan\theta\) are comparable, which proves cubic convergence of the eigenvector direction.
For angles at most \(\pi/4\), \(|\cos\theta|\geq1/\sqrt2\), so the denominator stays uniformly away from zero. The cubic bound therefore has a constant independent of sufficiently close iterates. We consider only nonsingular shifted solves, as stipulated before the theorem.
If \(\lambda_*\) is extremal, the spectral gap also gives two-sided local bounds \(c\sin^2\theta\leq|\sigma-\lambda_*|\leq C\sin^2\theta\). Applying the Rayleigh-quotient identity to the next iterate and using the cubic angle bound yields \[
\begin{align}
|\sigma^+-\lambda_*|
    \leq C_2\sin^2\angle(\bv^+,\bq_*)
    \leq C_3\sin^6\theta
    \leq C_4|\sigma-\lambda_*|^3.
\end{align}
\]
For an extremal simple eigenvalue, all differences \(\lambda_j-\lambda_*\) have one sign and magnitudes at least \(\gamma\). Hence their weighted average in the Rayleigh error cannot cancel, giving the lower bound \(\gamma\sin^2\theta\leq|\sigma-\lambda_*|\). This is the additional fact used to convert the angle estimate to a cubic eigenvalue estimate.

14.3 The Jacobi Eigenvalue Algorithm
For a symmetric matrix, an alternative to the QR algorithm drives the off-diagonal entries to zero directly. Each step applies an orthogonal similarity by a plane rotation chosen to annihilate one off-diagonal entry. The method, due to Jacobi in 1846, is slower than shifted QR for a general dense matrix. It remains useful because it can compute the small eigenvalues of graded positive definite matrices to high relative accuracy (Demmel 1997), and because rotations acting on disjoint pairs of indices can be applied in parallel.






NoteDefinition: Givens Plane Rotation Matrix



The matrix \(\bG(p, q, \theta) \in \fR^{n \times n}\) is an identity matrix except for the four entries at the intersections of rows and columns \(p\) and \(q\): \[
\begin{align}
g_{pp} = c, \qquad g_{pq} = s, \qquad g_{qp} = -s, \qquad g_{qq} = c,
\end{align}
\] where \(c = \cos\theta\) and \(s = \sin\theta\). The transformation \(\bA' = \bG^T \bA \bG\) modifies only rows and columns \(p\) and \(q\) of \(\bA\).








NoteTheorem: Derivation of the Optimal Rotation Angle



To zero the transformed off-diagonal entry \(a'_{pq} = 0\), we consider the \(2 \times 2\) orthogonal similarity transformation on the submatrix at indices \(\{p, q\}\): \[
\begin{align}
\begin{pmatrix} a'_{pp} & a'_{pq} \\ a'_{pq} & a'_{qq} \end{pmatrix}
    &=
    \begin{pmatrix} c & -s \\ s & c \end{pmatrix}
    \begin{pmatrix} a_{pp} & a_{pq} \\ a_{pq} & a_{qq} \end{pmatrix}
    \begin{pmatrix} c & s \\ -s & c \end{pmatrix} \\
    &=
    \begin{pmatrix}
        c^2 a_{pp} - 2cs a_{pq} + s^2 a_{qq} & (c^2 - s^2) a_{pq} + cs (a_{pp} - a_{qq}) \\
        (c^2 - s^2) a_{pq} + cs (a_{pp} - a_{qq}) & s^2 a_{pp} + 2cs a_{pq} + c^2 a_{qq}
    \end{pmatrix}.
\end{align}
\] Setting \(a'_{pq} = 0\) yields the trigonometric condition \[
\begin{align}
(c^2 - s^2) a_{pq} + cs (a_{pp} - a_{qq}) = 0
    \implies
    \frac{\cos(2\theta)}{\sin(2\theta)} = \frac{a_{qq} - a_{pp}}{2a_{pq}}.
\end{align}
\] Defining \(\tau = \cot(2\theta) = \frac{a_{qq} - a_{pp}}{2a_{pq}}\) and using \(\cot(2\theta) = \frac{1 - \tan^2\theta}{2\tan\theta}\), setting \(t = \tan\theta\) produces \[
\begin{align}
t^2 + 2\tau t - 1 = 0.
\end{align}
\] If \(a_{pq}=0\), no rotation is needed. Otherwise, to choose a rotation of magnitude at most \(\pi/4\) and avoid cancellation, select the root of smaller absolute value, with \(\operatorname{sign}(0)=1\): \[
\begin{align}
t = \frac{\operatorname{sign}(\tau)}{|\tau| + \sqrt{1 + \tau^2}}, \qquad c = \frac{1}{\sqrt{1 + t^2}}, \qquad s = ct.
\end{align}
\]








TipProof





The scalar equation is \(t^2+2\tau t-1=0\). Its roots are \(-\tau\pm\sqrt{1+\tau^2}\), and their product is \(-1\). Thus, unless both have magnitude one, one root has magnitude less than one and the other greater than one.
If \(\tau&gt;0\), the smaller-magnitude root is \(\sqrt{1+\tau^2}-\tau\). Rationalize it: \[
\begin{align}
\sqrt{1+\tau^2}-\tau
=\frac{(1+\tau^2)-\tau^2}{\sqrt{1+\tau^2}+\tau}
=\frac1{\sqrt{1+\tau^2}+\tau}.
\end{align}
\] If \(\tau&lt;0\), put \(a=-\tau&gt;0\). The smaller root is \(a-\sqrt{1+a^2}=-1/(a+\sqrt{1+a^2})\). Combining these cases gives \[
\begin{align}
t=\frac{\operatorname{sign}(\tau)}{|\tau|+\sqrt{1+\tau^2}}.
\end{align}
\] For \(\tau=0\), the roots are \(1\) and \(-1\). With the stated convention \(\operatorname{sign}(0)=1\), the formula selects \(1\).
The denominator is at least one, so \(|t|\leq1\). Set \(\theta=\arctan t\), giving \(|\theta|\leq\pi/4\), and define \(c=1/\sqrt{1+t^2}\), \(s=ct\). Then \(c^2+s^2=1\) and \(s/c=t\). Substitution into the off-diagonal formula gives zero because \(t\) satisfies the quadratic. The rationalized expression avoids subtracting two nearly equal positive numbers.









WarningExercise



Perform one Jacobi rotation by hand to zero out the off-diagonal entry of \[
\begin{align}
\bA = \begin{pmatrix} 3 & 2 \\ 2 & 6 \end{pmatrix}.
\end{align}
\] Explicitly compute \(\tau, t, c, s\), and the resulting diagonal matrix \(\bA'\).


A rotation that annihilates \(a_{pq}\) may make a previously annihilated entry nonzero again, so the method does not terminate after \(n(n-1)/2\) steps. Convergence follows instead from a quantity that decreases at every step.






NoteTheorem: Monotone Decrease of the Off-Diagonal Frobenius Norm



Let the off-diagonal squared norm be defined by \[
\begin{align}
\operatorname{off}(\bA)^2 = \sum_{i \neq j} a_{ij}^2 = \|\bA\|_F^2 - \sum_{i=1}^n a_{ii}^2.
\end{align}
\] Applying the Jacobi rotation \(\bA' = \bG(p, q, \theta)^T \bA \bG(p, q, \theta)\) with the optimal angle strictly reduces the off-diagonal norm: \[
\begin{align}
\operatorname{off}(\bA')^2 = \operatorname{off}(\bA)^2 - 2a_{pq}^2.
\end{align}
\]








TipProof





Orthogonal multiplication preserves the Frobenius norm because it preserves the Euclidean norm of each column on the left, or each row on the right. Applying this twice gives the invariance used below.
Because \(\bG\) is orthogonal, the total Frobenius norm is preserved: \(\|\bA'\|_F^2 = \|\bA\|_F^2\). The rotation affects only entries in rows and columns \(p\) and \(q\). For the \(2 \times 2\) submatrix, the sum of squares is invariant under orthogonal similarity: \[
\begin{align}
(a'_{pp})^2 + (a'_{qq})^2 + 2(a'_{pq})^2 = a_{pp}^2 + a_{qq}^2 + 2a_{pq}^2.
\end{align}
\] Because the rotation enforces \(a'_{pq} = 0\), the sum of squared diagonal entries increases: \[
\begin{align}
(a'_{pp})^2 + (a'_{qq})^2 = a_{pp}^2 + a_{qq}^2 + 2a_{pq}^2.
\end{align}
\] Subtract the old squared diagonal sum from the new one in this block. The difference is exactly \(2a_{pq}^2\). Thus diagonal energy has increased by that amount while total squared norm stays fixed.
All other diagonal entries \(a_{ii}\) (\(i \neq p, q\)) are untouched. Substituting this into \(\operatorname{off}(\bA')^2 = \|\bA'\|_F^2 - \sum (a'_{ii})^2\) proves that \(\operatorname{off}(\bA')^2 = \operatorname{off}(\bA)^2 - 2a_{pq}^2\).
In particular, a nonzero selected entry gives a strict decrease. If it was already zero and no rotation is performed, the formula gives equality. A single decreasing quantity does not by itself specify a global rate until the choice of pairs is considered.









WarningExercise



Prove that for an \(n \times n\) matrix, if the largest off-diagonal entry is selected at each step (Classical Jacobi), then \(\operatorname{off}(\bA^{(k+1)})^2 \leq \left( 1 - \frac{2}{n(n-1)} \right) \operatorname{off}(\bA^{(k)})^2\).








NoteDefinition: Cyclic Jacobi Algorithm



Let \(\bA^{(0)}=\bA\) and \(\bV=\bI_n\). While \(\operatorname{off}(\bA)&gt;`tol`\,\|\bA^{(0)}\|_F\), sweep through the pairs \(1\leq p&lt;q\leq n\) in a fixed order. For each pair, compute \(c\) and \(s\) from the referenced result, replace \(\bA\) by \(\bG(p,q,\theta)^T\bA\bG(p,q,\theta)\), and replace \(\bV\) by \(\bV\bG(p,q,\theta)\). On termination, the diagonal of \(\bA\) approximates the eigenvalues and the columns of \(\bV\) the eigenvectors.


the referenced result shows that \(\operatorname{off}(\bA)^2\) decreases by \(2a_{pq}^2\) at every rotation. Choosing the largest off-diagonal entry at each step gives linear convergence, as the exercises show. The cyclic order avoids that search, and once the off-diagonal entries are small compared with the gaps between the eigenvalues, cyclic Jacobi converges quadratically, measured sweep by sweep (Golub and Van Loan 2013). Each sweep costs \(O(n^3)\) flops.

15.3 Low-Rank Matrix Approximation






NoteTheorem: Eckart–Young–Mirsky Low-Rank Approximation Theorem



Let \(\bA \in \fR^{m \times n}\) have singular value decomposition \(\bA = \sum_{i=1}^r \sigma_i \bu_i \bv_i^T\). For any \(k &lt; r\), let the rank-\(k\) truncated SVD be \[
\begin{align}
\bA_k = \sum_{i=1}^k \sigma_i \bu_i \bv_i^T.
\end{align}
\] Then \(\bA_k\) is the best rank-\(k\) approximation to \(\bA\) in the spectral norm: \[
\begin{align}
\min_{\operatorname{rank}(\bB) \leq k} \|\bA - \bB\|_2 = \|\bA - \bA_k\|_2 = \sigma_{k+1},
\end{align}
\] and in the Frobenius norm: \[
\begin{align}
\min_{\operatorname{rank}(\bB) \leq k} \|\bA - \bB\|_F = \|\bA - \bA_k\|_F = \sqrt{\sum_{j=k+1}^r \sigma_j^2}.
\end{align}
\]








TipProof





First, evaluating the spectral norm of \(\bA - \bA_k\): \[
\begin{align}
\bA - \bA_k = \sum_{i=k+1}^r \sigma_i \bu_i \bv_i^T = \bU \operatorname{diag}(0, ..., 0, \sigma_{k+1}, ..., \sigma_r, 0, ..., 0) \bV^T.
\end{align}
\] Because the 2-norm is unitarily invariant, \(\|\bA - \bA_k\|_2 = \sigma_{k+1}\).
Now let \(\bB \in \fR^{m \times n}\) be any arbitrary matrix with \(\operatorname{rank}(\bB) \leq k\). By the Rank-Nullity Theorem, \(\dim \mathcal{N}(\bB) = n - \operatorname{rank}(\bB) \geq n - k\). Define the \((k+1)\)-dimensional subspace \(W_{k+1} = \operatorname{span}\{\bv_1, ..., \bv_{k+1}\} \subset \fR^n\). By the dimension formula, \[
\begin{align}
\dim(\mathcal{N}(\bB)\cap W_{k+1})
    &=\dim\mathcal N(\bB)+\dim W_{k+1}\\
    &\quad-\dim(\mathcal N(\bB)+W_{k+1})\\
    &\geq(n-k)+(k+1)-n=1.
\end{align}
\] Therefore, there exists a nonzero vector \(\bw \in \mathcal{N}(\bB) \cap W_{k+1}\), normalized so that \(\|\bw\|_2 = 1\).
Because \(\bw \in \mathcal{N}(\bB)\), \(\bB\bw = \bzero\). Expanding \(\bw = \sum_{j=1}^{k+1} c_j \bv_j\) with \(\sum_{j=1}^{k+1} c_j^2 = 1\), \[
\begin{align}
\|(\bA - \bB)\bw\|_2^2
    &= \|\bA\bw - \bB\bw\|_2^2
    = \|\bA\bw\|_2^2 \\
    &= \left\| \bA \sum_{j=1}^{k+1} c_j \bv_j \right\|_2^2
    = \left\| \sum_{j=1}^{k+1} c_j \sigma_j \bu_j \right\|_2^2 \\
    &= \sum_{j=1}^{k+1} c_j^2 \sigma_j^2
    \geq \sigma_{k+1}^2 \sum_{j=1}^{k+1} c_j^2
    = \sigma_{k+1}^2.
\end{align}
\] Consequently, the operator norm satisfies \[
\begin{align}
\|\bA - \bB\|_2 = \max_{\|\bx\|_2 = 1} \|(\bA - \bB)\bx\|_2 \geq \|(\bA - \bB)\bw\|_2 \geq \sigma_{k+1}.
\end{align}
\] This proves that \(\|\bA - \bB\|_2 \geq \sigma_{k+1}\) for every matrix \(\bB\) of rank at most \(k\).
For the Frobenius norm, let \(\bP\) be the orthogonal projector onto \(\mathcal{N}(\bB)\). Since \(\operatorname{rank}(\bB)\leq k\), this null space has dimension at least \(n-k\). Hence \[
\begin{align}
\|\bA-\bB\|_F^2
    \geq \|(\bA-\bB)\bP\|_F^2
    =\|\bA\bP\|_F^2.
\end{align}
\] Choose an \((n-k)\)-dimensional subspace of \(\mathcal{N}(\bB)\) if the null space is larger, and let \(\bP\) project onto that subspace. Expanding in the right singular-vector basis gives \[
\begin{align}
\|\bA\bP\|_F^2
    &= \operatorname{tr}(\bP^T\bA^T\bA\bP)
    = \operatorname{tr}\left( \bP \left(\sum_{i=1}^n \sigma_i^2 \bv_i\bv_i^T\right) \bP \right) \\
    &= \sum_{i=1}^n \sigma_i^2 \operatorname{tr}(\bP\bv_i\bv_i^T\bP)
    = \sum_{i=1}^n \sigma_i^2 \|\bP\bv_i\|_2^2.
\end{align}
\] The weights \(d_i=\|\bP\bv_i\|_2^2\) satisfy \(0\leq d_i\leq1\) and \(\sum_i d_i=\operatorname{tr}(\bP)=n-k\). To justify the minimum of this weighted sum, put \(s_i=\sigma_i^2\) in decreasing order. From \(\sum_i d_i=n-k\) we have \[
\begin{align}
\sum_{i\leq k}d_i=\sum_{i&gt;k}(1-d_i)=t\geq0.
\end{align}
\] Now subtract the desired tail sum: \[
\begin{align}
\sum_i s_i d_i-\sum_{i&gt;k}s_i
&=\sum_{i\leq k}s_i d_i-\sum_{i&gt;k}s_i(1-d_i)\\
&\geq s_{k+1}t-s_{k+1}t=0.
\end{align}
\] The inequality uses \(s_i\geq s_{k+1}\) in the first sum and \(s_i\leq s_{k+1}\) in the second. This proves the weighted minimum explicitly. Therefore \[
\begin{align}
\|\bA-\bB\|_F^2\geq\sum_{i=k+1}^r\sigma_i^2.
\end{align}
\] The truncated SVD attains this bound, completing the proof.
For the proposed truncation, its error has precisely the discarded singular values, so its squared Frobenius norm is their squared sum. For \(k\geq r\), the full matrix itself is admissible and both minimum errors are zero. The approximation theorem in these two norms is discussed in Golub and Van Loan (2013).









WarningExercise



(Low-rank error accounting) For a diagonal matrix \(\bA=\operatorname{diag}(9,4,1)\), compute the best rank-one and rank-two approximations. Verify both the spectral- and Frobenius-norm errors predicted by the referenced result.








TipRemark



(Numerical rank and noise filtering) In measured or discretized data, singular values rarely vanish exactly. Given a tolerance \(\tau&gt;0\), define the numerical rank by \[
\begin{align}
r_\tau = \#\{i:\sigma_i&gt;\tau\}.
\end{align}
\] A common floating-point default is \(\tau=\varepsilon_{\text{mach}}\max(m,n)\sigma_1\), but a scientifically meaningful threshold should reflect the noise and discretization scales. Truncation removes components below the chosen threshold. Calling those components noise requires a model of the data.

15.4 Least Squares and Regularization
The least-squares theory of the referenced section characterized every minimizer and the minimum-norm minimizer, but it computed a solution only when \(\bA\) has full column rank. The SVD computes all minimizers in every case. It also shows which components of the solution are sensitive to errors in \(\bb\), and how truncation and Tikhonov regularization modify those components.






NoteDefinition: Moore–Penrose Pseudoinverse



Let \(\bA \in \fR^{m \times n}\) have rank \(r\) and thin SVD \(\bA = \bU_1 \bsigma_1 \bV_1^T\). The Moore–Penrose pseudoinverse of \(\bA\) is \[
\begin{align}
\bA^\dagger = \bV_1 \bsigma_1^{-1} \bU_1^T = \sum_{i=1}^r \frac{1}{\sigma_i} \bv_i \bu_i^T \in \fR^{n \times m}.
\end{align}
\]








WarningExercise



(Verification of the four Moore–Penrose conditions) Using the thin SVD \(\bA = \bU_1 \bsigma_1 \bV_1^T\) and \(\bA^\dagger = \bV_1 \bsigma_1^{-1} \bU_1^T\), prove algebraically:

\(\bA\bA^\dagger\bA = \bA\).
\(\bA^\dagger\bA\bA^\dagger = \bA^\dagger\).
\((\bA\bA^\dagger)^T = \bA\bA^\dagger\) (orthogonal projector onto \(\mathcal{R}(\bA)\)).
\((\bA^\dagger\bA)^T = \bA^\dagger\bA\) (orthogonal projector onto \(\mathcal{R}(\bA^T)\)).









NoteTheorem: Least Squares by the SVD



Let \(\bA=\bU\bsigma\bV^T\) have rank \(r\). A vector \(\bx\) minimizes \(\|\bA\bx-\bb\|_2\) if and only if \[
\begin{align}
\bv_i^T\bx=\frac{\bu_i^T\bb}{\sigma_i},\qquad i=1,...,r.
\end{align}
\] The minimum residual norm is \(\big(\sum_{i=r+1}^m(\bu_i^T\bb)^2\big)^{1/2}\), and the unique minimizer of smallest Euclidean norm is \[
\begin{align}
\bA^\dagger\bb=\sum_{i=1}^r\frac{\bu_i^T\bb}{\sigma_i}\,\bv_i.
\end{align}
\]








TipProof





Introduce orthogonal coordinates \(\by=\bV^T\bx\) and \(\bc=\bU^T\bb\). These changes of coordinates are bijective and preserve Euclidean lengths. In particular, minimizing over \(\bx\) is equivalent to minimizing over every \(\by\in\fR^n\).
Multiply the residual by \(\bU^T\) and insert the SVD: \[
\begin{align}
\|\bA\bx-\bb\|_2^2
&=\|\bU^T(\bU\bsigma\bV^T\bx-\bb)\|_2^2\\
&=\|\bsigma\by-\bc\|_2^2\\
&=\sum_{i=1}^r(\sigma_iy_i-c_i)^2+\sum_{i=r+1}^m c_i^2.
\end{align}
\] The final sum is fixed by the data and cannot be changed by the solution. Each term in the first sum is nonnegative. Since \(\sigma_i&gt;0\) for \(i\leq r\), choose \(y_i=c_i/\sigma_i\) to make every such term zero. This proves the minimum residual value and identifies all necessary constrained coordinates.
The coordinates \(y_{r+1},...,y_n\) never appear in the residual because the corresponding columns of \(\bsigma\) vanish. They can therefore be arbitrary in a least-squares minimizer. To choose the minimizer of smallest norm, write \[
\begin{align}
\|\bx\|_2^2=\|\by\|_2^2
=\sum_{i=1}^r\frac{c_i^2}{\sigma_i^2}+\sum_{i=r+1}^n y_i^2.
\end{align}
\] The first sum is already fixed. The second is minimized uniquely by setting every free coordinate to zero. Transforming back gives \[
\begin{align}
\bx_{\min}=\sum_{i=1}^r\frac{\bu_i^T\bb}{\sigma_i}\bv_i
=\bA^\dagger\bb.
\end{align}
\] Empty index ranges, including rank zero, are interpreted as empty sums.



The coefficient \(\bu_i^T\bb/\sigma_i\) shows how errors in the data enter the solution. If \(\bb\) is replaced by \(\bb+\be\), the component of the minimum-norm solution along \(\bv_i\) changes by \(\bu_i^T\be/\sigma_i\). Directions with small singular values amplify the noise by \(1/\sigma_i\), which is the mechanism behind the ill-conditioning measured by \(\kappa_2(\bA)\). Regularization reduces this amplification by modifying the coefficients for small \(\sigma_i\).






NoteTheorem: Filter Factors for Truncation and Tikhonov Regularization



Let \(\bA\) have rank \(r\) and SVD \(\bA=\sum_{i=1}^r\sigma_i\bu_i\bv_i^T\). For \(k\leq r\), the minimum-norm least-squares solution for the truncated matrix \(\bA_k\) of the referenced result is \[
\begin{align}
\bx_k=\bA_k^\dagger\bb=\sum_{i=1}^k\frac{\bu_i^T\bb}{\sigma_i}\,\bv_i.
\end{align}
\] For \(\lambda&gt;0\), the Tikhonov solution of the referenced result is \[
\begin{align}
\bx_\lambda=\sum_{i=1}^r\frac{\sigma_i^2}{\sigma_i^2+\lambda}\,\frac{\bu_i^T\bb}{\sigma_i}\,\bv_i.
\end{align}
\]








TipProof





For the truncated matrix, only the first \(k\) positive singular triplets are retained. Applying the minimum-norm least-squares formula to that matrix gives \[
\begin{align}
\bx_k=\sum_{i=1}^k\frac{\bu_i^T\bb}{\sigma_i}\bv_i.
\end{align}
\] Relative to the untruncated pseudoinverse, the first \(k\) terms have multiplier one and every discarded term has multiplier zero. These are the truncation filters.
For Tikhonov regularization, let \(\by=\bV^T\bx\) and \(\bc=\bU^T\bb\). Substitute the SVD into the normal equations and multiply by \(\bV^T\) on the left: \[
\begin{align}
(\bsigma^T\bsigma+\lambda\bI)\by=\bsigma^T\bc.
\end{align}
\] This is a diagonal system. For \(i\leq r\), its \(i\)th equation is \((\sigma_i^2+\lambda)y_i=\sigma_ic_i\), so \[
\begin{align}
y_i=\frac{\sigma_i c_i}{\sigma_i^2+\lambda}
=\frac{\sigma_i^2}{\sigma_i^2+\lambda}\frac{c_i}{\sigma_i}.
\end{align}
\] For \(i&gt;r\), the equation is \(\lambda y_i=0\), giving \(y_i=0\). The assumption \(\lambda&gt;0\) makes all diagonal coefficients strictly positive, including those in null-space directions.
Multiplication by \(\bV\) gives the stated filtered sum. The filter \(\sigma_i^2/(\sigma_i^2+\lambda)\) lies strictly between zero and one. It is small for singular values much smaller than \(\sqrt\lambda\), whereas it approaches one when \(\sigma_i^2\) is large relative to \(\lambda\).



Both regularized solutions multiply the unregularized coefficient \(\bu_i^T\bb/\sigma_i\) by a filter factor. Truncation uses the factor one for \(i\leq k\) and zero for \(i&gt;k\), so it removes the weak directions entirely. Tikhonov regularization uses \(\sigma_i^2/(\sigma_i^2+\lambda)\), which is close to one when \(\sigma_i^2\gg\lambda\) and close to \(\sigma_i^2/\lambda\) when \(\sigma_i^2\ll\lambda\). The amplification \(1/\sigma_i\) is therefore replaced by at most \(1/(2\sqrt{\lambda})\), and the transition between kept and damped directions occurs near \(\sigma_i=\sqrt{\lambda}\). The choice between the two methods, and the choice of \(k\) or \(\lambda\), depends on the noise level in \(\bb\) and on the assumption about the solution discussed after the referenced result.






TipRemark



(Computing the SVD) The proof of the referenced result constructs the SVD from the eigendecomposition of \(\bA^T\bA\), but practical codes do not compute it that way. A backward stable eigensolver applied to \(\bA^T\bA\) computes its eigenvalues with absolute errors of order \(u\sigma_1^2\), so singular values below about \(\sqrt{u}\,\sigma_1\) lose all relative accuracy. Golub and Kahan instead reduce \(\bA\) to an upper bidiagonal matrix \(\bB=\bU_0^T\bA\bV_0\) by applying Householder reflections alternately from the left and from the right (Golub and Kahan 1965). A shifted QR iteration applied implicitly to \(\bB\) then computes the singular values, and standard dense libraries follow this two-phase approach. For a large sparse matrix, the bidiagonal matrix can instead be built one column at a time by a recurrence that uses only products with \(\bA\) and \(\bA^T\). The recurrence is closely related to the Lanczos iteration of the referenced section applied to \(\bA^T\bA\).

18.2 The Lagrange Form
The Lagrange formulation circumvents the ill-conditioned Vandermonde linear system by constructing a basis of polynomials tailored to the specific nodes \(x_i\).






NoteDefinition: Lagrange Basis Polynomials



Given \(n+1\) distinct nodes \(x_0, x_1, ..., x_n\), the \(i\)-th Lagrange basis polynomial \(L_i \in \mathcal{P}_n\) is defined by \[
\begin{align}
L_i(x) = \prod_{\substack{j=0 \\ j \neq i}}^n \frac{x - x_j}{x_i - x_j} = \frac{(x - x_0)\cdots(x - x_{i-1})(x - x_{i+1})\cdots(x - x_n)}{(x_i - x_0)\cdots(x_i - x_{i-1})(x_i - x_{i+1})\cdots(x_i - x_n)}.
\end{align}
\] The Lagrange basis polynomials satisfy the cardinal Kronecker delta property: \[
\begin{align}
L_i(x_j) = \delta_{ij} =
    \begin{cases}
        1, & \text{if } i = j, \\
        0, & \text{if } i \neq j.
    \end{cases}
\end{align}
\] The interpolating polynomial in Lagrange form is \[
\begin{align}
P_n(x) = \sum_{i=0}^n y_i L_i(x).
\end{align}
\]








WarningExercise



Verify \(L_i(x_j)=\delta_{ij}\). Use uniqueness of interpolation to prove \(\sum_iL_i(x)=1\) and \(\sum_i x_iL_i(x)=x\) when \(n\geq1\). For the Vandermonde determinant, regard the last row as a polynomial in the last node. Identify its roots and leading coefficient, then complete an induction on the number of nodes.








WarningExercise



For the data \((0,1)\), \((1,3)\), and \((3,2)\), construct the interpolant first by solving the Vandermonde system and then by constructing the three Lagrange polynomials. Verify agreement by comparing coefficients. Once the barycentric formula has been derived below, use it to evaluate the same polynomial at \(x=2\).








NoteExample



To interpolate the three data points \((-1, 1)\), \((0, 0)\), and \((1, 1)\), we construct the degree-2 Lagrange basis polynomials on \(x_0 = -1, x_1 = 0, x_2 = 1\): \[
\begin{align}
L_0(x) &= \frac{(x - 0)(x - 1)}{(-1 - 0)(-1 - 1)} = \frac{x(x - 1)}{2} = \frac{x^2 - x}{2}, \\
    L_1(x) &= \frac{(x - (-1))(x - 1)}{(0 - (-1))(0 - 1)} = \frac{(x + 1)(x - 1)}{-1} = 1 - x^2, \\
    L_2(x) &= \frac{(x - (-1))(x - 0)}{(1 - (-1))(1 - 0)} = \frac{x(x + 1)}{2} = \frac{x^2 + x}{2}.
\end{align}
\] Multiplying by the data values \(y_0 = 1, y_1 = 0, y_2 = 1\) gives \[
\begin{align}
P_2(x) = 1 \cdot \left(\frac{x^2 - x}{2}\right) + 0 \cdot (1 - x^2) + 1 \cdot \left(\frac{x^2 + x}{2}\right) = \frac{x^2 - x + x^2 + x}{2} = x^2.
\end{align}
\]








TipRemark



The standard Lagrange form has two practical shortcomings. First, evaluating \(P_n(x)\) at a single point \(x\) requires \(O(n^2)\) floating-point operations. Second, adding a new node \((x_{n+1}, y_{n+1})\) alters every single basis polynomial \(L_i(x)\), requiring a complete recomputation from scratch.

18.3 The Barycentric Interpolation Formula
The products in the Lagrange basis repeat much of the same work. Factoring out their common nodal polynomial gives a formula that costs \(O(n)\) operations per evaluation after the weights have been computed. Its sensitivity still depends on the nodes.






NoteTheorem: Barycentric Interpolation Formulas



Let \(\ell(x) = \prod_{j=0}^n (x - x_j)\) denote the nodal polynomial. For each node \(x_i\), define the barycentric weights \[
\begin{align}
w_i = \frac{1}{\prod_{j \neq i} (x_i - x_j)} = \frac{1}{\ell'(x_i)}, \qquad i = 0, 1, ..., n.
\end{align}
\] Then the interpolating polynomial satisfies:

First Barycentric Form: \[
\begin{align}
P_n(x) = \ell(x) \sum_{i=0}^n \frac{w_i}{x - x_i} y_i.
\end{align}
\]
Second (True) Barycentric Form: \[
\begin{align}
P_n(x) = \frac{\sum_{i=0}^n \frac{w_i}{x - x_i} y_i}{\sum_{i=0}^n \frac{w_i}{x - x_i}}.
\end{align}
\]

Both displayed formulas use division by \(x-x_i\) and are applied only away from the nodes. If \(x=x_j\), return \(y_j\) directly. The singularities in the formulas are removable, but should not be evaluated as divisions by zero.








TipProof





Assume first that \(x\) is not any of the nodes. The nodal polynomial contains every factor \(x-x_j\). Removing the \(i\)th factor gives \[
\begin{align}
\frac{\ell(x)}{x-x_i}=\prod_{j\neq i}(x-x_j).
\end{align}
\] Multiplication by the weight \(w_i=1/\prod_{j\neq i}(x_i-x_j)\) gives precisely \(L_i(x)\). Substitute into the Lagrange interpolant: \[
\begin{align}
P_n(x)=\sum_i y_iL_i(x)
=\ell(x)\sum_i\frac{w_i y_i}{x-x_i}.
\end{align}
\] This proves the first formula.
Now apply it to data \(y_i=1\) for every \(i\). The constant polynomial \(1\) interpolates those data. By uniqueness it is the interpolant, so \[
\begin{align}
1=\ell(x)\sum_i\frac{w_i}{x-x_i}.
\end{align}
\] Because \(x\) is away from the nodes, \(\ell(x)\neq0\), and the sum in this identity is \(1/\ell(x)\), also nonzero. Divide the first formula by this identity and cancel \(\ell(x)\). The resulting quotient is the second formula.
At \(x=x_j\), each displayed expression contains a division by zero and is not evaluated as written. In the second formula, multiply its numerator and denominator by \(x-x_j\) and let \(x\to x_j\). All other terms vanish in this limit, leaving \(w_jy_j/w_j=y_j\). The weight is nonzero because all nodes are distinct. Thus the singularity is removable and the direct prescription to return \(y_j\) is consistent.









WarningExercise



Interpolate the data \(y_i=1\) to prove \(1=\ell(x)\sum_iw_i/(x-x_i)\). Divide the first formula by this identity to obtain the second. Why does multiplying every weight by the same nonzero constant leave the second formula unchanged? Take the limit as \(x\to x_j\) and verify that it recovers \(y_j\).








TipRemark



The two barycentric formulas have different rounding-error properties. The modified Lagrange (first barycentric) formula admits a backward-error analysis; the second formula has favorable forward-error bounds when the Lebesgue function is modest. Neither representation removes the sensitivity caused by poorly chosen nodes. Furthermore, common multiplicative factors in the weights \(w_i\) cancel out in the numerator and denominator, allowing simple analytical expressions for standard node families (such as Chebyshev nodes).

18.4 The Interpolation Error Formula
Exact agreement at the nodes says nothing by itself about the error between them. To estimate that error, we must assume something about the function beyond its sampled values. The following theorem assumes a bounded derivative of order \(n+1\). Its nodal factor vanishes at each data point, as the error must.






NoteTheorem: Cauchy Remainder Error Theorem



Let \(f \in C^{n+1}[a, b]\), and let \(P_n \in \mathcal{P}_n\) interpolate \(f\) at \(n+1\) distinct nodes \(x_0, x_1, ..., x_n \in [a, b]\). For each point \(x \in [a, b]\), there exists a point \(\xi_x \in (a, b)\) such that \[
\begin{align}
f(x) - P_n(x) = \frac{f^{(n+1)}(\xi_x)}{(n+1)!} \prod_{j=0}^n (x - x_j) = \frac{f^{(n+1)}(\xi_x)}{(n+1)!} \omega_{n+1}(x),
\end{align}
\] where \(\omega_{n+1}(x) = \prod_{j=0}^n (x - x_j)\) is the nodal error polynomial.








TipProof





If \(x = x_i\) for some node \(x_i\), both sides vanish and the formula holds trivially for any \(\xi_x\). This case must be separated before dividing by the nodal polynomial, because that polynomial is zero at every node.
Now fix \(x \in [a, b]\) with \(x \neq x_i\) for all \(i\). Define the constant \[
\begin{align}
\lambda = \frac{f(x) - P_n(x)}{\omega_{n+1}(x)},
\end{align}
\] and consider the auxiliary function \(g : [a, b] \to \fR\) defined by \[
\begin{align}
g(t) = f(t) - P_n(t) - \lambda \omega_{n+1}(t).
\end{align}
\] Because \(f \in C^{n+1}[a, b]\) and polynomials are infinitely differentiable, \(g \in C^{n+1}[a, b]\). We evaluate \(g\) at the \(n+2\) distinct points \(x_0, x_1, ..., x_n\) and \(x\): \[
\begin{align}
g(x_i) &= f(x_i) - P_n(x_i) - \lambda \omega_{n+1}(x_i) = y_i - y_i - 0 = 0, \qquad i = 0, ..., n, \\
    g(x) &= f(x) - P_n(x) - \left( \frac{f(x) - P_n(x)}{\omega_{n+1}(x)} \right) \omega_{n+1}(x) = 0.
\end{align}
\] Thus \(g(t)\) vanishes at \(n+2\) distinct points in \([a, b]\).
The extra zero at the evaluation point is the reason for choosing \(\lambda\) in this way. The interpolation error alone has \(n+1\) known zeros. Subtracting the scaled nodal polynomial creates one additional zero while preserving all the original zeros.
By Rolle’s Theorem, between any two adjacent zeros of \(g(t)\), its derivative \(g'(t)\) has at least one zero. Therefore, \(g'(t)\) has at least \(n+1\) distinct zeros in \((a, b)\). Applying Rolle’s Theorem successively:

\(g''(t)\) has at least \(n\) distinct zeros in \((a, b)\),
\(g'''(t)\) has at least \(n-1\) distinct zeros in \((a, b)\),
Continuing inductively, \(g^{(n+1)}(t)\) has at least one zero in \((a, b)\), which we denote by \(\xi_x\).

We now differentiate \(g(t)\) exactly \(n+1\) times with respect to \(t\): \[
\begin{align}
g^{(n+1)}(t) = f^{(n+1)}(t) - P_n^{(n+1)}(t) - \lambda \frac{d^{n+1}}{dt^{n+1}} [\omega_{n+1}(t)].
\end{align}
\] Since \(P_n\) has degree at most \(n\), its \((n+1)\)-th derivative is identically zero: \(P_n^{(n+1)}(t) \equiv 0\). The nodal polynomial \(\omega_{n+1}(t) = t^{n+1} + ...\) is a monic polynomial of degree \(n+1\), so its \((n+1)\)-th derivative is \((n+1)!\). Evaluating at \(t = \xi_x\) gives \[
\begin{align}
0 = g^{(n+1)}(\xi_x) = f^{(n+1)}(\xi_x) - 0 - \lambda (n+1)!.
\end{align}
\] Solving for \(\lambda\) produces \[
\begin{align}
\lambda = \frac{f^{(n+1)}(\xi_x)}{(n+1)!}.
\end{align}
\] Equating this to our definition of \(\lambda = (f(x) - P_n(x)) / \omega_{n+1}(x)\) completes the proof.
The point supplied by repeated Rolle arguments depends on the chosen evaluation point \(x\). There is no assertion that a single \(\xi\) works for every \(x\) in the interval. The differentiability assumption is used exactly when Rolle’s theorem is repeated through order \(n+1\).

18.6 Piecewise Polynomials: Cubic Splines
A single polynomial makes every coefficient depend on all the data. An alternative is to use a polynomial of low degree on each interval between consecutive nodes. Continuity of derivatives at the joins prevents corners. Cubic splines impose continuity through the second derivative, leaving two additional boundary conditions to specify.






NoteDefinition: Cubic Spline Interpolant



Given \(n+1\) distinct points \(a = x_0 &lt; x_1 &lt; ... &lt; x_n = b\) and values \(y_0, y_1, ..., y_n\), a cubic spline \(S(x)\) is a function such that:

On each subinterval \([x_i, x_{i+1}]\), \(S(x) = S_i(x)\) is a cubic polynomial: \[
\begin{align}
S_i(x) = a_i + b_i(x - x_i) + c_i(x - x_i)^2 + d_i(x - x_i)^3, \qquad i = 0, ..., n-1.
\end{align}
\]
Interpolation conditions: \(S(x_i) = y_i\) for all \(i = 0, ..., n\).
\(C^2\) continuity: \(S(x)\), \(S'(x)\), and \(S''(x)\) are continuous on the entire interval \([a, b]\).









NoteTheorem: The Tridiagonal Spline System



Let \(h_i = x_{i+1} - x_i\) and let \(M_i = S''(x_i)\) denote the second derivative of the spline at node \(x_i\). The condition that \(S'(x)\) is continuous at each interior node \(x_i\) (\(i = 1, ..., n-1\)) leads to the linear system \[
\begin{align}
h_{i-1} M_{i-1} + 2(h_{i-1} + h_i) M_i + h_i M_{i+1} = 6 \left( \frac{y_{i+1} - y_i}{h_i} - \frac{y_i - y_{i-1}}{h_{i-1}} \right).
\end{align}
\] To close the system of \(n-1\) equations in \(n+1\) unknowns, two boundary conditions are imposed:

Natural (Free) Spline: \(M_0 = M_n = 0\).
Clamped (Complete) Spline: \(S'(x_0) = f'(a)\) and \(S'(x_n) = f'(b)\).
Periodic Spline: Require \(y_0=y_n\) and impose \(S'(x_0)=S'(x_n)\) and \(M_0=M_n\).

For natural conditions, eliminate the prescribed endpoint values \(M_0=M_n=0\). The resulting interior system is strictly diagonally dominant and symmetric positive definite. For clamped conditions, the endpoint equations obtained from the prescribed first derivatives give the same properties. It can be solved in \(O(n)\) time using the Thomas algorithm.








TipProof





Since \(S_i(x)\) is a cubic polynomial on \([x_i, x_{i+1}]\), its second derivative \(S_i''(x)\) is a linear function interpolating \(M_i = S''(x_i)\) and \(M_{i+1} = S''(x_{i+1})\): \[
\begin{align}
S_i''(x) = M_i \frac{x_{i+1} - x}{h_i} + M_{i+1} \frac{x - x_i}{h_i}.
\end{align}
\] The formula for the second derivative is the unique line taking values \(M_i\) and \(M_{i+1}\) at the two endpoints. It is therefore convenient to integrate it before imposing the function values.
Integrating twice with respect to \(x\) gives \[
\begin{align}
S_i(x) = \frac{M_i (x_{i+1} - x)^3}{6 h_i} + \frac{M_{i+1} (x - x_i)^3}{6 h_i} + C_i (x_{i+1} - x) + D_i (x - x_i).
\end{align}
\] Applying the interpolation conditions \(S_i(x_i) = y_i\) and \(S_i(x_{i+1}) = y_{i+1}\) determines the constants \(C_i\) and \(D_i\): \[
\begin{align}
C_i = \frac{y_i}{h_i} - \frac{M_i h_i}{6}, \qquad D_i = \frac{y_{i+1}}{h_i} - \frac{M_{i+1} h_i}{6}.
\end{align}
\] Differentiating \(S_i(x)\) to obtain \(S_i'(x)\) and evaluating at the endpoints of \([x_i, x_{i+1}]\) yields \[
\begin{align}
S_i'(x_i) &= -\frac{M_i h_i}{3} - \frac{M_{i+1} h_i}{6} + \frac{y_{i+1} - y_i}{h_i}, \\
    S_{i-1}'(x_i) &= \frac{M_{i-1} h_{i-1}}{6} + \frac{M_i h_{i-1}}{3} + \frac{y_i - y_{i-1}}{h_{i-1}}.
\end{align}
\] Each interior knot has two candidate first derivatives, one from the interval on its left and one from the interval on its right. The function and second derivative already agree there by construction. Only equality of these first derivatives remains to impose.
Equating \(S_{i-1}'(x_i) = S_i'(x_i)\) for continuity of the first derivative and gathering terms produces the stated tridiagonal equation. Strict diagonal dominance follows from \(2(h_{i-1} + h_i) &gt; h_{i-1} + h_i\), which gives nonsingularity. Symmetry, positive diagonal entries, and strict diagonal dominance give positive definiteness, so tridiagonal Cholesky can be used.
For natural boundary conditions, eliminating \(M_0\) and \(M_n\) leaves the interior tridiagonal system. The off-diagonal entry between equations \(i\) and \(i+1\) is \(h_i\) in both positions, proving symmetry. In each row, the positive diagonal \(2(h_{i-1}+h_i)\) exceeds the sum of remaining off-diagonal magnitudes. Every eigenvalue is therefore positive by the symmetric diagonal-dominance bound. This also proves uniqueness of the natural spline coefficients. Clamped endpoint equations arise by setting the displayed endpoint derivative formulas equal to the prescribed slopes.









WarningExercise



Set up the \(2\times2\) system for the natural cubic spline through the data \[
\begin{align}
(0,0),\qquad(1,1),\qquad(2,0),\qquad(3,1).
\end{align}
\] Solve for \(M_1\) and \(M_2\), and write down the explicit cubic polynomial for each subinterval.








NoteTheorem: Minimum Curvature (Energy Minimization) Property



Let \(S(x)\) be the natural cubic spline interpolating \(f(x)\) at \(x_0, x_1, ..., x_n\). Let \(g \in C^2[a, b]\) be any other function satisfying \(g(x_i) = y_i\) for all \(i = 0, ..., n\). Then \[
\begin{align}
\int_a^b [S''(x)]^2\,dx \leq \int_a^b [g''(x)]^2\,dx.
\end{align}
\] Natural cubic splines are the smoothest possible interpolants in the sense of minimizing total bending energy.








TipProof





Let \(h(x) = g(x) - S(x)\). Since both \(g\) and \(S\) interpolate \(f\) at the knots \(x_0, x_1, ..., x_n\), we have \[
\begin{align}
h(x_i) = g(x_i) - S(x_i) = y_i - y_i = 0, \qquad i = 0, 1, ..., n.
\end{align}
\] Expanding the squared second derivative of \(g = S + h\): \[
\begin{align}
\int_a^b [g''(x)]^2\,dx
    &= \int_a^b [S''(x) + h''(x)]^2\,dx \\
    &= \int_a^b [S''(x)]^2\,dx + 2\int_a^b S''(x)h''(x)\,dx + \int_a^b [h''(x)]^2\,dx.
\end{align}
\] If the cross term is zero, the final term is a square integral and can only increase the energy. We therefore calculate that cross term rather than attempting to compare the two energies directly.
We evaluate the cross term by integrating by parts on each subinterval \([x_i, x_{i+1}]\): \[
\begin{align}
\int_{x_i}^{x_{i+1}} S''(x)h''(x)\,dx
    &= \left[ S''(x) h'(x) \right]_{x_i}^{x_{i+1}} - \int_{x_i}^{x_{i+1}} S'''(x) h'(x)\,dx.
\end{align}
\] Because \(S(x)\) is a cubic polynomial on \([x_i, x_{i+1}]\), its third derivative \(S_i'''(x)\) is constant on \((x_i, x_{i+1})\). Let \(S_i'''(x) = c_i\). Then \[
\begin{align}
\int_{x_i}^{x_{i+1}} S'''(x) h'(x)\,dx
    &= c_i \int_{x_i}^{x_{i+1}} h'(x)\,dx
    = c_i [h(x_{i+1}) - h(x_i)] = 0,
\end{align}
\] since \(h(x_{i+1}) = h(x_i) = 0\). Summing the boundary terms over all subintervals: \[
\begin{align}
\int_a^b S''(x)h''(x)\,dx
    &= \sum_{i=0}^{n-1} \left( S''(x_{i+1}^-) h'(x_{i+1}) - S''(x_i^+) h'(x_i) \right) \\
    &= S''(b) h'(b) - S''(a) h'(a) + \sum_{i=1}^{n-1} \left( S''(x_i^-) - S''(x_i^+) \right) h'(x_i).
\end{align}
\] At each interior knot, the contribution from the interval on the left has a plus sign and the contribution from the interval on the right a minus sign. They involve the same \(h'(x_i)\) because \(h\) is continuously differentiable. This explains the cancellation structure in the sum.
Because \(S \in C^2[a, b]\), the second derivative is continuous across interior knots: \(S''(x_i^-) - S''(x_i^+) = 0\). For a natural cubic spline, \(S''(a) = S''(b) = 0\), so the boundary terms vanish as well. Therefore \[
\begin{align}
\int_a^b S''(x)h''(x)\,dx = 0.
\end{align}
\] Substituting this back yields \[
\begin{align}
\int_a^b [g''(x)]^2\,dx
    &= \int_a^b [S''(x)]^2\,dx + \int_a^b [h''(x)]^2\,dx
    \geq \int_a^b [S''(x)]^2\,dx,
\end{align}
\] with equality if and only if \(h''(x) \equiv 0\), meaning \(h\) is linear and hence identically zero because \(h(x_0) = h(x_n) = 0\).
If the remaining integral of \((h'')^2\) is zero, continuity of \(h''\) forces it to vanish at every point: a nonzero value would give a neighborhood with a positive contribution. Then \(h\) is linear. Its zero values at the two distinct endpoints force both linear coefficients to be zero. Thus equality holds only for the spline itself.

19.2 Chebyshev Polynomials and Fundamental Identities






NoteDefinition: Chebyshev Polynomials of the First Kind



For \(x \in [-1, 1]\), the Chebyshev polynomial of degree \(k\) is defined by \[
\begin{align}
T_k(x) = \cos(k \arccos x).
\end{align}
\] Under the substitution \(x = \cos\theta\) with \(\theta \in [0, \pi]\), \[
\begin{align}
T_k(\cos\theta) = \cos(k\theta).
\end{align}
\]








NoteLemma: Chebyshev Three-Term Recurrence



The functions \(T_k(x)\) are algebraic polynomials satisfying the three-term recurrence relation \[
\begin{align}
T_0(x) = 1, \qquad T_1(x) = x, \qquad T_{k+1}(x) = 2x T_k(x) - T_{k-1}(x) \quad \text{for } k \geq 1.
\end{align}
\] Furthermore, for every \(k \geq 1\), the leading coefficient of \(T_k(x)\) is \(2^{k-1}\).








TipProof





The cosine addition formulas give \[
\begin{align}
\cos((k+1)\theta)&=\cos(k\theta)\cos\theta-\sin(k\theta)\sin\theta,\\
\cos((k-1)\theta)&=\cos(k\theta)\cos\theta+\sin(k\theta)\sin\theta.
\end{align}
\] Adding cancels the sine terms. Substitute \(x=\cos\theta\) and \(T_j(x)=\cos(j\theta)\) to obtain \[
\begin{align}
T_{k+1}(x)+T_{k-1}(x)=2xT_k(x).
\end{align}
\] Also \(T_0(x)=1\) and \(T_1(x)=x\). Hence the recurrence constructs polynomials from polynomial starting values, although the original definition used trigonometric functions.
For the leading coefficient, first compute \(T_2=2x^2-1\), whose leading coefficient is \(2\). The formula is also true for \(T_1\), with leading coefficient \(1=2^0\). Suppose \(T_k\) has degree \(k\) and leading coefficient \(2^{k-1}\). The product \(2xT_k\) has degree \(k+1\) and leading coefficient \(2^k\). Subtracting \(T_{k-1}\), of smaller degree, cannot cancel that leading term. Thus \(T_{k+1}\) has the claimed degree and coefficient. This completes the induction.









WarningExercise



Compute \(T_2,T_3,T_4\) from the recurrence. Prove by induction that \(T_k\) has degree \(k\) and leading coefficient \(2^{k-1}\) for \(k\geq1\). Which term in the recurrence determines the leading coefficient? After proving the minimax theorem below, map \([a,b]\) to \([-1,1]\) and show that the monic minimax polynomial of degree \(n\geq1\) on \([a,b]\) has uniform norm \((b-a)^n/2^{2n-1}\).








NoteTheorem: Orthogonality of Chebyshev Polynomials



Chebyshev polynomials are orthogonal on \([-1, 1]\) with respect to the singular weight function \(w(x) = (1 - x^2)^{-1/2}\): \[
\begin{align}
\int_{-1}^1 T_j(x) T_k(x) \frac{dx}{\sqrt{1 - x^2}}
    =
    \begin{cases}
        0, & j \neq k, \\
        \pi, & j = k = 0, \\
        \frac{\pi}{2}, & j = k \geq 1.
    \end{cases}
\end{align}
\]








TipProof





Use \(x=\cos\theta\) with \(0\leq\theta\leq\pi\). Then \(dx=-\sin\theta\,d\theta\) and \(\sqrt{1-x^2}=\sin\theta\) on the interior. Reversing the endpoints cancels the minus sign, giving \[
\begin{align}
\int_{-1}^1\frac{T_j(x)T_k(x)}{\sqrt{1-x^2}}\,dx
=\int_0^\pi\cos(j\theta)\cos(k\theta)\,d\theta.
\end{align}
\] The original endpoint singularities are integrable under this substitution.
If \(j\neq k\), use the product identity \[
\begin{align}
\cos(j\theta)\cos(k\theta)
=\tfrac12\cos((j-k)\theta)+\tfrac12\cos((j+k)\theta).
\end{align}
\] Both frequencies are nonzero integers, including when one of \(j,k\) is zero. For any nonzero integer \(l\), \[
\begin{align}
\int_0^\pi\cos(l\theta)\,d\theta
=[\sin(l\theta)/l]_0^\pi=0.
\end{align}
\] Thus the cross integral vanishes.
If \(j=k=0\), the integrand is one and its integral is \(\pi\). If \(j=k\geq1\), use \(\cos^2(k\theta)=(1+\cos(2k\theta))/2\). The constant term integrates to \(\pi/2\) and the oscillating term to zero. These three cases give every value in the statement.

19.3 Roots, Extrema, and the Nodal Error Polynomial






NoteTheorem: Zeros and Extreme Points of \(T_n\)



For each \(n \geq 1\), the polynomial \(T_n(x)\) has:

Exactly \(n\) distinct real zeros in \((-1, 1)\), given by \[
\begin{align}
x_j = \cos\left( \frac{2j - 1}{2n} \pi \right), \qquad j = 1, ..., n.
\end{align}
\]
Exactly \(n+1\) alternating extreme points on \([-1, 1]\), given by \[
\begin{align}
y_j = \cos\left( \frac{j\pi}{n} \right), \qquad j = 0, ..., n,
\end{align}
\] at which \(T_n(y_j) = (-1)^j\).









TipProof





On \([-1,1]\), the substitution \(x=\cos\theta\) with \(\theta\in[0,\pi]\) is one-to-one. Thus we can find all roots by solving \(\cos(n\theta)=0\) on that angle interval.
The cosine is zero at \(n\theta=(j-1/2)\pi\). The choices in \([0,n\pi]\) are exactly \(j=1,...,n\). Therefore \[
\begin{align}
\theta_j=\frac{2j-1}{2n}\pi,
\qquad x_j=\cos\theta_j.
\end{align}
\] All angles are strictly between zero and \(\pi\), and distinct angles give distinct points because cosine is strictly decreasing there. These are \(n\) distinct roots of a degree-\(n\) polynomial, so there are no additional roots.
For extreme values, \(|T_n(x)|=|\cos(n\theta)|\leq1\). Equality occurs exactly when \(n\theta=j\pi\) for \(j=0,...,n\). The corresponding points are \(y_j=\cos(j\pi/n)\), and \[
\begin{align}
T_n(y_j)=\cos(j\pi)=(-1)^j.
\end{align}
\] They are therefore alternating points attaining the global upper and lower values, including the two endpoints. In the interior, differentiating gives \(T_n'(\cos\theta)=n\sin(n\theta)/\sin\theta\), so no other interior extremum is possible.









WarningExercise



List all angles in \([0,\pi]\) that satisfy each condition in the proof. Apply \(x=\cos\theta\) and show that the resulting nodes are distinct. Why are there no additional zeros? Sketch \(T_3\) and mark both its zeros and its alternating extreme values.








NoteDefinition: Chebyshev Nodes



The \(n\) Chebyshev nodes are the roots of \(T_n(x)\). They interpolate polynomials of degree at most \(n-1\): \[
\begin{align}
x_j = \cos\left( \frac{2j - 1}{2n} \pi \right), \qquad j = 1, ..., n.
\end{align}
\] These nodes cluster quadratically near the interval endpoints \(\pm 1\), with spacing magnitude \(|x_{j+1}-x_j|=O(n^{-2})\) near the boundaries and \(O(1/n)\) in the interior.








NoteTheorem: Representation of the Chebyshev Nodal Polynomial



Let \(\{x_j\}_{j=1}^n\) be the Chebyshev nodes from the referenced result. The monic nodal polynomial satisfies \[
\begin{align}
\omega_n(x) = \prod_{j=1}^n (x - x_j) = 2^{1-n} T_n(x).
\end{align}
\] Consequently, the uniform norm of the nodal polynomial is \[
\begin{align}
\|\omega_n\|_\infty = 2^{1-n} \|T_n\|_\infty = 2^{1-n}.
\end{align}
\]








TipProof





Both \(\omega_n(x)=\prod_{j=1}^n(x-x_j)\) and \(T_n(x)\) have the same \(n\) distinct zeros. Repeated use of the factor theorem makes \(T_n\) divisible by this product. Since both degrees are \(n\), the quotient is a nonzero constant, say \(C\).
The leading coefficient of the product \(\omega_n\) is one: choosing \(x\) from every factor gives \(x^n\). The leading coefficient of \(T_n\) is \(2^{n-1}\) by the recurrence theorem. Comparing the coefficients of \(x^n\) in \(T_n=C\omega_n\) gives \(C=2^{n-1}\). Hence \[
\begin{align}
\omega_n(x)=2^{1-n}T_n(x).
\end{align}
\] For every \(x\in[-1,1]\), write \(x=\cos\theta\). Then \(|T_n(x)|=|\cos(n\theta)|\leq1\). At \(x=1\), its value is exactly one. Thus \(\|T_n\|_\infty=1\), and homogeneity of the uniform norm gives \[
\begin{align}
\|\omega_n\|_\infty=2^{1-n}\|T_n\|_\infty=2^{1-n}.
\end{align}
\] This proves both the polynomial identity and its precise norm.

19.5 Chebyshev Series and Spectral Accuracy






NoteDefinition: Chebyshev Series Expansion



A function with a convergent Chebyshev expansion is written using the primed-sum convention \[
\begin{align}
f(x) = \sideset{}{'}\sum_{k=0}^\infty c_k T_k(x),
\end{align}
\] where the prime on the summation indicates that the initial term \(k = 0\) carries a factor of \(1/2\): \[
\begin{align}
c_k = \frac{2}{\pi} \int_{-1}^1 f(x) T_k(x) \frac{dx}{\sqrt{1 - x^2}} = \frac{2}{\pi} \int_0^\pi f(\cos\theta) \cos(k\theta)\, d\theta.
\end{align}
\]








NoteTheorem: Geometric Convergence for Analytic Functions



Let \(f\) be analytic on and inside a Bernstein ellipse whose boundary is \[
\begin{align}
\mathcal{E}_\rho = \left\{ z \in \fC : z = \frac{w + w^{-1}}{2}, \; |w| = \rho \right\}
\end{align}
\] with \(\rho&gt;1\), semi-axes \(a = \frac{\rho + \rho^{-1}}{2}\) and \(b = \frac{\rho - \rho^{-1}}{2}\). Suppose \(|f(z)|\leq M\) on this boundary. Then the Chebyshev coefficients satisfy \[
\begin{align}
|c_k| \leq \frac{2M}{\rho^k} \qquad \text{for all } k \geq 0.
\end{align}
\] Consequently, the truncated polynomial approximation \(p_n(x) = \sideset{}{'}\sum_{k=0}^n c_k T_k(x)\) converges geometrically: \[
\begin{align}
\|f - p_n\|_\infty \leq \frac{2M}{\rho - 1} \rho^{-n}.
\end{align}
\]








TipProof





Under the conformal map \(z = \frac{w + w^{-1}}{2}\), the circle \(|w| = r\) for \(1 &lt; r &lt; \rho\) maps to the ellipse \(\mathcal{E}_r\) in the complex \(z\)-plane. The transformed function \(F(w) = f\left(\frac{w + w^{-1}}{2}\right)\) is analytic in the annulus \(r^{-1} &lt; |w| &lt; r\) and satisfies \(F(w) = F(w^{-1})\).
Here an annulus is the region between two circles. A Laurent series is a power series allowing negative as well as nonnegative integer powers, which is the appropriate expansion on such a region. The symmetry under \(w\mapsto w^{-1}\) pairs its positive and negative coefficients.
Expanding \(F(w)\) in its Laurent series \(F(w) = \sum_{k=-\infty}^\infty a_k w^k\), the symmetry \(F(w) = F(w^{-1})\) implies \(a_k = a_{-k}\). On the unit circle \(w = e^{i\theta}\), \(F(e^{i\theta}) = f(\cos\theta) = a_0 + 2\sum_{k=1}^\infty a_k \cos(k\theta)\), so the Chebyshev coefficients are related to the Laurent coefficients by \(c_k = 2 a_k\) for \(k \geq 1\). By Cauchy’s Integral Formula, for any \(1 &lt; r &lt; \rho\), \[
\begin{align}
a_k = \frac{1}{2\pi i} \oint_{|w|=r} \frac{F(w)}{w^{k+1}}\, dw.
\end{align}
\] On the contour \(|w|=r\), the factor \(|w^{-(k+1)}|\) equals \(r^{-(k+1)}\) and the contour length is \(2\pi r\). Consequently the absolute value of the integral is bounded by \((2\pi)^{-1}(2\pi r)Mr^{-(k+1)}=Mr^{-k}\). The bound \(M\) also holds on the smaller ellipses by the maximum modulus principle.
Taking absolute values and using \(|F(w)| \leq M\) gives \(|a_k| \leq M r^{-k}\). Taking the infimum over \(r \to \rho^-\) yields \(|c_k| \leq 2M \rho^{-k}\). Summing the tail of the series gives the uniform error bound: \[
\begin{align}
\|f - p_n\|_\infty \leq \sum_{k=n+1}^\infty |c_k| \leq 2M \sum_{k=n+1}^\infty \rho^{-k} = \frac{2M \rho^{-n}}{\rho - 1}.
\end{align}
\]
For the primed constant term, \(c_0/2=a_0\), so \(|c_0|\leq2M\) as well. In the tail estimate, \(|T_k(x)|\leq1\) for real \(x\in[-1,1]\). Thus the coefficient tail dominates the uniform function error. This connects the complex coefficient estimate to approximation on the real interval (Trefethen 2013).

20.2 Newton–Cotes Formulas and Composite Rules
Newton–Cotes rules are interpolatory quadrature formulas built on equally spaced nodes. Because high-order single-panel Newton–Cotes rules suffer from negative weights and Runge’s phenomenon, practical computations use composite rules on small subintervals of uniform width \(h = (b - a)/N\).






NoteDefinition: Composite Trapezoidal and Simpson’s Rules



Dividing \([a, b]\) into \(N\) subintervals with uniform width \(h = (b - a)/N\) and grid points \(x_j = a + jh\):

The Composite Trapezoidal Rule is \[
\begin{align}
T_N(f) = h \left[ \frac{f(a) + f(b)}{2} + \sum_{j=1}^{N-1} f(x_j) \right].
\end{align}
\]
For an even number of panels \(N\), the Composite Simpson’s Rule is \[
\begin{align}
S_N(f) = \frac{h}{3} \left[ f(x_0) + 4\sum_{\substack{j=1 \\ j \text{ odd}}}^{N-1} f(x_j) + 2\sum_{\substack{j=2 \\ j \text{ even}}}^{N-2} f(x_j) + f(x_N) \right].
\end{align}
\]









NoteTheorem: Composite Error Formulas



Let \(f \in C^2[a, b]\) for the trapezoidal rule, and let \(f \in C^4[a, b]\) for Simpson’s rule.

The composite trapezoidal error satisfies \[
\begin{align}
I(f) - T_N(f) = -\frac{b - a}{12} h^2 f''(\xi_T), \qquad \xi_T \in (a, b).
\end{align}
\]
The composite Simpson error satisfies \[
\begin{align}
I(f) - S_N(f) = -\frac{b - a}{180} h^4 f^{(4)}(\xi_S), \qquad \xi_S \in (a, b).
\end{align}
\]









TipProof





For the trapezoidal rule on panel \([x_j, x_{j+1}]\), the Cauchy interpolation error formula (the referenced result) gives \[
\begin{align}
f(x) - P_1(x) = \frac{f''(\xi_j(x))}{2}(x - x_j)(x - x_{j+1}).
\end{align}
\] Because \((x - x_j)(x - x_{j+1}) \leq 0\) on this panel, the Mean Value Theorem for Integrals implies there exists \(\mu_j \in (x_j, x_{j+1})\) such that \[
\begin{align}
\int_{x_j}^{x_{j+1}} [f(x) - P_1(x)]\,dx = \frac{f''(\mu_j)}{2} \int_{x_j}^{x_{j+1}} (x - x_j)(x - x_{j+1})\,dx = -\frac{h^3}{12} f''(\mu_j).
\end{align}
\] Summing across all \(N\) panels and applying the Intermediate Value Theorem to the average \(\frac{1}{N}\sum_{j=0}^{N-1} f''(\mu_j) = f''(\xi_T)\) yields the trapezoidal error formula: \[
\begin{align}
I(f) - T_N(f) = -\frac{h^3}{12} \sum_{j=0}^{N-1} f''(\mu_j) = -\frac{N h^3}{12} f''(\xi_T) = -\frac{b - a}{12} h^2 f''(\xi_T).
\end{align}
\] The intermediate mean-value expression for the trapezoidal rule can be justified without assuming that a choice of \(\xi_j(x)\) is continuous: the minimum and maximum of \(f''\) bound the pointwise interpolation error multiplied by its fixed-sign nodal factor. Integrating these bounds puts the scaled panel error between the minimum and maximum values of \(f''\). Continuity then supplies the stated \(\mu_j\).
For Simpson’s rule use the integral form of Taylor’s theorem, which requires only \(f\in C^4\). On the reference interval \([-1,1]\), the error functional annihilates cubics. Its fourth-derivative kernel is \[
\begin{align}
K(t)&=\frac{1}{6}E_x((x-t)_+^3)\\
&=\frac{(1-t)^4}{24}-\frac{2}{9}(-t)_+^3-\frac{(1-t)^3}{18}\\
&=-\frac{(1-|t|)^3(1+3|t|)}{72},\qquad -1\leq t\leq1.
\end{align}
\] The notation \(E_x\) means that the quadrature-error operator acts on the variable \(x\), with \(t\) held fixed. The factor \(1/6\) comes from the third power in Taylor’s integral remainder. On \([-1,1]\), integrating \((x-t)_+^3/6\) gives \((1-t)^4/24\). Evaluating it at the Simpson nodes gives exactly the two nonzero sampled terms displayed above.
Thus \(K\leq0\) and \(\int_{-1}^1K(t)\,dt=-1/90\). Taylor’s integral remainder gives \(E(f)=\int_{-1}^1K(t)f^{(4)}(t)\,dt\); the next theorem proves this general kernel representation. A weighted mean-value argument and scaling \(x=c_j+ht\) give the double-panel error \(-h^5 f^{(4)}(\mu_j)/90\). Summing the \(N/2\) double panels and using continuity of \(f^{(4)}\) proves the composite formula.
On a double panel, \(x=c_j+ht\) has Jacobian \(h\) and its fourth derivative with respect to \(t\) is \(h^4 f^{(4)}(c_j+ht)\). These give the combined factor \(h^5\). There are \(N/2\) such panels, so \(-(N/2)h^5/90=-(b-a)h^4/180\), which is the composite constant.









WarningExercise



Verify the Simpson kernel formula separately for \(t\geq0\) and \(t\leq0\), and integrate it. Apply the single-panel formulas to \(f(x)=x^2\) for the trapezoidal rule and \(f(x)=x^4\) for Simpson’s rule. Check both the sign and the power of \(h\).

20.4 Gauss–Legendre Quadrature
Newton–Cotes rules fix equispaced nodes and optimize only the weights. Gaussian quadrature optimizes both the nodes \(x_1, ..., x_p\) and the weights \(w_1, ..., w_p\), providing \(2p\) degrees of freedom to maximize the degree of exactness.
We need polynomials orthogonal for the ordinary integral on \([-1,1]\), rather than the Chebyshev weight. Applying Gram–Schmidt to \(1,x,x^2,...\) with inner product \(\int_{-1}^1p(x)q(x)\,dx\) constructs them. Up to normalization these are the Legendre polynomials. Orthogonality will make part of the quadrature error vanish without adding more nodes.






WarningExercise



Construct the first three polynomials in this process. You may leave their norms arbitrary. Show that the polynomial of degree two is proportional to \(x^2-1/3\). Use its roots and exactness for \(1\) and \(x\) to derive the two-point Gaussian rule.








NoteLemma: Roots of Orthogonal Polynomials



Let \(P_p(x)\) be the degree-\(p\) Legendre polynomial orthogonal on \([-1, 1]\) with respect to weight \(w(x) \equiv 1\): \[
\begin{align}
\int_{-1}^1 P_p(x) q(x)\,dx = 0 \qquad \text{for all } q \in \mathcal{P}_{p-1}.
\end{align}
\] Then \(P_p(x)\) has exactly \(p\) distinct, real roots, and all roots lie in the open interval \((-1, 1)\).








TipProof





Let \(x_1, ..., x_k\) denote the points in \((-1, 1)\) where \(P_p(x)\) changes sign. Because \(P_p\) is continuous and orthogonal to the constant polynomial \(q(x) = 1\), we have \(\int_{-1}^1 P_p(x)\,dx = 0\), so \(P_p\) must change sign at least once in \((-1, 1)\), meaning \(k \geq 1\). We count sign-changing roots rather than all roots. A root of even multiplicity does not reverse the sign, whereas a root of odd multiplicity does. The polynomial is nonzero, so it cannot vanish on an interval.
Suppose for contradiction that \(k &lt; p\). Construct the polynomial \(q(x) = \prod_{j=1}^k (x - x_j) \in \mathcal{P}_k\). By construction, \(P_p(x)\) and \(q(x)\) change sign at identical points in \((-1, 1)\), so their product \(P_p(x) q(x)\) has constant sign on \([-1, 1]\) and is not identically zero. Consequently, \[
\begin{align}
\int_{-1}^1 P_p(x) q(x)\,dx \neq 0.
\end{align}
\] Across each listed point, both factors change sign and their product does not. At every other root, \(P_p\) has even multiplicity and again the product does not change sign. Away from finitely many roots the product is nonzero. Its integral over the interval therefore cannot vanish.
However, because \(\deg(q) = k &lt; p\), orthogonality requires \(\int_{-1}^1 P_p(x) q(x)\,dx = 0\), producing a contradiction. Therefore, \(k = p\), proving that \(P_p\) has \(p\) distinct real roots in \((-1, 1)\).
A degree-\(p\) polynomial cannot have more than \(p\) distinct roots. The argument has found \(p\) distinct sign-changing roots inside the interval, exhausting the degree. Thus they are simple and there are no other roots, real or complex.









NoteTheorem: Fundamental Theorem of Gaussian Quadrature



Let \(x_1, ..., x_p\) be the roots of the degree-\(p\) Legendre polynomial \(P_p(x)\) on \([-1, 1]\), and let \(w_i = \int_{-1}^1 L_i(x)\,dx\). The \(p\)-point Gauss–Legendre quadrature rule \[
\begin{align}
\int_{-1}^1 f(x)\,dx \approx \sum_{i=1}^p w_i f(x_i)
\end{align}
\] has degree of exactness \(2p - 1\). No \(p\)-point quadrature rule can achieve a degree of exactness higher than \(2p - 1\). Furthermore, all weights are strictly positive: \(w_i &gt; 0\) for all \(i = 1, ..., p\).








TipProof





Let \(f \in \mathcal{P}_{2p-1}\). Polynomial long division of \(f(x)\) by \(P_p(x)\) yields \[
\begin{align}
f(x) = q(x) P_p(x) + r(x), \qquad \deg(q) \leq p - 1, \quad \deg(r) \leq p - 1.
\end{align}
\] Integrating both sides over \([-1, 1]\): \[
\begin{align}
\int_{-1}^1 f(x)\,dx = \int_{-1}^1 q(x) P_p(x)\,dx + \int_{-1}^1 r(x)\,dx.
\end{align}
\] Because \(\deg(q) \leq p - 1\), orthogonality of \(P_p\) against \(\mathcal{P}_{p-1}\) implies \(\int_{-1}^1 q(x) P_p(x)\,dx = 0\), giving \[
\begin{align}
\int_{-1}^1 f(x)\,dx = \int_{-1}^1 r(x)\,dx.
\end{align}
\] Applying the \(p\)-point quadrature sum at the Legendre roots \(\{x_i\}_{i=1}^p\) where \(P_p(x_i) = 0\): \[
\begin{align}
\sum_{i=1}^p w_i f(x_i) &= \sum_{i=1}^p w_i \left[ q(x_i) P_p(x_i) + r(x_i) \right] \\
    &= \sum_{i=1}^p w_i \left[ 0 + r(x_i) \right] = \sum_{i=1}^p w_i r(x_i).
\end{align}
\] Because \(\deg(r) \leq p - 1\), interpolatory quadrature on \(p\) distinct nodes integrates \(r(x)\) exactly by the referenced result: \[
\begin{align}
\sum_{i=1}^p w_i r(x_i) = \int_{-1}^1 r(x)\,dx = \int_{-1}^1 f(x)\,dx.
\end{align}
\] Thus \(\sum_{i=1}^p w_i f(x_i) = \int_{-1}^1 f(x)\,dx\) for all \(f \in \mathcal{P}_{2p-1}\). Both the exact integral and the quadrature value have now been reduced to the same remainder polynomial \(r\) of degree at most \(p-1\). This proves exactness through degree \(2p-1\), rather than merely through the interpolation degree.
To prove that \(2p - 1\) is the maximal degree, define the nodal polynomial \[
\begin{align}
g(x) = \prod_{i=1}^p (x - x_i)^2 \in \mathcal{P}_{2p}.
\end{align}
\] Because \(g(x) \geq 0\) and vanishes only at isolated points, its integral is strictly positive: \[
\begin{align}
\int_{-1}^1 g(x)\,dx &gt; 0.
\end{align}
\] However, evaluating the quadrature sum yields \[
\begin{align}
\sum_{i=1}^p w_i g(x_i) = \sum_{i=1}^p w_i \cdot 0 = 0 \neq \int_{-1}^1 g(x)\,dx.
\end{align}
\] Therefore no \(p\)-point quadrature rule can integrate all degree-\(2p\) polynomials. This obstruction applies to any proposed \(p\) nodes, regardless of their weights. The squared nodal polynomial is tailored to those nodes, has a positive integral, and is invisible to the quadrature rule.
For weight positivity, let \(L_i \in \mathcal{P}_{p-1}\) be the \(i\)-th Lagrange basis polynomial. The square satisfies \(\deg(L_i^2) = 2p - 2 \leq 2p - 1\). The rule integrates \(L_i^2\) exactly: \[
\begin{align}
0 &lt; \int_{-1}^1 L_i^2(x)\,dx = \sum_{j=1}^p w_j L_i^2(x_j) = \sum_{j=1}^p w_j \delta_{ij} = w_i.
\end{align}
\] Every weight \(w_i\) is strictly positive.
The positivity integral is strict because \(L_i(x_i)=1\), so \(L_i\) is not the zero polynomial and its square is positive on a neighborhood. The equality with the single weight uses \(L_i(x_j)^2=\delta_{ij}\). Gaussian quadrature and its error arguments are developed in Stoer and Bulirsch (2002).

20.5 Richardson Extrapolation and Romberg Integration
When the error of a numerical method admits an asymptotic expansion in powers of \(h\), Richardson extrapolation algebraically cancels the leading error terms.






NoteTheorem: Euler–Maclaurin Summation and Romberg Integration



Let \(f \in C^{2m+2}[a, b]\). The composite trapezoidal rule satisfies the asymptotic expansion \[
\begin{align}
T(h) = I(f) + c_2 h^2 + c_4 h^4 + c_6 h^6 + ... + c_{2m} h^{2m} + O(h^{2m+2}),
\end{align}
\] where \(c_{2k} = \frac{B_{2k}}{(2k)!}[f^{(2k-1)}(b) - f^{(2k-1)}(a)]\) depend on the Bernoulli numbers \(B_{2k}\). Romberg integration systematically eliminates powers of \(h^2\) via the tableau recurrence \[
\begin{align}
R(k, 0) = T(h_k), \qquad R(k, j) = \frac{4^j R(k, j-1) - R(k-1, j-1)}{4^j - 1} \quad (1 \leq j \leq k),
\end{align}
\] where \(h_k = (b - a)/2^k\). Column \(j\) achieves order \(O(h^{2j+2})\).








TipProof





Consider two consecutive grid widths, \(h_k\) and \(h_{k-1}=2h_k\). The first trapezoidal expansion gives \[
\begin{align}
R(k,0)&=I+c_2h_k^2+c_4h_k^4+O(h_k^6),\\
R(k-1,0)&=I+4c_2h_k^2+16c_4h_k^4+O(h_k^6).
\end{align}
\] Multiply the first by four and subtract the second. The \(h_k^2\) terms cancel, while the constant term becomes \(3I\). Dividing by three therefore gives \[
\begin{align}
R(k,1)=\frac{4R(k,0)-R(k-1,0)}3=I+O(h_k^4).
\end{align}
\] This proves the first extrapolation step, including the reason for its two weights.
For the general step, suppose column \(j-1\) has an expansion whose first possible error term has order \(2j\): \[
\begin{align}
R(k,j-1)=I+d_{2j}h_k^{2j}+O(h_k^{2j+2}).
\end{align}
\] The coefficients here are the same when the entire construction is carried out with width \(2h_k\), because the expansion coefficients are independent of the width. Hence \[
\begin{align}
R(k-1,j-1)=I+2^{2j}d_{2j}h_k^{2j}+O(h_k^{2j+2}).
\end{align}
\] Since \(2^{2j}=4^j\), the combination in the stated recurrence cancels this leading term and divides its constant term by \(4^j-1\). Thus \(R(k,j)=I+O(h_k^{2j+2})\). Induction proves the order of each fixed column for which the assumed smoothness supplies the required expansion. The starting Euler–Maclaurin expansion itself is a standard result of quadrature theory (Stoer and Bulirsch 2002).

20.6 Periodic Functions and Spectral Convergence






NoteTheorem: Spectral Accuracy of the Trapezoidal Rule on Periodic Domains



Let \(f\) be a smooth, \(2\pi\)-periodic function on \(\fR\). All endpoint derivatives in the Euler–Maclaurin expansion cancel identically: \[
\begin{align}
f^{(2k-1)}(2\pi) - f^{(2k-1)}(0) = 0 \qquad \text{for all } k \geq 1.
\end{align}
\] Consequently, all algebraic error coefficients \(c_2, c_4, c_6, ...\) vanish. If \(f\) is analytic and bounded in a strip \(\{z \in \fC : |\operatorname{Im}(z)| &lt; d\}\), the composite trapezoidal rule converges exponentially fast: \[
\begin{align}
|I(f) - T_N(f)| \leq C e^{-d N}.
\end{align}
\] On smooth periodic domains, the composite trapezoidal rule achieves spectral accuracy.








TipProof





Because \(f\) is periodic and smooth, \(f^{(m)}(2\pi) = f^{(m)}(0)\) for all \(m \geq 0\). Every term \(c_{2k}\) in the Euler–Maclaurin formula is proportional to \(f^{(2k-1)}(2\pi) - f^{(2k-1)}(0) = 0\), so the error decays faster than any power of \(h = 2\pi / N\). For any chosen algebraic power, apply Euler–Maclaurin with enough derivatives. Its endpoint terms vanish and only a remainder of higher order remains. This is the meaning of faster-than-algebraic convergence for a smooth periodic function. Exponential convergence requires the additional analyticity assumption.
To quantify the exponential rate, expand \(f(x)\) in its Fourier series: \[
\begin{align}
f(x) = \sum_{k=-\infty}^\infty \hat{f}_k e^{ikx}, \qquad \hat{f}_k = \frac{1}{2\pi} \int_0^{2\pi} f(x) e^{-ikx}\,dx.
\end{align}
\] The exact integral is \(I(f) = \int_0^{2\pi} f(x)\,dx = 2\pi \hat{f}_0\). The composite trapezoidal rule with \(h = 2\pi / N\) and nodes \(x_j = 2\pi j / N\) for \(j = 0, ..., N - 1\) gives \[
\begin{align}
T_N(f) = \frac{2\pi}{N} \sum_{j=0}^{N-1} f(x_j) &= \frac{2\pi}{N} \sum_{j=0}^{N-1} \sum_{k=-\infty}^\infty \hat{f}_k e^{2\pi i j k / N} \\
    &= 2\pi \sum_{k=-\infty}^\infty \hat{f}_k \left( \frac{1}{N} \sum_{j=0}^{N-1} e^{2\pi i j k / N} \right).
\end{align}
\] The inner sum satisfies discrete orthogonality: \[
\begin{align}
\frac{1}{N} \sum_{j=0}^{N-1} e^{2\pi i j k / N} = \begin{cases}
        1, & k = mN \text{ for some } m \in \fZ, \\
        0, & k \not\equiv 0 \pmod N.
    \end{cases}
\end{align}
\] For a frequency not divisible by \(N\), the inner sum is a geometric sum with ratio \(r=e^{2\pi i k/N}\neq1\) and \(r^N=1\). Its value is \((1-r^N)/(1-r)=0\). If \(k\) is divisible by \(N\), every term is one and the normalized sum is one.
Substituting this indicator isolates the aliased modes: \[
\begin{align}
T_N(f) = 2\pi \sum_{m=-\infty}^\infty \hat{f}_{mN} = 2\pi \hat{f}_0 + 2\pi \sum_{m \neq 0} \hat{f}_{mN}.
\end{align}
\] The quadrature error is therefore the tail sum: \[
\begin{align}
I(f) - T_N(f) = -2\pi \sum_{m \neq 0} \hat{f}_{mN}.
\end{align}
\] Because \(f\) is analytic and bounded by \(M_0\) in the strip \(|\operatorname{Im}(z)| &lt; d\), shifting the integration path in the definition of \(\hat{f}_k\) gives the geometric decay \(|\hat{f}_k| \leq M e^{-d|k|}\). Applying this bound: \[
\begin{align}
|I(f) - T_N(f)| \leq 2\pi M \sum_{m \neq 0} e^{-d|m|N} = 4\pi M \sum_{m=1}^\infty (e^{-dN})^m = 4\pi M \frac{e^{-dN}}{1 - e^{-dN}} = O(e^{-dN}).
\end{align}
\]
Because \(d&gt;0\) and \(N\geq1\), \(1-e^{-dN}\geq1-e^{-d}&gt;0\). Hence the displayed fraction is bounded by a fixed constant times \(e^{-dN}\). This supplies a constant independent of \(N\) for the claimed geometric bound.

21.3 The Fast Fourier Transform
Computing a matrix-vector product with a general dense \(n \times n\) matrix requires \(2n^2\) flops. The Cooley–Tukey Fast Fourier Transform (FFT) factors the DFT matrix into sparse structured factors, reducing the complexity to \(O(n \log n)\) operations.






NoteTheorem: Radix-2 Cooley–Tukey Decomposition



Let \(n\) be an even integer, and let \(\bx \in \fC^n\). Partition \(\bx\) into even-indexed and odd-indexed subvectors \(\bx_E, \bx_O \in \fC^{n/2}\) defined by \((\bx_E)_j = x_{2j}\) and \((\bx_O)_j = x_{2j+1}\) for \(j = 0, ..., n/2 - 1\). The \(n\)-point unnormalized DFT \(\hat{x}_k = \sum_{j=0}^{n-1} x_j \omega_n^{jk}\) satisfies the butterfly identity \[
\begin{align}
\hat{x}_k &= (\hat{\bx}_E)_k + \omega_n^k (\hat{\bx}_O)_k, \\
    \hat{x}_{k + n/2} &= (\hat{\bx}_E)_k - \omega_n^k (\hat{\bx}_O)_k,
\end{align}
\] for each \(k = 0, 1, ..., n/2 - 1\), where \(\hat{\bx}_E, \hat{\bx}_O\) are the \((n/2)\)-point DFTs of \(\bx_E\) and \(\bx_O\). When \(n = 2^p\), applying this reduction recursively requires \(O(n \log n)\) floating-point operations.








TipProof





Splitting the DFT sum into even and odd indices gives \[
\begin{align}
\hat{x}_k = \sum_{m=0}^{n/2 - 1} x_{2m} \omega_n^{2mk} + \sum_{m=0}^{n/2 - 1} x_{2m+1} \omega_n^{(2m+1)k}.
\end{align}
\] Because \(\omega_n^2 = e^{-4\pi i / n} = e^{-2\pi i / (n/2)} = \omega_{n/2}\), the first sum is the \((n/2)\)-point transform of \(\bx_E\): \[
\begin{align}
\sum_{m=0}^{n/2 - 1} x_{2m} \omega_{n/2}^{mk} = (\hat{\bx}_E)_k.
\end{align}
\] Factoring \(\omega_n^k\) out of the second sum gives \(\omega_n^k \sum_{m=0}^{n/2 - 1} x_{2m+1} \omega_{n/2}^{mk} = \omega_n^k (\hat{\bx}_O)_k\). This establishes the first identity: \[
\begin{align}
\hat{x}_k = (\hat{\bx}_E)_k + \omega_n^k (\hat{\bx}_O)_k.
\end{align}
\] For the second half of the spectrum \(k + n/2\), the periodicity \(\omega_{n/2}^{m(k + n/2)} = \omega_{n/2}^{mk} (\omega_{n/2}^{n/2})^m = \omega_{n/2}^{mk}\) and the half-period phase shift \[
\begin{align}
\omega_n^{k + n/2} = \omega_n^k \omega_n^{n/2} = \omega_n^k e^{-\pi i} = -\omega_n^k
\end{align}
\] give the complementary butterfly equation: \[
\begin{align}
\hat{x}_{k + n/2} = (\hat{\bx}_E)_k - \omega_n^k (\hat{\bx}_O)_k.
\end{align}
\] The second half of the output reuses the same two smaller transforms. Thus the reduction needs two transforms of length \(n/2\), rather than four. The minus sign distinguishes the second half without requiring additional recursive calls.
In matrix form, introducing the diagonal twiddle matrix \(\boldsymbol{\Omega}_{n/2} = \operatorname{diag}(1, \omega_n, ..., \omega_n^{n/2-1})\) and the even–odd permutation matrix \(\bP_n\): \[
\begin{align}
\hat{\bx} = \begin{pmatrix} \bI_{n/2} & \boldsymbol{\Omega}_{n/2} \\ \bI_{n/2} & -\boldsymbol{\Omega}_{n/2} \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} \hat{\bx}_E \\ \hat{\bx}_O \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} \bI_{n/2} & \boldsymbol{\Omega}_{n/2} \\ \bI_{n/2} & -\boldsymbol{\Omega}_{n/2} \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} \mathbf{W}_{n/2} & \bzero \\ \bzero & \mathbf{W}_{n/2} \end{pmatrix} \bP_n \bx.
\end{align}
\] At each recursion stage, the butterfly recombination performs \(n/2\) complex multiplications and \(n\) additions. The arithmetic complexity satisfies the recurrence \[
\begin{align}
T(n) = 2 T(n/2) + c n.
\end{align}
\] For \(n = 2^p\), the depth of the recursion tree is \(\log_2 n\), yielding total cost \(T(n) = c n \log_2 n = O(n \log n)\).
To solve the work recurrence explicitly, divide it by \(n\) and put \(n=2^p\). Then \(T(2^p)/2^p=T(2^{p-1})/2^{p-1}+c\). Repeating this equality \(p\) times gives \(T(n)/n=T(1)+cp\). Since \(p=\log_2n\), the work is \(nT(1)+cn\log_2n\). This supplies the stated order including the base-case contribution.

21.4 Spectral Differentiation
For smooth periodic functions, differentiation in the Fourier domain replaces finite-difference stencils with exact multiplication in frequency.






NoteTheorem: Spectral Differentiation of Periodic Functions



Let \(u: \fR \to \fC\) be a continuously differentiable, \(2\pi\)-periodic function. Representing \(u\) by its Fourier series \(u(x) = \sum_{k=-\infty}^\infty \hat{u}_k e^{ikx}\), the derivative has Fourier coefficients \[
\begin{align}
\widehat{(u')}_k = ik \hat{u}_k.
\end{align}
\] If \(u\) is analytic and bounded in a strip \(\{z \in \fC : |\operatorname{Im}(z)| &lt; d\}\), the truncation error of the discrete spectral derivative converges geometrically to zero: \[
\begin{align}
\max_{x_j} |u'(x_j) - (D_n \bu)_j| = O(n e^{-d n/2}).
\end{align}
\]








TipProof





For the coefficient of the derivative, integrate by parts over one period: \[
\begin{align}
\widehat{(u')}_k
&=\frac1{2\pi}\int_0^{2\pi}u'(x)e^{-ikx}\,dx\\
&=\frac1{2\pi}[u(x)e^{-ikx}]_0^{2\pi}
   +\frac{ik}{2\pi}\int_0^{2\pi}u(x)e^{-ikx}\,dx
=ik\hat u_k.
\end{align}
\] The boundary term is zero because \(u(2\pi)=u(0)\) and \(e^{-2\pi ik}=1\) for integer \(k\). This argument requires only the stated continuous differentiability, rather than an unjustified differentiation of a conditionally convergent series.
For the error bound, assume the additional analyticity and boundedness in the strip, with bound \(M\). For \(k&gt;0\), shift the coefficient integral to the line with imaginary part \(-d'\), where \(0&lt;d'&lt;d\). Periodicity cancels the two vertical sides of the contour. On the shifted line, \(|e^{-ik(x-id')}|=e^{-kd'}\), so \(|\hat u_k|\leq Me^{-kd'}\). For \(k&lt;0\), shift upwards instead. Letting \(d'\uparrow d\) gives \(|\hat u_k|\leq Me^{-d|k|}\).
Differentiation multiplies coefficient magnitude by \(|k|\). If \(K\) is of order \(n/2\), the differentiated tail is bounded by \[
\begin{align}
2M\sum_{k=K}^\infty k e^{-dk}.
\end{align}
\] Set \(q=e^{-d}&lt;1\) and \(k=K+j\). The sum is \[
\begin{align}
\sum_{k=K}^\infty kq^k
=q^K\left(K\sum_{j=0}^\infty q^j+\sum_{j=0}^\infty jq^j\right)
=q^K\left(\frac K{1-q}+\frac q{(1-q)^2}\right).
\end{align}
\] It is therefore \(O(Ke^{-dK})\).
Sampling replaces a high mode by its aliased grid frequency. The difference between the true derivative multiplier and the aliased multiplier is at most \(|k|+K\), which is at most a constant times \(|k|\) for an omitted mode. Summing their coefficient bounds therefore gives the same order for aliasing as for the tail. Taking \(K\) of order \(n/2\) yields \(O(ne^{-dn/2})\).
For even \(n\) and real data, the Nyquist samples are those of a cosine whose derivative vanishes on the grid. Setting that first-derivative component to zero gives the derivative of the real trigonometric interpolant, as stipulated in the theorem.

NoteDefinition: Jacobi Iteration



Let \(\bA \in \fR^{n \times n}\) with nonzero diagonal entries \(a_{ii} \neq 0\). The Jacobi method corresponds to the splitting \(\bM = \bD\) and \(\mathbf{N} = \bL_A + \bU_A\). In component form, the update is \[
\begin{align}
x_i^{(k+1)} = \frac{1}{a_{ii}} \left( b_i - \sum_{j \neq i} a_{ij} x_j^{(k)} \right), \qquad i = 1, 2, ..., n.
\end{align}
\] In matrix notation, the iteration is given by \[
\begin{align}
\bx^{(k+1)} = \bT_J \bx^{(k)} + \bc_J,
\end{align}
\] where \(\bT_J = \bD^{-1}(\bL_A + \bU_A) = \bI - \bD^{-1}\bA\) is the Jacobi iteration matrix and \(\bc_J = \bD^{-1}\bb\).








TipRemark



(Parallel execution and memory) Because each component \(x_i^{(k+1)}\) depends only on values from the previous iteration vector \(\bx^{(k)}\), all \(n\) components can be computed concurrently. This fine-grained parallelism makes Jacobi attractive on vector and GPU hardware. However, it requires double buffering in memory to store \(\bx^{(k)}\) and \(\bx^{(k+1)}\) simultaneously.

24.2 The Gauss–Seidel Method
The Gauss–Seidel method uses updated variable values as soon as they become available within a sweep. This changes the iteration matrix, so its convergence must be examined separately.






NoteDefinition: Gauss–Seidel Iteration



Let \(\bA \in \fR^{n \times n}\) with nonzero diagonal entries. The Gauss–Seidel method corresponds to the triangular splitting \(\bM = \bD - \bL_A\) and \(\mathbf{N} = \bU_A\). In component form, the update is \[
\begin{align}
x_i^{(k+1)} = \frac{1}{a_{ii}} \left( b_i - \sum_{j &lt; i} a_{ij} x_j^{(k+1)} - \sum_{j &gt; i} a_{ij} x_j^{(k)} \right), \qquad i = 1, 2, ..., n.
\end{align}
\] In matrix notation, the iteration is given by \[
\begin{align}
(\bD - \bL_A) \bx^{(k+1)} = \bU_A \bx^{(k)} + \bb \iff \bx^{(k+1)} = \bT_{GS} \bx^{(k)} + \bc_{GS},
\end{align}
\] where \(\bT_{GS} = (\bD - \bL_A)^{-1}\bU_A\) is the Gauss–Seidel iteration matrix and \(\bc_{GS} = (\bD - \bL_A)^{-1}\bb\).








WarningExercise



Compute the Jacobi and Gauss-Seidel iteration matrices for \(\bA = \begin{pmatrix} 2 & 1 \\ 1 & 2 \end{pmatrix}\). Find their spectral radii and verify that \(\rho(\bT_{GS}) = \rho(\bT_J)^2\).








TipRemark



(Sequential dependencies and in-place updates) Because the calculation of \(x_i^{(k+1)}\) requires the newly computed values \(x_1^{(k+1)}, ..., x_{i-1}^{(k+1)}\), the standard Gauss–Seidel sweep is inherently sequential. However, it permits in-place updates, in which new values immediately overwrite old values in a single vector. For the standard Poisson problem in the natural ordering, the Gauss–Seidel spectral radius is the square of the Jacobi spectral radius. Such a comparison needs structure and does not hold for arbitrary matrices.








NoteExample



(Comparison of one Jacobi and Gauss–Seidel step) Consider the linear system \[
\begin{align}
\begin{pmatrix} 4 & 1 & 0 \\ 1 & 4 & 1 \\ 0 & 1 & 4 \end{pmatrix}
    \begin{pmatrix} x_1 \\ x_2 \\ x_3 \end{pmatrix}
    =
    \begin{pmatrix} 5 \\ 6 \\ 5 \end{pmatrix},
\end{align}
\] whose exact solution is \(\bx^* = (1, 1, 1)^T\). Let the initial guess be \(\bx^{(0)} = (0, 0, 0)^T\).

Jacobi Step: \[
  \begin{align}
  x_1^{(1)} &= \frac{1}{4}(5 - 0 - 0) = 1.25, \\
          x_2^{(1)} &= \frac{1}{4}(6 - 0 - 0) = 1.50, \\
          x_3^{(1)} &= \frac{1}{4}(5 - 0 - 0) = 1.25, \qquad \implies \bx_J^{(1)} = \begin{pmatrix} 1.25 \\ 1.50 \\ 1.25 \end{pmatrix}.
  \end{align}
  \]
Gauss–Seidel Step: \[
  \begin{align}
  x_1^{(1)} &= \frac{1}{4}(5 - 0 - 0) = 1.25, \\
          x_2^{(1)} &= \frac{1}{4}(6 - (1.25) - 0) = \frac{4.75}{4} = 1.1875, \\
          x_3^{(1)} &= \frac{1}{4}(5 - (1.1875) - 0) = \frac{3.8125}{4} \approx 0.9531, \qquad \implies \bx_{GS}^{(1)} \approx \begin{pmatrix} 1.2500 \\ 1.1875 \\ 0.9531 \end{pmatrix}.
  \end{align}
  \]

The Gauss–Seidel iterate is closer to the true solution \((1, 1, 1)^T\) because the update for \(x_2\) immediately used the new value of \(x_1\), and the update for \(x_3\) used the new value of \(x_2\).

24.3 Convergence Theorems






NoteTheorem: Convergence Under Strict Diagonal Dominance



A matrix \(\bA \in \fR^{n \times n}\) is strictly diagonally dominant if \[
\begin{align}
|a_{ii}| &gt; \sum_{j \neq i} |a_{ij}| \qquad \text{for all } i = 1, 2, ..., n.
\end{align}
\] If \(\bA\) is strictly diagonally dominant, then both the Jacobi iteration and the Gauss–Seidel iteration converge for any initial guess \(\bx^{(0)} \in \fR^n\).








TipProof





We show that the matrix \(\infty\)-norm of the iteration matrix \(\bT_J = \bD^{-1}(\bL_A + \bU_A)\) is strictly bounded by one. The entries of \(\bT_J\) are \[
\begin{align}
(\bT_J)_{ij} = \begin{cases}
        -\frac{a_{ij}}{a_{ii}}, & i \neq j, \\
        0, & i = j.
    \end{cases}
\end{align}
\] The induced \(\infty\)-norm is the maximum absolute row sum: \[
\begin{align}
\|\bT_J\|_\infty
    = \max_{1 \leq i \leq n} \sum_{j=1}^n |(\bT_J)_{ij}|
    = \max_{1 \leq i \leq n} \sum_{j \neq i} \frac{|a_{ij}|}{|a_{ii}|}.
\end{align}
\] Because \(\bA\) is strictly diagonally dominant, \(|a_{ii}| &gt; \sum_{j \neq i} |a_{ij}|\) for each \(i \in \{1, ..., n\}\), so \[
\begin{align}
\sum_{j \neq i} \frac{|a_{ij}|}{|a_{ii}|} &lt; 1
    \implies
    \|\bT_J\|_\infty &lt; 1.
\end{align}
\] By Gelfand’s spectral radius theorem, \(\rho(\bT_J) \leq \|\bT_J\|_\infty &lt; 1\), which establishes convergence of the Jacobi iteration.
The estimate is uniform over all rows. Hence its maximum is a fixed number smaller than one, rather than a different bound for each iterate. Repeated multiplication by the iteration matrix then makes every Jacobi error tend to zero.
For Gauss–Seidel, let \(\lambda\) be an eigenvalue of \(\bT_{GS} = (\bD - \bL_A)^{-1}\bU_A\) with eigenvector \(\bv \neq \bzero\), normalized so that \(\|\bv\|_\infty = |v_p| = 1\). From \(\bU_A \bv = \lambda(\bD - \bL_A)\bv\), row \(p\) reads \[
\begin{align}
-\sum_{j &gt; p} a_{pj} v_j = \lambda a_{pp} v_p + \lambda \sum_{j &lt; p} a_{pj} v_j.
\end{align}
\] Rearranging and applying the triangle inequality with \(|v_j| \leq 1\) yields \[
\begin{align}
|\lambda| |a_{pp}|
    \leq \sum_{j &gt; p} |a_{pj}| + |\lambda| \sum_{j &lt; p} |a_{pj}|.
\end{align}
\] If \(|\lambda| \ge 1\), then \[
\begin{align}
|a_{pp}| \leq \frac{1}{|\lambda|} \sum_{j &gt; p} |a_{pj}| + \sum_{j &lt; p} |a_{pj}| \leq \sum_{j \neq p} |a_{pj}|,
\end{align}
\] which contradicts strict diagonal dominance. The contradiction came from choosing an entry of maximal eigenvector magnitude. Such an entry is nonzero because the eigenvector is nonzero, so the normalization and divisions were legitimate. The index need not be the first or last row.
Hence \(|\lambda| &lt; 1\), proving \(\rho(\bT_{GS}) &lt; 1\).









NoteTheorem: Convergence of Gauss–Seidel for Symmetric Positive Definite Systems



If \(\bA \in \fR^{n \times n}\) is symmetric positive definite, then the Gauss–Seidel method converges for every initial vector \(\bx^{(0)} \in \fR^n\). The Jacobi method, however, is not guaranteed to converge for arbitrary SPD matrices.








TipProof





Let \(\bx^*\) be the exact solution and define \(\phi(\bx)=\tfrac12\bx^T\bA\bx-\bb^T\bx\). During a sweep, let \(\by\) denote the vector after any preceding coordinate updates, and let \(r_i=b_i-(\bA\by)_i\) be its current residual entry.
Changing only coordinate \(i\) by \(d\) gives \[
\begin{align}
\phi(\by+d\be_i)-\phi(\by)=-dr_i+\tfrac12a_{ii}d^2.
\end{align}
\] The diagonal entry is positive because \(\bA\) is SPD. Completing the square gives \[
\begin{align}
-dr_i+\tfrac12a_{ii}d^2
=\tfrac12a_{ii}(d-r_i/a_{ii})^2-\frac{r_i^2}{2a_{ii}}.
\end{align}
\] The Gauss–Seidel update is exactly \(d=r_i/a_{ii}\), using all coordinates at their current values. It minimizes this expression and decreases \(\phi\) by \(a_{ii}d^2/2\).
Sum the decreases over the sweep. They are all nonnegative. Their sum vanishes only when every coordinate change is zero. If no coordinate changes, the vector remains the same throughout the sweep, and \(r_i=0\) when each row is visited. All rows are therefore satisfied, so the vector is \(\bx^*\).
The energy identity gives \(\phi(\by)-\phi(\bx^*)=\tfrac12\|\by-\bx^*\|_{\bA}^2\). Thus one sweep strictly decreases the energy norm of every nonzero error. Let \(\bT_{GS}\) be its linear error map. The function \(\be\mapsto\|\bT_{GS}\be\|_{\bA}\) is continuous on the compact energy-unit sphere. Its attained maximum is a number \(q&lt;1\), because every value there is strictly less than one. Homogeneity yields \(\|\bT_{GS}\be\|_{\bA}\leq q\|\be\|_{\bA}\) for every error. Repeating sweeps gives \(\|\be^{(k)}\|_{\bA}\leq q^k\|\be^{(0)}\|_{\bA}\to0\). This proves convergence. See also Saad (2003) for stationary-iteration theory.









WarningExercise



Complete the square for a coordinate update and derive the decrease \(a_{ii}d_i^2/2\). To see why the same conclusion need not hold for Jacobi, take the \(3\times3\) matrix with diagonal entries \(1\) and all off-diagonal entries \(3/4\). Compute its eigenvalues and those of its Jacobi iteration matrix.

24.4 Successive Over-Relaxation (SOR)
In an SOR sweep, compute the value proposed by a Gauss–Seidel coordinate update and take a fraction \(\omega\) of the change. Values \(0&lt;\omega&lt;1\) shorten the change. Values \(\omega&gt;1\) extend it. This can accelerate convergence for a suitable matrix and parameter, but can also cause divergence.






NoteDefinition: Successive Over-Relaxation Scheme



Let \(\omega &gt; 0\) be a relaxation parameter. The SOR method computes the updated components via \[
\begin{align}
x_i^{(k+1)} = (1 - \omega) x_i^{(k)} + \frac{\omega}{a_{ii}} \left( b_i - \sum_{j &lt; i} a_{ij} x_j^{(k+1)} - \sum_{j &gt; i} a_{ij} x_j^{(k)} \right).
\end{align}
\] In matrix notation, this corresponds to the splitting \(\bM_\omega = \frac{1}{\omega}\bD - \bL_A\) and \(\mathbf{N}_\omega = \left( \frac{1}{\omega} - 1 \right)\bD + \bU_A\). The iteration matrix is \[
\begin{align}
\bT_\omega = (\bD - \omega \bL_A)^{-1} \left( (1 - \omega)\bD + \omega \bU_A \right).
\end{align}
\]








WarningExercise



Prove the Kahan theorem: for any matrix \(\bA\) with nonzero diagonal entries, \(\rho(\bT_\omega) \geq |\omega - 1|\), which implies that SOR can only converge if \(0 &lt; \omega &lt; 2\).








NoteTheorem: Ostrowski–Reich Theorem and the Optimal Parameter



If \(\bA\) is symmetric positive definite, then the SOR iteration converges if and only if the relaxation parameter satisfies \[
\begin{align}
0 &lt; \omega &lt; 2.
\end{align}
\] For the one-dimensional Dirichlet Poisson matrix in its natural ordering, with Jacobi spectral radius \(\rho(\bT_J)=\cos(\pi h)&lt;1\), the optimal relaxation parameter that minimizes \(\rho(\bT_\omega)\) is \[
\begin{align}
\omega^* = \frac{2}{1 + \sqrt{1 - \rho(\bT_J)^2}}.
\end{align}
\] With this optimal choice, the spectral radius satisfies \(\rho(\bT_{\omega^*}) = \omega^* - 1\).


Let \(\phi(\bx)=\tfrac12\bx^T\bA\bx-\bb^T\bx\), and consider the current vector just before coordinate \(i\) is updated. Write \(g_i=(\bA\bx-\bb)_i\). Holding the other coordinates fixed gives \[
\begin{align}
\phi(\bx+d_i\be_i)-\phi(\bx)=g_i d_i+\tfrac12a_{ii}d_i^2.
\end{align}
\] The Gauss–Seidel change is \(-g_i/a_{ii}\). SOR takes \(d_i=-\omega g_i/a_{ii}\), so \(g_i=-a_{ii}d_i/\omega\). Substitution gives \[
\begin{align}
\phi(\bx)-\phi(\bx+d_i\be_i)
=\frac{a_{ii}(2-\omega)}{2\omega}d_i^2.
\end{align}
\] For an SPD matrix, \(a_{ii}&gt;0\). Thus every nonzero coordinate change decreases the energy when \(0&lt;\omega&lt;2\).
Let \(\bx_*\) solve the system and let \(\be=\bx-\bx_*\). Expanding the quadratic and using \(\bA\bx_*=\bb\) gives \(\phi(\bx)-\phi(\bx_*)=\tfrac12\|\be\|_{\bA}^2\). A complete sweep therefore strictly reduces the error norm unless every coordinate change is zero. If all changes are zero, the vector remains fixed throughout the sweep, and \(g_i=0\) for every \(i\). It is then already the solution.
The error after a sweep is \(\bT_\omega\be\). The continuous function \(\be\mapsto\|\bT_\omega\be\|_{\bA}\) attains a maximum \(q\) on the compact unit sphere \(\|\be\|_{\bA}=1\). The strict decrease just proved holds at every point on that sphere, including a maximizing point, so \(q&lt;1\). Scaling gives \(\|\bT_\omega\be\|_{\bA}\leq q\|\be\|_{\bA}\) for every error. After \(k\) sweeps the norm is at most \(q^k\) times its initial value, which tends to zero.
For necessity, the determinant exercise above gives \(\rho(\bT_\omega)\geq|\omega-1|\). Convergence requires spectral radius less than one, hence \(|\omega-1|&lt;1\), or \(0&lt;\omega&lt;2\). The optimal-parameter formula for the Poisson problem is stated without proof; see the treatment of SOR in (Saad 2003).






TipRemark



(Acceleration for discrete Poisson equations) For the 1D discrete Laplacian on a grid with spacing \(h\), the Jacobi spectral radius is \(\rho(\bT_J) = \cos(\pi h) \approx 1 - \frac{1}{2}\pi^2 h^2\). Consequently, Jacobi and Gauss–Seidel require \(O(h^{-2}) = O(n^2)\) iterations to converge. With optimal SOR, \(\omega^* \approx 2 - 2\pi h\), yielding \(\rho(\bT_{\omega^*}) \approx 1 - 2\pi h\). This reduces the required iteration count to \(O(h^{-1}) = O(n)\), providing an order-of-magnitude computational speedup.

25.1 Quadratic Energy
Suppose \(\bA\) is symmetric positive definite and we want to solve \(\bA\bx=\bb\). A factorization is one way to find \(\bx\). For a large sparse matrix, we would like to use the cheaper operation \(\bv\mapsto\bA\bv\) instead. Starting with a guess, how should we choose a correction that brings us closer to the solution?
Conjugate gradients answers this question by associating a scalar function with the system. The solution is the point where this function is smallest. We first establish that correspondence, then learn how to minimize the function along one direction. The final ingredient is to choose successive directions so that a new correction preserves the progress made along earlier ones. The resulting method uses one matrix–vector product per step and stores only a few vectors.
Consider first the scalar equation \(ax=b\), with \(a&gt;0\). The function \[
\begin{align}
q(x)=\frac{a}{2}x^2-bx
\end{align}
\] has derivative \(q'(x)=ax-b\). Thus the equation we want to solve appears when we set this derivative to zero. More is true: completing the square gives \[
\begin{align}
q(x)=\frac{a}{2}\left(x-\frac ba\right)^2-\frac{b^2}{2a}.
\end{align}
\] Because \(a&gt;0\), the squared term is nonnegative and vanishes only at \(x=b/a\). Solving the equation and minimizing \(q\) therefore give the same answer.
For a symmetric matrix, the analogous function is \[
\begin{align}
\phi(\bx)=\frac12\bx^T\bA\bx-\bb^T\bx.
\end{align}
\] It takes a vector as input and returns one real number. The word quadratic refers to the products \(x_ix_j\) in the first term. We call \(\phi\) an energy. In some applications it is a physical potential energy. Here its role is to help us solve the equations. Its value need not be positive.
The same completion-of-the-square argument works in any dimension. Recall that for an SPD matrix, \(\|\be\|_{\bA}^2=\be^T\bA\be\) is positive for every nonzero \(\be\).
This identity explains the choice of energy. Lowering \(\phi\) lowers the squared solution error measured in the \(\bA\)-norm by exactly the same amount, apart from the factor \(1/2\). We do not know \(\bx^*\), so we cannot evaluate that error directly. We can evaluate \(\phi(\bx)\) and, more usefully, its change under a proposed correction using only \(\bA\), \(\bb\), and the current guess.

25.3 Conjugate Directions
Suppose we have minimized along a direction \(\bp\), arriving at \(\by\) with residual \(\br_{\by}\). The zero-slope condition is \(\bp^T\br_{\by}=0\). Now take a step of length \(\beta\) along another direction \(\bq\). The new residual is \(\br_{\by}-\beta\bA\bq\), so \[
\begin{align}
\bp^T(\br_{\by}-\beta\bA\bq)
    =-\beta\bp^T\bA\bq.
\end{align}
\] If \(\bp^T\bA\bq=0\), the slope along \(\bp\) remains zero after the new step. We have found the condition that lets us retain an earlier line minimization. It is orthogonality in the energy inner product.






NoteDefinition: A-Conjugate Vectors



Nonzero vectors \(\bp_0,...,\bp_{k-1}\) are \(\bA\)-conjugate if \[
\begin{align}
\bp_i^T\bA\bp_j=0\qquad(i\neq j).
\end{align}
\] Equivalently, they are orthogonal in the inner product \(\langle\bu,\bv\rangle_{\bA}=\bu^T\bA\bv\).








WarningExercise



Return to \(\bA=\begin{pmatrix}2&1\\1&2\end{pmatrix}\) and \(\bb=(4,5)^T\).

Take \(\bp_0=(1,0)^T\). Find \(c\) so that \(\bp_1=(c,1)^T\) is \(\bA\)-conjugate to \(\bp_0\). Are the two directions Euclidean-orthogonal?
Starting at the origin, minimize first along \(\bp_0\) and then along \(\bp_1\). Verify that you arrive at \((1,2)^T\) and that the second step preserves the zero slope along \(\bp_0\).
Prove that any collection of nonzero \(\bA\)-conjugate vectors is linearly independent. Take the \(\bA\)-inner product of a vanishing linear combination with each direction.









NoteTheorem: Minimization along Conjugate Directions



Let \(\bp_0,...,\bp_{n-1}\) be an \(\bA\)-conjugate basis. Starting from \(\bx^{(0)}\), minimize successively along these directions. With \(\br^{(k)}=\bb-\bA\bx^{(k)}\), the steps are \[
\begin{align}
\alpha_k&=\frac{\bp_k^T\br^{(k)}}{\bp_k^T\bA\bp_k}
             =\frac{\bp_k^T\br^{(0)}}{\bp_k^T\bA\bp_k},\\
    \bx^{(k+1)}&=\bx^{(k)}+\alpha_k\bp_k.
\end{align}
\] After \(k\) steps, the iterate minimizes \(\phi\), and hence the \(\bA\)-norm of the solution error, among all points \[
\begin{align}
\bx^{(0)}+c_0\bp_0+\cdots+c_{k-1}\bp_{k-1}.
\end{align}
\] After \(n\) steps it is the exact solution.








TipProof





The essential point is that conjugacy separates a problem in several coefficients into independent scalar quadratics.

Put \(\bd=\sum_{i=0}^{k-1}c_i\bp_i\) and \(\br^{(0)}=\bb-\bA\bx^{(0)}\). The preceding energy expansion gives \[
\begin{align}
\phi(\bx^{(0)}+\bd)
  =\phi(\bx^{(0)})-\bd^T\br^{(0)}+\tfrac12\bd^T\bA\bd.
\end{align}
\] Substituting the sum for \(\bd\) produces a double sum in the last term: \[
\begin{align}
\bd^T\bA\bd=\sum_{i,j=0}^{k-1}c_ic_j\bp_i^T\bA\bp_j
  =\sum_{i=0}^{k-1}c_i^2\bp_i^T\bA\bp_i.
\end{align}
\] The terms with \(i\neq j\) vanish by conjugacy.
Define \(d_i=\bp_i^T\bA\bp_i&gt;0\) and \(g_i=\bp_i^T\br^{(0)}\). The terms depending on \(c_i\) are \[
\begin{align}
\tfrac12d_ic_i^2-g_ic_i
  =\tfrac12d_i(c_i-g_i/d_i)^2-\frac{g_i^2}{2d_i}.
\end{align}
\] Their unique minimum occurs at \(c_i=g_i/d_i\). No other coefficient occurs in this expression, so optimizing a later coefficient cannot change an earlier minimizing value.
After the first \(k\) updates, \[
\begin{align}
\br^{(k)}=\br^{(0)}-\sum_{i=0}^{k-1}\alpha_i\bA\bp_i.
\end{align}
\] Multiply by \(\bp_k^T\). Every term in the sum vanishes, giving \(\bp_k^T\br^{(k)}=\bp_k^T\br^{(0)}\). Hence the line-search coefficient computed from the current residual is exactly \(g_k/d_k\).
After \(k\) steps, every available coefficient has its unique minimizing value. After \(n\) steps, the basis spans \(\fR^n\), so the admissible points include the unique global minimizer \(\bx^*\). Thus the final point is the exact solution.

25.4 The Conjugate Gradient Algorithm
The theorem assumes that a conjugate basis is already available. Constructing and storing an entire basis before solving would be too expensive. CG instead builds one new direction per step from the current residual and the preceding direction. The proof below explains why this short recurrence makes the new direction conjugate to all earlier ones.
The first \(k\) directions span \(\mathcal K_k(\bA,\br^{(0)})\). Thus the points available after \(k\) steps form \(\bx^{(0)}+\mathcal K_k\), the initial guess plus a Krylov subspace. The preceding theorem explains what CG optimizes within that set: it minimizes the energy, or equivalently the error in the \(\bA\)-norm.






NoteTheorem: The Conjugate Gradient Algorithm



Let \(\bA \in \fR^{n \times n}\) be symmetric positive definite and let \(\bb \in \fR^n\). Given an initial estimate \(\bx^{(0)}\), compute \(\br^{(0)} = \bb - \bA\bx^{(0)}\) and set \(\bp^{(0)} = \br^{(0)}\). If the initial residual is zero, return immediately. Otherwise, for \(k = 0, 1, 2, ...\):

Compute the matrix–vector product \(\bw^{(k)} = \bA \bp^{(k)}\).
Compute the step length: \[
\begin{align}
\alpha_k = \frac{\br^{(k)T}\br^{(k)}}{\bp^{(k)T}\bw^{(k)}}.
\end{align}
\]
Update the solution iterate: \[
\begin{align}
\bx^{(k+1)} = \bx^{(k)} + \alpha_k \bp^{(k)}.
\end{align}
\]
Update the residual vector: \[
\begin{align}
\br^{(k+1)} = \br^{(k)} - \alpha_k \bw^{(k)}.
\end{align}
\]
Check termination: if \(\|\br^{(k+1)}\|_2\leq\max(`atol`,`tol`\|\bb\|_2)\), terminate. Here \(`tol`\) is a relative tolerance and \(`atol`\) is an absolute tolerance, used also when \(\bb=\bzero\).
Compute the orthogonalization coefficient: \[
\begin{align}
\beta_k = \frac{\br^{(k+1)T}\br^{(k+1)}}{\br^{(k)T}\br^{(k)}}.
\end{align}
\]
Compute the next search direction: \[
\begin{align}
\bp^{(k+1)} = \br^{(k+1)} + \beta_k \bp^{(k)}.
\end{align}
\]









NoteTheorem: Orthogonality Properties of CG



In exact arithmetic, the sequences generated by the Conjugate Gradient algorithm satisfy the following orthogonality relations for all \(i \neq j\): \[
\begin{align}
\br^{(i)T}\br^{(j)} = 0 \qquad \text{and} \qquad \bp^{(i)T}\bA\bp^{(j)} = 0.
\end{align}
\] Moreover, the residual vectors and search directions span the Krylov subspace: \[
\begin{align}
\operatorname{span}\{\br^{(0)}, \br^{(1)}, ..., \br^{(k)}\} = \operatorname{span}\{\bp^{(0)}, \bp^{(1)}, ..., \bp^{(k)}\} = \mathcal{K}_{k+1}(\bA, \br^{(0)}).
\end{align}
\] Consequently, the method is guaranteed to reach the exact solution in at most \(n\) iterations in exact arithmetic.








TipProof





We use induction to establish the structure that makes the short recurrence valid. Write \(\mathcal K_k=\mathcal K_k(\bA,\br^{(0)})\) and set \(\mathcal K_0=\{\bzero\}\). All calculations are in exact arithmetic and stop when a residual is zero.

At the beginning of step \(k\), we claim that the previous directions \(\bp^{(0)},...,\bp^{(k-1)}\) are conjugate and span \(\mathcal K_k\), while \(\br^{(k)}\in\mathcal K_{k+1}\) and \(\br^{(k)}\perp\mathcal K_k\). For \(k=0\), there are no previous directions, and \(\br^{(0)}\in\mathcal K_1\) by definition. Its orthogonality to \(\mathcal K_0\) is automatic. Set \(\bp^{(0)}=\br^{(0)}\).
Suppose \(k\geq1\) and the claims hold through the preceding step. Rearrange each previous residual update: \[
\begin{align}
\bA\bp^{(i)}=\frac{\br^{(i)}-\br^{(i+1)}}{\alpha_i}.
\end{align}
\] For \(i&lt;k-1\), both residuals on the right belong to \(\mathcal K_k\). Therefore \[
\begin{align}
\br^{(k)T}\bA\bp^{(i)}
  =\frac{\br^{(k)T}\br^{(i)}-\br^{(k)T}\br^{(i+1)}}{\alpha_i}=0.
\end{align}
\] Only the direction with index \(k-1\) can require a correction.
In \(\bp^{(k)}=\br^{(k)}+\beta\bp^{(k-1)}\), require conjugacy with \(\bp^{(k-1)}\). This gives \[
\begin{align}
\beta=-\frac{\br^{(k)T}\bA\bp^{(k-1)}}{\bp^{(k-1)T}\bA\bp^{(k-1)}}.
\end{align}
\] The numerator is evaluated with the previous residual update. Since \(\br^{(k)T}\br^{(k-1)}=0\), \[
\begin{align}
\br^{(k)T}\bA\bp^{(k-1)}
  =-\frac{\|\br^{(k)}\|_2^2}{\alpha_{k-1}}.
\end{align}
\] The previous step length gives \(\alpha_{k-1}\bp^{(k-1)T}\bA\bp^{(k-1)}=\|\br^{(k-1)}\|_2^2\). Substitution yields exactly \(\beta_{k-1}=\|\br^{(k)}\|_2^2/\|\br^{(k-1)}\|_2^2\). For every \(i&lt;k-1\), the new direction is conjugate to \(\bp^{(i)}\) because both its residual term and its previous-direction term have zero \(\bA\)-inner product with that direction.
A nonzero \(\br^{(k)}\) is perpendicular to \(\mathcal K_k\), so it cannot belong to that space. It supplies one independent direction in \(\mathcal K_{k+1}\). Adding a multiple of \(\bp^{(k-1)}\in\mathcal K_k\) does not change the expanded span. Therefore the first \(k+1\) directions span \(\mathcal K_{k+1}\). For \(k=0\), the identity \(\bp^{(0)T}\br^{(0)}=\|\br^{(0)}\|_2^2\) follows from \(\bp^{(0)}=\br^{(0)}\). For \(k\geq1\), orthogonality gives \[
\begin{align}
\bp^{(k)T}\br^{(k)}
  =\|\br^{(k)}\|_2^2+\beta_{k-1}\bp^{(k-1)T}\br^{(k)}
  =\|\br^{(k)}\|_2^2.
\end{align}
\] Thus exact line minimization gives the algorithm’s stated \(\alpha_k\). Its denominator is positive because \(\bA\) is SPD and \(\bp^{(k)}\neq\bzero\).
For \(i&lt;k\), multiply the new residual update by \(\bp^{(i)T}\). The old residual is perpendicular to \(\bp^{(i)}\), and conjugacy removes the update term. For \(i=k\), the step-length formula gives zero directly. Hence \(\br^{(k+1)}\) is perpendicular to every direction spanning \(\mathcal K_{k+1}\). The update also puts it in \(\mathcal K_{k+2}\). This proves the induction assertions at the next step. The preceding conjugate-direction theorem proves energy minimality.
Earlier residuals lie in the previous Krylov spaces, so the orthogonality just proved makes all nonzero residuals mutually perpendicular. The direction recurrence gives equality of the residual and direction spans. There cannot be more than \(n\) independent directions in \(\fR^n\). If \(n\) nonzero directions have been generated, they span the whole space, and the next residual, perpendicular to that space, is zero. Thus termination occurs in at most \(n\) steps.

The standard CG analysis is developed in Saad (2003).









TipRemark



(Connection with Lanczos) By the referenced result, the residuals \(\br^{(0)},...,\br^{(k-1)}\) are mutually orthogonal and the first \(j\) of them span \(\mathcal{K}_j(\bA,\br^{(0)})\). The Lanczos iteration of the referenced result, started from \(\bv_1=\br^{(0)}/\|\br^{(0)}\|_2\), produces an orthonormal basis with the same nested spans. Such a basis is unique up to the signs of its vectors, and the signs work out to \[
\begin{align}
\bv_{j+1}=(-1)^j\frac{\br^{(j)}}{\|\br^{(j)}\|_2},\qquad j=0,...,k-1.
\end{align}
\] The CG coefficients also determine the Lanczos matrix \(\bT_k\) of the referenced result. Its entries are \[
\begin{align}
(\bT_k)_{11}=\frac{1}{\alpha_0},\qquad
    (\bT_k)_{j+1,j+1}=\frac{1}{\alpha_j}+\frac{\beta_{j-1}}{\alpha_{j-1}},\qquad
    (\bT_k)_{j+1,j}=(\bT_k)_{j,j+1}=\frac{\sqrt{\beta_{j-1}}}{\alpha_{j-1}},
\end{align}
\] where \(\alpha_j\) and \(\beta_j\) are the CG step length and direction coefficient. Conjugate gradients may therefore be read as the Lanczos algorithm applied to a linear system.
The correspondence also describes the CG iterate directly. Write \(\bx^{(k)}=\bx^{(0)}+\bV_k\by_k\). Since \(\br^{(k)}\) is orthogonal to \(\mathcal{K}_k\), and since \(\bV_k^T\br^{(0)}=\|\br^{(0)}\|_2\be_1\) and \(\bV_k^T\bA\bV_k=\bT_k\), we obtain \[
\begin{align}
\bzero=\bV_k^T\br^{(k)}=\bV_k^T(\br^{(0)}-\bA\bV_k\by_k)=\|\br^{(0)}\|_2\be_1-\bT_k\by_k.
\end{align}
\] The CG iterate is thus obtained by solving the projected system \(\bT_k\by_k=\|\br^{(0)}\|_2\be_1\). In addition, the extreme eigenvalues of \(\bT_k\) approach \(\lambda_{\min}\) and \(\lambda_{\max}\) by the referenced result, so the CG coefficients give a running estimate of \(\kappa_2(\bA)\) at no extra cost in products with \(\bA\).








NoteExample



For \(\bA=\begin{pmatrix}2&1\\1&2\end{pmatrix}\), \(\bb=(4,5)^T\), and \(\bx^{(0)}=\bzero\), the initial direction is \(\bp^{(0)}=\br^{(0)}=(4,5)^T\). Thus \[
\begin{align}
\bA\bp^{(0)}&=(13,14)^T,&
\alpha_0&=\frac{41}{122},\\
\bx^{(1)}&=\frac1{122}(164,205)^T,&
\br^{(1)}&=\frac1{122}(-45,36)^T.
\end{align}
\] The new residual is perpendicular to the old residual because \(-45\cdot4+36\cdot5=0\). Continuing along \(\br^{(1)}\) alone would be steepest descent. CG instead adds a multiple of the old search direction to preserve the preceding minimization.








