NoteTheorem: The GMRES Projected Least-Squares Problem



Assume the Arnoldi process has completed \(k\) steps without breakdown. Let any vector in the affine space \(\bx^{(0)} + \mathcal{K}_k(\bA, \br^{(0)})\) be parameterized as \(\bx = \bx^{(0)} + \bV_k \by\) for some vector \(\by \in \fR^k\). The residual vector satisfies \[
\begin{align}
\br(\bx) = \bb - \bA(\bx^{(0)} + \bV_k \by) = \br^{(0)} - \bA \bV_k \by = \beta \bv_1 - \bV_{k+1} \bar{\bH}_k \by = \bV_{k+1} (\beta \be_1 - \bar{\bH}_k \by).
\end{align}
\] Because the columns of \(\bV_{k+1}\) are orthonormal, \(\bV_{k+1}\) is an isometry in the Euclidean norm: \[
\begin{align}
\|\bb - \bA\bx\|_2 = \|\beta \be_1 - \bar{\bH}_k \by\|_2,
\end{align}
\] where \(\be_1 = (1, 0, ..., 0)^T \in \fR^{k+1}\). The GMRES iterate is \(\bx^{(k)} = \bx^{(0)} + \bV_k \by_k\), where \(\by_k\) uniquely solves the \((k+1) \times k\) upper Hessenberg least-squares problem \[
\begin{align}
\by_k = \arg\min_{\by \in \fR^k} \|\beta \be_1 - \bar{\bH}_k \by\|_2.
\end{align}
\]








TipProof





The unknown in the reduced problem is the coefficient vector \(\by\), not the full vector \(\bx\). Since \(\bV_k\) is a basis of the correction space, every admissible approximation has a unique representation \(\bx=\bx^{(0)}+\bV_k\by\).
Substitute it into the residual and keep track of the dimensions. The Arnoldi relation uses \(\bar{\bH}_k\in\fR^{(k+1)\times k}\) and \(\bV_{k+1}\in\fR^{n\times(k+1)}\): \[
\begin{align}
\bb-\bA\bx
&=\bb-\bA\bx^{(0)}-\bA\bV_k\by\\
&=\br^{(0)}-\bV_{k+1}\bar{\bH}_k\by\\
&=\beta\bv_1-\bV_{k+1}\bar{\bH}_k\by\\
&=\bV_{k+1}(\beta\be_1-\bar{\bH}_k\by).
\end{align}
\] The last equality uses \(\bV_{k+1}\be_1=\bv_1\).
Put \(\bz=\beta\be_1-\bar{\bH}_k\by\). Orthonormality gives \[
\begin{align}
\|\bV_{k+1}\bz\|_2^2
=\bz^T\bV_{k+1}^T\bV_{k+1}\bz
=\bz^T\bz=\|\bz\|_2^2.
\end{align}
\] Taking square roots proves equality of the full and reduced residual norms for every coefficient vector. Hence minimizing one is exactly equivalent to minimizing the other.
For uniqueness, suppose \(\bar{\bH}_k\by=\bzero\). Its last row gives \(h_{k+1,k}y_k=0\). Before breakdown this subdiagonal entry is nonzero, so \(y_k=0\). The preceding row then gives \(h_{k,k-1}y_{k-1}=0\), so \(y_{k-1}=0\). Continue upwards to obtain all entries zero. Thus the reduced matrix has full column rank and its least-squares minimizer is unique. This is the standard GMRES reduction (Saad and Schultz 1986; Saad 2003).









WarningExercise



Before Arnoldi breakdown, show that \(\bar{\bH}_k\) has full column rank. Start with its last row and use the nonzero subdiagonal entries to show that \(\bar{\bH}_k\by=\bzero\) implies \(\by=\bzero\). Then use least-squares orthogonality to prove \(\br^{(k)}\perp\bA\mathcal K_k\). Explain why enlarging the subspace cannot increase the minimum residual norm.








NoteTheorem: Polynomial Residual Characterization and Spectral Bound



The \(k\)-th GMRES residual satisfies the polynomial minimization property \[
\begin{align}
\|\br^{(k)}\|_2 = \min_{p \in \mathcal{P}_k,\, p(0)=1} \|p(\bA)\br^{(0)}\|_2.
\end{align}
\] If \(\bA\) is diagonalizable with \(\bA = \bX \boldsymbol{\Lambda} \bX^{-1}\), where \(\boldsymbol{\Lambda} = \operatorname{diag}(\lambda_1, ..., \lambda_n)\), then the relative residual is bounded by \[
\begin{align}
\frac{\|\br^{(k)}\|_2}{\|\br^{(0)}\|_2} \leq \kappa_2(\bX) \min_{p \in \mathcal{P}_k,\, p(0)=1} \max_{\lambda \in \sigma(\bA)} |p(\lambda)|.
\end{align}
\]








TipProof





Write a general Krylov correction using its generating powers: \[
\begin{align}
\bx-\bx^{(0)}=\sum_{j=0}^{k-1}c_j\bA^j\br^{(0)}
=q(\bA)\br^{(0)},\qquad q(t)=\sum_{j=0}^{k-1}c_jt^j.
\end{align}
\] Multiplication by \(\bA\) and subtraction from the initial residual gives \[
\begin{align}
\br(\bx)&=\br^{(0)}-\bA q(\bA)\br^{(0)}\\
&=[\bI-\bA q(\bA)]\br^{(0)}=p(\bA)\br^{(0)},
\end{align}
\] where \(p(t)=1-tq(t)\) has degree at most \(k\) and satisfies \(p(0)=1\).
Conversely, let \(p(t)=1+a_1t+\cdots+a_kt^k\). Then \(q(t)=-a_1-a_2t-\cdots-a_kt^{k-1}\) is a polynomial and \(p(t)=1-tq(t)\). Thus every normalized polynomial in the statement corresponds to an admissible correction. GMRES minimizes over exactly this set, proving the first equality.
Now assume diagonalizability, \(\bA=\bX\boldsymbol\Lambda\bX^{-1}\). Induction gives \(\bA^j=\bX\boldsymbol\Lambda^j\bX^{-1}\), and substituting these powers into a polynomial gives \(p(\bA)=\bX p(\boldsymbol\Lambda)\bX^{-1}\). For any admissible \(p\), \[
\begin{align}
\|p(\bA)\br^{(0)}\|_2
&\leq\|\bX\|_2\|p(\boldsymbol\Lambda)\|_2
       \|\bX^{-1}\|_2\|\br^{(0)}\|_2\\
&=\kappa_2(\bX)\max_i|p(\lambda_i)|\|\br^{(0)}\|_2.
\end{align}
\] The Euclidean norm of a diagonal matrix is its largest diagonal magnitude, which explains the last equality. GMRES has residual no larger than that of this particular candidate. Taking the minimum of the upper bound over admissible polynomials and dividing by the nonzero initial residual norm proves the spectral estimate. If the initial residual is zero, the system is already solved and no relative ratio is needed.

WarningExercise



For \(\bA=\begin{pmatrix}2&c\\c&3\end{pmatrix}\), list the values of \(\bz^T\bA\bz\) for all four sign vectors. Compute their mean and variance. Explain why the mean is independent of \(c\) although the uncertainty is not.








NoteTheorem: Hutchinson’s Trace Estimator



Let \(\bA\in\fR^{n\times n}\) be symmetric, and let \(\bz\in\fR^n\) have independent entries with mean zero and variance one. Then \(\mathbb{E}[\bz^T\bA\bz]=\operatorname{tr}(\bA)\). If the entries of \(\bz\) are \(\pm1\) with equal probability, then \[
\begin{align}
\operatorname{Var}(\bz^T\bA\bz)=2\Big(\|\bA\|_F^2-\sum_{i=1}^na_{ii}^2\Big).
\end{align}
\] If the entries of \(\bz\) are standard normal, then \(\operatorname{Var}(\bz^T\bA\bz)=2\|\bA\|_F^2\).








TipProof





Write the quadratic form as \(\bz^T\bA\bz=\sum_{i,j}a_{ij}z_iz_j\). Independence and zero means give \(\mathbb E[z_iz_j]=0\) for \(i\neq j\), while unit variance gives \(\mathbb E[z_i^2]=1\). Linearity of expectation therefore gives \[
\begin{align}
\mathbb E[\bz^T\bA\bz]=\sum_i a_{ii}=\operatorname{tr}(\bA).
\end{align}
\] This expectation argument needs no assumption about higher moments.
For independent signs, \(z_i^2=1\) on every outcome. Symmetry of \(\bA\) pairs the off-diagonal terms, so the centered estimator is \[
\begin{align}
Z=\bz^T\bA\bz-\operatorname{tr}(\bA)
=2\sum_{i&lt;j}a_{ij}z_iz_j.
\end{align}
\] Each product has mean zero and squared value one. Two different unordered pairs have zero cross expectation. If the pairs are disjoint, all four independent factors have mean zero. If they share one index, its square is one and the two remaining independent factors each have mean zero. Thus \[
\begin{align}
\operatorname{Var}(Z)=\mathbb E[Z^2]
=4\sum_{i&lt;j}a_{ij}^2
=2\sum_{i\neq j}a_{ij}^2.
\end{align}
\] Subtracting the diagonal squares from \(\|\bA\|_F^2\) gives the first variance formula. The products need not be independent: the zero cross expectations are sufficient.
For Gaussian probes, diagonalize \(\bA=\bQ\boldsymbol\Lambda\bQ^T\) and put \(\bg=\bQ^T\bz\). The standard Gaussian density is proportional to \(e^{-\|\bz\|_2^2/2}\). Orthogonal changes preserve both the norm and volume, so \(\bg\) has the same independent standard normal coordinates. Hence \[
\begin{align}
\bz^T\bA\bz=\sum_i\lambda_i g_i^2.
\end{align}
\] A standard normal has second moment one and fourth moment three, obtained by integration by parts against its density. Thus \(\operatorname{Var}(g_i^2)=3-1=2\). Independence makes the covariances of distinct squared coordinates zero, giving \[
\begin{align}
\operatorname{Var}(\bz^T\bA\bz)=2\sum_i\lambda_i^2=2\|\bA\|_F^2.
\end{align}
\] The sign-probe estimator is attributed to Hutchinson (1990).









WarningExercise



Show that Hutchinson’s estimator with \(\pm1\) entries is exact for every diagonal matrix. Then show that, for every symmetric matrix, its variance is at most that of the Gaussian estimator, with equality exactly when the diagonal of \(\bA\) is zero.









NoteProposition: Diagonal Estimator



Let \(\bz_1,...,\bz_k\) be independent vectors with independent \(\pm1\) entries, and let \(\odot\) denote the entrywise product. Then \[
\begin{align}
\bd_k=\frac1k\sum_{l=1}^k\bz_l\odot(\bA\bz_l)
\end{align}
\] satisfies \(\mathbb{E}[\bd_k]=\operatorname{diag}(\bA)\).








WarningExercise



Expand entry \(i\) of \(\bz\odot(\bA\bz)\). Separate the diagonal term, use \(z_i^2=1\), and take expectations. Derive the variance of this entry and show that averaging \(k\) independent probes divides it by \(k\).

28.3 Random Sampling in Gaussian Elimination
the referenced section showed that eliminating a vertex joins all of its neighbors into a clique, and that the resulting fill makes sparse Cholesky expensive on large meshes. For the Laplacian of a weighted graph, the clique has an explicit form. Random sampling can replace it by a sparse graph that is correct on average.






NoteProposition: Elimination in a Graph Laplacian



Let \(\bL=\bD-\bW\) be the Laplacian of a connected graph with nonnegative edge weights \(w_{ij}\) and degrees \(d_i=\sum_jw_{ij}\). Eliminating vertex \(v\) produces a Schur complement that is again a graph Laplacian. Its graph is the graph of \(\bL\) with \(v\) removed, plus an edge of weight \(w_{iv}w_{jv}/d_v\) between each pair of neighbors \(i,j\) of \(v\).








TipProof





Place the eliminated vertex first and write its Laplacian in blocks as \[
\begin{align}
\bL=\begin{pmatrix}d_v&\br^T\\\br&\bB\end{pmatrix},
\qquad r_i=-w_{iv}.
\end{align}
\] For a connected graph with more than one vertex, \(d_v&gt;0\), so elimination is defined. Its Schur complement is \(\bS=\bB-\br\br^T/d_v\).
For distinct remaining indices \(i,j\), expand the entry: \[
\begin{align}
s_{ij}=-w_{ij}-\frac{(-w_{iv})(-w_{jv})}{d_v}
=-\left(w_{ij}+\frac{w_{iv}w_{jv}}{d_v}\right).
\end{align}
\] Thus a pair of neighbors acquires the additional edge weight in the statement. If either is not a neighbor, its contribution is zero. An existing edge receives that weight in addition to its previous weight.
We also check the diagonal through conservation of row sums. Removing column \(v\) from row \(i\) removes entry \(-w_{iv}\), so the row sum of \(\bB\) is \(w_{iv}\). On the other hand, \[
\begin{align}
\sum_j\frac{r_ir_j}{d_v}
=\frac{w_{iv}}{d_v}\sum_jw_{jv}=w_{iv}.
\end{align}
\] The row sum of \(\bS\) is therefore zero. Symmetry is preserved, and all off-diagonal entries are nonpositive. Its diagonal is consequently the sum of its incident edge weights, which proves that the whole Schur complement, not just its off-diagonal part, is the claimed graph Laplacian.









NoteProposition: An Unbiased Sparse Replacement for the Clique



Let vertex \(v\) have neighbors \(1,...,\delta\) with weights \(w_i=w_{iv}\) and \(d=\sum_iw_i\). For each neighbor \(i\), draw one neighbor \(j\) with probability \(w_j/d\) and, if \(j\neq i\), add an edge between \(i\) and \(j\) with weight \(w_i/2\). The Laplacian of the resulting graph of at most \(\delta\) edges has expected value equal to the Laplacian of the clique in the referenced result.








TipProof





For a unit edge between distinct vertices \(i\) and \(j\), let \(\bL_{ij}=(\be_i-\be_j)(\be_i-\be_j)^T\). The same matrix represents the edge in either order: \(\bL_{ij}=\bL_{ji}\). Define \(\bL_{ii}=\bzero\), matching the rule that a self-selection adds no edge.
For the draw associated with vertex \(i\), the event of selecting \(j\) has probability \(w_j/d\). Its contributed Laplacian is \((w_i/2)\bL_{ij}\). Taking its expectation gives \[
\begin{align}
\sum_j\frac{w_j}{d}\frac{w_i}{2}\bL_{ij}.
\end{align}
\] The Laplacian of a sum of edges is the sum of their Laplacians. Linearity of expectation therefore makes the expected total matrix \[
\begin{align}
\sum_i\sum_j\frac{w_iw_j}{2d}\bL_{ij}.
\end{align}
\] Terms with \(i=j\) are zero. For each unordered pair \(i&lt;j\), there are two equal terms, from the draws associated with \(i\) and \(j\). Their combined coefficient is \(w_iw_j/d\). Thus the expected matrix is \[
\begin{align}
\sum_{i&lt;j}\frac{w_iw_j}{d}\bL_{ij},
\end{align}
\] exactly the elimination clique. At most one edge is added per neighbor, so a realization has at most \(\delta\) edges before merging duplicates. This calculation proves unbiasedness only, not a bound on the error of a realization.









WarningExercise



Verify the referenced result for a vertex with three neighbors of weights \(1\), \(2\), and \(3\) by listing every outcome of the sampling and its probability.


An unbiased clique replacement does not by itself control the accumulated error or guarantee a useful preconditioner. A single sample may even disconnect the graph. A full approximate-elimination solver therefore needs concentration estimates and a carefully designed sampling scheme. For a connected Laplacian, the solve is restricted to the subspace orthogonal to \(\mathbf1\), or one vertex is grounded, since the full matrix is singular. Kyng and Sachdeva use a refinement of this sampling, eliminate the vertices in random order, and prove that the resulting solver reaches relative accuracy \(\varepsilon\) in \(O(m\log^3n\log(1/\varepsilon))\) expected time for a graph with \(m\) edges (Kyng and Sachdeva 2016). Practical implementations eliminate vertices in minimum-degree order, which reduces fill further but is not covered by the proof.

NoteDefinition: Initial Value Problem



An initial value problem (IVP) for a system of ordinary differential equations has the form \[
\begin{align}
\by'(t) = \boldsymbol{f}(t, \by(t)), \qquad \by(t_0) = \by_0,
\end{align}
\] where \(t \in [t_0, T]\) and \(\by(t) \in \fR^m\). The function \(\boldsymbol{f}: [t_0, T] \times \fR^m \to \fR^m\) is the vector field.








NoteExample



For \(y'=-y\) and \(y(0)=1\), the exact solution is \(y(t)=e^{-t}\). At time \(t_n\), the derivative suggests the linear approximation \(y(t_n+h)\approx y(t_n)+hy'(t_n)\). Replacing the exact value by the current approximation gives \[
\begin{align}
y_{n+1}=y_n-hy_n=(1-h)y_n.
\end{align}
\] This is forward Euler. Repeating the step gives \(y_n=(1-h)^n\). We can already ask two different questions: does it approach \(e^{-t_n}\) as \(h\) decreases, and does it decay for a fixed \(h\)?








WarningExercise



Compute four Euler steps with \(h=1/2\) and compare with \(e^{-2}\). Repeat with \(h=2\) and \(h=3\). When does the numerical sequence alternate in sign? When does its magnitude grow? Explain why the differential equation itself has none of this growth.









NoteTheorem: Picard–Lindelöf Existence and Uniqueness



Let \(\boldsymbol{f}(t, \by)\) be continuous in \(t\) and uniformly Lipschitz continuous in \(\by\) on the domain \([t_0, T] \times \fR^m\): \[
\begin{align}
\|\boldsymbol{f}(t, \bu) - \boldsymbol{f}(t, \bv)\| \leq L \|\bu - \bv\| \qquad \text{for all } \bu, \bv \in \fR^m,
\end{align}
\] where \(L &lt; \infty\) is the Lipschitz constant. Then the IVP has a unique continuous solution \(\by(t)\) on \([t_0, T]\).








TipProof





Integrate the differential equation to obtain \[
\begin{align}
\by(t)=\by_0+\int_{t_0}^t\boldsymbol f(s,\by(s))\,ds.
\end{align}
\] On an interval of length \(\Delta\) with \(L\Delta&lt;1\), define \(\mathcal T\bu\) by the right-hand side with \(\bu\) in the integrand. In the supremum norm, \[
\begin{align}
\|\mathcal T\bu-\mathcal T\bv\|_\infty
\leq L\Delta\,\|\bu-\bv\|_\infty.
\end{align}
\] Thus applying \(\mathcal T\) repeatedly contracts differences by the factor \(L\Delta\). Choose any initial continuous function \(\bu_0\) and define \(\bu_{j+1}=\mathcal T\bu_j\). With \(q=L\Delta&lt;1\), the contraction estimate implies \[
\begin{align}
\|\bu_{j+1}-\bu_j\|_\infty\leq q^j\|\bu_1-\bu_0\|_\infty.
\end{align}
\] For \(r\geq1\), the triangle inequality gives \[
\begin{align}
\|\bu_{j+r}-\bu_j\|_\infty
\leq\sum_{l=j}^{j+r-1}q^l\|\bu_1-\bu_0\|_\infty
\leq\frac{q^j}{1-q}\|\bu_1-\bu_0\|_\infty.
\end{align}
\] This tends to zero independently of \(r\). Completeness of the space of continuous functions in the supremum norm gives a uniform limit \(\by\).
Starting with a continuous function, the successive differences are bounded by a geometric series. The iterates converge uniformly to a continuous function. Uniform convergence and the Lipschitz estimate bound the difference of the integrands uniformly by \(L\|\bu_j-\by\|_\infty\). The difference of their integrals is therefore at most \(L\Delta\|\bu_j-\by\|_\infty\), which tends to zero. Hence passing to the limit in the integral gives a fixed point and hence a solution. If there were two fixed points, the same inequality would force their distance to be zero.
Use the endpoint value as initial data on the next interval of length at most \(\Delta\). The global Lipschitz assumption gives the same contraction factor on every interval. Finitely many intervals cover \([t_0,T]\) and give the unique solution. If \(L=0\), the map is independent of its argument and the conclusion follows directly.
The integral of the continuous function \(\boldsymbol f(s,\by(s))\) is continuously differentiable by the fundamental theorem of calculus. Thus the fixed point satisfies the original differential equation, not just its integral version. The finite-interval continuation is valid because the Lipschitz condition is global in the state variable.









WarningExercise



Let \(q=L\Delta&lt;1\) and \(\bu_{j+1}=\mathcal T\bu_j\). Prove \(\|\bu_{j+1}-\bu_j\|_\infty\leq q^j\|\bu_1-\bu_0\|_\infty\) and bound \(\|\bu_{j+r}-\bu_j\|_\infty\) by the tail of a geometric series. Explain how this establishes convergence and why the limit satisfies the integral equation. This is the contraction argument used in the proof.

29.3 Consistency, Stability, and the Discrete Gronwall Lemma
A numerical time-stepping method is convergent if the computed trajectory approaches the exact trajectory as the step size \(h \to 0\). We quantify error at each step and track its accumulation over a finite time interval.






NoteDefinition: Local Truncation Error and One-Step Defect



Let \(\by_{n+1} = \Phi_h(t_n, \by_n)\) denote an autonomous or non-autonomous one-step method. The local truncation error is the defect of the exact solution: \[
\begin{align}
\boldsymbol{\tau}_{n+1} = \frac{\by(t_{n+1}) - \Phi_h(t_n, \by(t_n))}{h}.
\end{align}
\] The method is consistent of order \(p\) if \(\|\boldsymbol{\tau}_{n+1}\| = O(h^p)\) as \(h \to 0\) for all sufficiently smooth solutions.


There are two common error conventions. The unnormalized one-step defect is the numerator in this definition and is \(h\boldsymbol\tau_{n+1}\). Our local truncation error divides it by \(h\). For forward Euler, Taylor expansion gives a defect of order \(h^2\) and a local truncation error of order \(h\). Both statements describe the same calculation.






NoteLemma: Discrete Gronwall Lemma



Let \(\{e_n\}_{n=0}^N\) be a sequence of non-negative real numbers satisfying the linear recurrence \[
\begin{align}
e_{n+1} \leq (1 + \alpha) e_n + \beta, \qquad n = 0, 1, ..., N-1,
\end{align}
\] where \(\alpha, \beta \geq 0\). For \(\alpha&gt;0\), \[
\begin{align}
e_n \leq e^{n \alpha} e_0 + \beta \frac{e^{n \alpha} - 1}{\alpha}.
\end{align}
\] For \(\alpha=0\), the corresponding bound is \(e_n\leq e_0+n\beta\).








TipProof





First let \(\alpha&gt;0\). Apply the recurrence twice to see the pattern: \[
\begin{align}
e_1&\leq(1+\alpha)e_0+\beta,\\
e_2&\leq(1+\alpha)^2e_0+\beta[(1+\alpha)+1].
\end{align}
\] Induction gives \[
\begin{align}
e_n\leq(1+\alpha)^ne_0+\beta\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}(1+\alpha)^j.
\end{align}
\] Indeed, multiplying the bound at step \(n\) by \(1+\alpha\) shifts all powers up by one, and adding \(\beta\) supplies the missing power zero. This is exactly the same formula for \(n+1\).
The finite geometric sum is \([(1+\alpha)^n-1]/\alpha\), because its ratio differs from one by \(\alpha\). The elementary inequality \(1+\alpha\leq e^\alpha\) implies \((1+\alpha)^n\leq e^{n\alpha}\). Since \(e_0,\beta\) are nonnegative, replacing the powers by this upper bound preserves the inequality: \[
\begin{align}
e_n\leq e^{n\alpha}e_0+\beta\frac{e^{n\alpha}-1}{\alpha}.
\end{align}
\] For \(\alpha=0\), no division by \(\alpha\) is allowed. The recurrence reduces to \(e_{n+1}\leq e_n+\beta\). Applying it \(n\) times gives \(e_n\leq e_0+n\beta\). Thus the separate zero case agrees with the limiting formula.









NoteTheorem: Fundamental Theorem of One-Step Convergence



Let \(\Phi_h\) be a consistent one-step method of order \(p\) applied to an IVP whose vector field \(\boldsymbol{f}\) is Lipschitz continuous with constant \(L\). Suppose the numerical increment function satisfies \(\|\Phi_h(t, \bu) - \Phi_h(t, \bv)\| \leq (1 + \Lambda h) \|\bu - \bv\|\) for some constant \(\Lambda \geq 0\). Then on the fixed interval \([t_0, T]\) with \(Nh = T - t_0\), the global error \(\be_n = \by(t_n) - \by_n\) satisfies \[
\begin{align}
\max_{0 \leq n \leq N} \|\be_n\| \leq e^{\Lambda(T - t_0)} \|\be_0\| + \frac{e^{\Lambda(T - t_0)} - 1}{\Lambda} \max_k \|\boldsymbol{\tau}_k\|,
\end{align}
\] where the quotient is interpreted as \(T-t_0\) when \(\Lambda=0\). Consequently, when the numerical and exact solutions use the same initial value, a one-step defect of order \(O(h^{p+1})\) yields a global error of order \(O(h^p)\).








TipProof





Express the error at step \(n+1\) by subtracting the numerical update from the exact solution: \[
\begin{align}
\be_{n+1} &= \by(t_{n+1}) - \by_{n+1} \\
    &= \left[ \Phi_h(t_n, \by(t_n)) + h \boldsymbol{\tau}_{n+1} \right] - \Phi_h(t_n, \by_n) \\
    &= \left[ \Phi_h(t_n, \by(t_n)) - \Phi_h(t_n, \by_n) \right] + h \boldsymbol{\tau}_{n+1}.
\end{align}
\] Taking norms and applying the triangle inequality yields \[
\begin{align}
\|\be_{n+1}\| \leq (1 + \Lambda h) \|\be_n\| + h \max_k \|\boldsymbol{\tau}_k\|.
\end{align}
\] The first bracket compares two numerical steps starting from different states. The second term is the defect of a step starting from the exact state. Keeping these contributions separate explains why one is controlled by the step map’s Lipschitz constant and the other by consistency.
Applying the Discrete Gronwall Lemma (the referenced result) with \(\alpha = \Lambda h\) and \(\beta = h \max_k \|\boldsymbol{\tau}_k\|\) gives \[
\begin{align}
\|\be_n\| \leq e^{n \Lambda h} \|\be_0\| + h \max_k \|\boldsymbol{\tau}_k\| \frac{e^{n \Lambda h} - 1}{\Lambda h} = e^{\Lambda(t_n - t_0)} \|\be_0\| + \frac{e^{\Lambda(t_n - t_0)} - 1}{\Lambda} \max_k \|\boldsymbol{\tau}_k\|.
\end{align}
\] Since \(t_n - t_0 \leq T - t_0\) and \(\max_k \|\boldsymbol{\tau}_k\| = O(h^p)\), the global error is bounded by \(O(h^p)\) when initial data are exact (\(\be_0 = \bzero\)).
If \(\Lambda=0\), use the zero-parameter case of the lemma directly: after \(n\) steps the added defect is at most \(nh\max_k\|\boldsymbol\tau_k\|\leq(T-t_0)\max_k\|\boldsymbol\tau_k\|\). This justifies the quotient convention without dividing by zero. For fixed \(T-t_0\), the exponential constants do not change as \(h\to0\), so a uniform \(O(h^p)\) local truncation bound gives the stated global order.

29.4 Euler Methods and Stability Functions






NoteTheorem: Stability Functions for Linear Test Equations



Applied to the Dahlquist test equation \(y' = \lambda y\) with \(z = h\lambda \in \fC\), the numerical update satisfies \(y_{n+1} = R(z) y_n\). The stability functions are: \[
\begin{align}
R_{\mathrm{FE}}(z) = 1 + z, \qquad
    R_{\mathrm{BE}}(z) = \frac{1}{1 - z}, \qquad
    R_{\mathrm{TR}}(z) = \frac{1 + z/2}{1 - z/2}.
\end{align}
\] The region of absolute stability is \(\mathcal{S} = \{z \in \fC : |R(z)| \leq 1\}\). Backward Euler and the Trapezoidal Rule are A-stable: \(\fC^- = \{z \in \fC : \operatorname{Re}(z) \leq 0\} \subseteq \mathcal{S}\). Backward Euler is additionally L-stable: \(\lim_{z \to -\infty} R_{\mathrm{BE}}(z) = 0\).








TipProof





Let \(z=h\lambda\). Forward Euler substitutes the old value into the derivative: \[
\begin{align}
y_{n+1}=y_n+h\lambda y_n=(1+z)y_n.
\end{align}
\] Thus its multiplier is \(R_{\rm FE}(z)=1+z\).
For backward Euler, the derivative uses the new value. Move its term to the left before dividing: \[
\begin{align}
y_{n+1}=y_n+zy_{n+1},\qquad
(1-z)y_{n+1}=y_n.
\end{align}
\] Whenever \(1-z\neq0\), the multiplier is \(1/(1-z)\). Similarly the trapezoidal update gives \[
\begin{align}
y_{n+1}=y_n+\tfrac z2(y_n+y_{n+1}),\qquad
(1-z/2)y_{n+1}=(1+z/2)y_n,
\end{align}
\] so its multiplier is \((1+z/2)/(1-z/2)\) when defined.
To prove the stability assertions, write \(z=x+iy\) with \(x\leq0\). Then \[
\begin{align}
|1-z|^2=(1-x)^2+y^2\geq1.
\end{align}
\] The denominator of backward Euler is therefore nonzero and \(|R_{\rm BE}(z)|\leq1\). For the trapezoidal rule, \[
\begin{align}
|1-z/2|^2-|1+z/2|^2
&=[(1-x/2)^2+(y/2)^2]\\
&\quad-[(1+x/2)^2+(y/2)^2]=-2x\geq0.
\end{align}
\] Its denominator is also nonzero on this half-plane, so \(|R_{\rm TR}(z)|\leq1\). This proves A-stability for both implicit methods.
Along the negative real axis, \(1/(1-z)\to0\) as \(z\to-\infty\), whereas the trapezoidal multiplier tends to \(-1\) after dividing numerator and denominator by \(z\). Thus backward Euler has the additional L-stability property and the trapezoidal rule does not. See Hairer et al. (1993) for these stability notions.









WarningExercise



Derive \(R_{\mathrm{TR}}(z)\) from the trapezoidal update. Write \(z=x+iy\) and compute \(|1-z/2|^2-|1+z/2|^2\). Use its sign to prove A-stability and to locate the points where \(|R_{\mathrm{TR}}(z)|=1\). Take the limit along the negative real axis to show that the method is not L-stable. Finally, apply both implicit methods to \(y'=-1000y\) with \(h=0.1\). Compare their first three values with the exact decay. Preservation of the magnitude of an oscillation does not imply exact phase: determine the trapezoidal phase change when \(z=i\omega h\).

31.3 Diffusion Maps and Nonlinear Manifolds






TipRemark



(From graphs to manifolds) When data points \(\bx_1, ..., \bx_N \in \fR^d\) lie on a low-dimensional manifold embedded in high-dimensional space, ambient Euclidean distances can obscure intrinsic connectivity or create shortcuts across a curved manifold. Diffusion maps define a random walk on a graph constructed over the point cloud and, under suitable sampling and bandwidth limits, approximate diffusion generated by a Laplace–Beltrami operator.








NoteDefinition: Diffusion Operator and Random Walk



Given point cloud \(\{\bx_i\}_{i=1}^N \subset \fR^d\), construct a pairwise affinity matrix \(\bK \in \fR^{N \times N}\) using a Gaussian kernel with bandwidth \(\delta &gt; 0\): \[
\begin{align}
K_{ij} = \exp\left(-\frac{\|\bx_i - \bx_j\|^2}{2\delta^2}\right).
\end{align}
\] Let \(d_i = \sum_{j=1}^N K_{ij}\) denote the row sums, and define \(\bD = \operatorname{diag}(d_1, ..., d_N)\). The row-stochastic diffusion matrix is \[
\begin{align}
\mathbf{M} = \bD^{-1} \bK.
\end{align}
\] The entry \(M_{ij} = K_{ij}/d_i\) defines the transition probability from point \(\bx_i\) to point \(\bx_j\) in a single diffusion step.








NoteTheorem: Spectral Decomposition of the Diffusion Operator



The matrix \(\mathbf{M} = \bD^{-1}\bK\) is similar to the real symmetric matrix \[
\begin{align}
\bS = \bD^{-1/2} \bK \bD^{-1/2} = \bD^{1/2} \mathbf{M} \bD^{-1/2}.
\end{align}
\] Let \(\bS = \bV \boldsymbol{\Lambda} \bV^T\) be the eigendecomposition of \(\bS\) with orthonormal eigenvectors \(\bV = [\bv_0, \bv_1, ..., \bv_{N-1}]\) and eigenvalues \[
\begin{align}
1 = \lambda_0 &gt; \lambda_1 \geq \lambda_2 \geq ... \geq \lambda_{N-1} \geq 0.
\end{align}
\] Then \(\mathbf{M}\) has real eigenvalues \(\lambda_k\) with right eigenvectors \(\boldsymbol{\psi}_k = \bD^{-1/2}\bv_k\) and left eigenvectors \(\boldsymbol{\phi}_k = \bD^{1/2}\bv_k\). The right eigenvectors satisfy \(\mathbf{M} \boldsymbol{\psi}_k = \lambda_k \boldsymbol{\psi}_k\), with \(\boldsymbol{\psi}_0=\mathbf1/\sqrt{\sum_i d_i}\) when \(\bv_0\) is normalized as above.








TipProof





All degrees are positive because every Gaussian affinity is positive. Thus the diagonal square roots and inverse square roots of \(\bD\) are well defined. Since \(\bK\) is symmetric, \[
\begin{align}
\bS^T=(\bD^{-1/2}\bK\bD^{-1/2})^T
=\bD^{-1/2}\bK\bD^{-1/2}=\bS.
\end{align}
\] Also \(\bD^{1/2}\mathbf M\bD^{-1/2}=\bS\), so the two matrices are similar.
We justify positive semidefiniteness of the Gaussian kernel rather than inferring it merely from positive entries. Write \[
\begin{align}
K_{ij}=a_i a_j\exp(\bx_i^T\bx_j/\delta^2),
\qquad a_i=\exp(-\|\bx_i\|_2^2/(2\delta^2)).
\end{align}
\] Expand the last exponential in powers. For every nonnegative integer \(l\), the kernel \((\bx_i^T\bx_j)^l\) is a Gram kernel: expand the dot product power into sums indexed by \(l\) coordinate choices. For real coefficients \(c_i\), the resulting quadratic form is a sum of squares of quantities \(\sum_i c_i x_{i,s_1}\cdots x_{i,s_l}\). It is nonnegative. The exponential is a convergent sum of these kernels with positive weights \(1/(l!\delta^{2l})\). Multiplication by \(a_i a_j\) replaces \(c_i\) by \(a_ic_i\), preserving nonnegativity. Thus \(\bK\), and consequently \(\bS\), are positive semidefinite.
The symmetric spectral theorem now gives an orthonormal eigenbasis \(\bv_k\) of \(\bS\) with nonnegative eigenvalues. The row sums of \(\mathbf M\) are one. For any eigenvector of \(\mathbf M\), selecting an entry of maximal magnitude in its eigenvalue equation gives \(|\lambda|\leq1\) by the triangle inequality. Also \(\mathbf M\mathbf1=\mathbf1\), so one eigenvalue is one.
Its eigenspace is one-dimensional. For a real vector with \(\mathbf M\boldsymbol\psi=\boldsymbol\psi\), a maximal entry is a strictly positive weighted average of all entries. It can equal their maximum only if every entry equals that maximum. Thus the vector is constant. Similarity to a symmetric matrix gives diagonalizability, so the eigenvalue one is algebraically simple as well. Hence all remaining eigenvalues are less than one.
To recover eigenvectors, use \(\mathbf M=\bD^{-1/2}\bS\bD^{1/2}\): \[
\begin{align}
\mathbf M(\bD^{-1/2}\bv_k)
&=\bD^{-1/2}\bS\bv_k
=\lambda_k\bD^{-1/2}\bv_k,\\
(\bD^{1/2}\bv_k)^T\mathbf M
&=\bv_k^T\bS\bD^{1/2}
=\lambda_k\bv_k^T\bD^{1/2}.
\end{align}
\] These are the claimed right and left eigenvectors. Their pairing is \(\boldsymbol\phi_j^T\boldsymbol\psi_k=\bv_j^T\bv_k=\delta_{jk}\). Finally the eigenvector of \(\bS\) for one is proportional to \(\bD^{1/2}\mathbf1\). Its squared norm is \(\sum_i d_i\), yielding the stated normalization of \(\boldsymbol\psi_0\). Diffusion-map normalization is discussed in Coifman and Lafon (2006).









NoteDefinition: Diffusion Distance and Diffusion Map Coordinates



The \(t\)-step diffusion operator \(\mathbf{M}^t\) represents running the random walk forward for time \(t\). The diffusion distance between points \(\bx_i\) and \(\bx_j\) measures the difference between their conditional transition distributions weighted by inverse degrees. Here \(\pi_i=d_i/\sum_jd_j\) is the stationary probability; using inverse stationary probabilities instead multiplies the following squared distance by \(\sum_jd_j\): \[
\begin{align}
D_t(\bx_i, \bx_j)^2
    =
    \|\mathbf{M}^t(i, :) - \mathbf{M}^t(j, :)\|_{\bD^{-1}}^2
    =
    \sum_{k=1}^{N-1} \lambda_k^{2t} \big(\psi_k(i) - \psi_k(j)\big)^2.
\end{align}
\] The diffusion map embedding of point \(\bx_i\) into an \(m\)-dimensional Euclidean space is \[
\begin{align}
\Psi_t(\bx_i) = \begin{pmatrix} \lambda_1^t \psi_1(i) \\ \lambda_2^t \psi_2(i) \\ \vdots \\ \lambda_m^t \psi_m(i) \end{pmatrix} \in \fR^m.
\end{align}
\] Under this embedding, Euclidean distance in \(\fR^m\) equals the truncated diffusion distance: \[
\begin{align}
\|\Psi_t(\bx_i) - \Psi_t(\bx_j)\|_2^2 \approx D_t(\bx_i, \bx_j)^2.
\end{align}
\]








TipRemark



(Diffusion time as a geometric low-pass filter) The factor \(\lambda_k^{2t}\) in the diffusion distance acts as an exponential low-pass filter on manifold coordinates. Because \(\lambda_k &lt; 1\) for all \(k \geq 1\), increasing \(t\) rapidly suppresses high-frequency modes associated with small eigenvalues, leaving only the dominant macroscopic geometric coordinates.








TipRemark



(Density normalization) The random walk of the referenced result depends on how densely the points sample the manifold as well as on its geometry. Coifman and Lafon separate the two effects by renormalizing the kernel before forming the random walk (Coifman and Lafon 2006). For a parameter \(\alpha\in[0,1]\), they replace \(K_{ij}\) by \(K_{ij}/(d_i^\alpha d_j^\alpha)\) and then compute new row sums and the row-stochastic matrix as before. In the limit of many points and small bandwidth, \(\alpha=0\) gives the classical normalized graph Laplacian, whose limit depends on the sampling density. The choice \(\alpha=1/2\) gives the generator of a Fokker–Planck diffusion, and \(\alpha=1\) recovers the Laplace–Beltrami operator independently of the density. The renormalized kernel is still symmetric, so the referenced result applies with the new row sums.

32.2 Krylov Subspace Approximation of the Exponential Action
To compute the action \(e^{t\bA}\bv\) for large sparse \(\bA\), we project the evolution onto an expanding Krylov subspace \(\mathcal{K}_m(\bA, \bv) = \operatorname{span}\{\bv, \bA\bv, ..., \bA^{m-1}\bv\}\).






NoteTheorem: Arnoldi Approximation of \(e^{t\bA}\bv\)



Let \(\bA \in \fC^{n \times n}\) and let \(\bv \in \fC^n\) be a nonzero vector with \(\beta = \|\bv\|_2\). After \(m\) steps of the Arnoldi iteration starting from \(\bv_1 = \bv / \beta\), we obtain the Arnoldi relation \[
\begin{align}
\bA \bV_m = \bV_m \bH_m + h_{m+1,m}\bv_{m+1}\be_m^T,
\end{align}
\] where \(\bV_m \in \fC^{n \times m}\) has orthonormal columns and \(\bH_m = \bV_m^* \bA \bV_m \in \fC^{m \times m}\) is upper Hessenberg. The Arnoldi approximation to the exponential action is \[
\begin{align}
e^{t\bA}\bv \approx \beta \bV_m e^{t\bH_m}\be_1.
\end{align}
\]








TipProof





We approximate the solution of \(\bu'(t) = \bA\bu(t)\) with \(\bu(0) = \bv\) within the subspace \(\mathcal{K}_m(\bA, \bv)\) by setting \(\bu_m(t) = \bV_m \bz_m(t)\), where \(\bz_m(t) \in \fC^m\). The residual defect of the ODE is \(\br_m(t) = \bu_m'(t) - \bA\bu_m(t) = \bV_m \bz_m'(t) - \bA\bV_m\bz_m(t)\). The vector \(\bz_m(t)\) contains time-dependent coordinates in the fixed basis \(\bV_m\). We cannot generally make every component of the full differential defect zero with only these \(m\) coordinates. Instead we require zero components along all basis vectors. This is the meaning of the following Galerkin condition.
Imposing the Galerkin orthogonality condition \(\bV_m^* \br_m(t) = \bzero\) yields \[
\begin{align}
\bV_m^* \bV_m \bz_m'(t) - \bV_m^* \bA \bV_m \bz_m(t) = \bzero \implies \bz_m'(t) = \bH_m \bz_m(t).
\end{align}
\] The initial condition \(\bu_m(0) = \bv = \beta \bv_1 = \beta \bV_m \be_1\) requires \(\bz_m(0) = \beta \be_1\). Solving the reduced \(m \times m\) linear system gives \(\bz_m(t) = \beta e^{t\bH_m}\be_1\). Multiplying by \(\bV_m\) recovers \(\bu_m(t) = \beta \bV_m e^{t\bH_m}\be_1\).
The small exponential is \(m\times m\), and \(\be_1\) has length \(m\). The final multiplication by \(\bV_m\) returns the vector to the original \(n\)-dimensional space. No \(n\times n\) exponential was formed. At an invariant-subspace termination the same reduced equation is exact for this initial vector.



An approximate trajectory can have the right initial value but fail to satisfy the differential equation. Its defect is the difference \(\bu_m'-\bA\bu_m\). This is computable from the projected solution. Subtracting the approximate equation from the exact one produces a forced linear equation for the error. Variation of constants then shows how past defects propagate to the final time.






NoteTheorem: Residual Representation and A Posteriori Error Bound



The defect in the differential equation satisfies \[
\begin{align}
\bu_m'(t) - \bA\bu_m(t) = -\beta h_{m+1,m} [\be_m^T e^{t\bH_m}\be_1] \bv_{m+1}.
\end{align}
\] Integrating the defect via variation of constants yields the exact error identity \[
\begin{align}
e^{t\bA}\bv - \beta \bV_m e^{t\bH_m}\be_1 = \beta h_{m+1,m} \int_0^t e^{(t-\tau)\bA} \bv_{m+1} [\be_m^T e^{\tau\bH_m}\be_1]\,d\tau.
\end{align}
\] In the Euclidean norm, if the operator \(\bA\) generates a contractive semigroup satisfying \(\|e^{\tau\bA}\|_2 \leq 1\) for all \(\tau \geq 0\), the error is bounded by \[
\begin{align}
\|e^{t\bA}\bv - \beta \bV_m e^{t\bH_m}\be_1\|_2 \leq \beta |h_{m+1,m}| \int_0^t |\be_m^T e^{\tau\bH_m}\be_1|\,d\tau.
\end{align}
\]








TipProof





Using the Arnoldi relation \(\bA\bV_m = \bV_m\bH_m + h_{m+1,m}\bv_{m+1}\be_m^T\), the ODE defect is \[
\begin{align}
\bu_m'(t) - \bA\bu_m(t) &= \bV_m \bz_m'(t) - \bA\bV_m \bz_m(t) \\
    &= \bV_m \bH_m \bz_m(t) - \left( \bV_m\bH_m + h_{m+1,m}\bv_{m+1}\be_m^T \right) \bz_m(t) \\
    &= -h_{m+1,m} \bv_{m+1} \be_m^T \bz_m(t) \\
    &= -\beta h_{m+1,m} [\be_m^T e^{t\bH_m}\be_1] \bv_{m+1}.
\end{align}
\] The approximation error \(\be(t) = \bu(t) - \bu_m(t)\) satisfies \(\be'(t) = \bA\be(t) - (\bu_m'(t) - \bA\bu_m(t))\) with \(\be(0) = \bzero\). The error equation can be integrated directly. Differentiate \(e^{-t\bA}\be(t)\), using the product rule and the fact that \(\bA\) commutes with its own exponential. The \(\bA\be\) terms cancel, leaving \(e^{-t\bA}[\bA\bu_m(t)-\bu_m'(t)]\). Integrate from zero to \(t\) and multiply by \(e^{t\bA}\). The initial error is zero because both trajectories start at \(\bv\).
Integrating via variation of constants: \[
\begin{align}
\be(t) = \int_0^t e^{(t-\tau)\bA} \left[ \bA\bu_m(\tau) - \bu_m'(\tau) \right] d\tau = \beta h_{m+1,m} \int_0^t e^{(t-\tau)\bA} \bv_{m+1} [\be_m^T e^{\tau\bH_m}\be_1]\,d\tau.
\end{align}
\] Taking norms with \(\|e^{(t-\tau)\bA}\| \leq 1\) and \(\|\bv_{m+1}\|_2 = 1\) yields \[
\begin{align}
\|\be(t)\| \leq \beta |h_{m+1,m}| \int_0^t \|e^{(t-\tau)\bA}\|_2 \|\bv_{m+1}\|_2 |\be_m^T e^{\tau\bH_m}\be_1|\,d\tau \leq \beta |h_{m+1,m}| \int_0^t |\be_m^T e^{\tau\bH_m}\be_1|\,d\tau.
\end{align}
\]
The last inequality requires \(t\geq0\) and the stated bound on the propagator at every intervening time \(t-\tau\). Without that contractivity assumption, the exact error identity remains valid, but its norm bound must retain \(\|e^{(t-\tau)\bA}\|_2\) under the integral. This distinguishes an exact representation from an estimate requiring an extra hypothesis.









WarningExercise



Repeat the norm estimate in the \(1\)-norm. Explain why the right-hand side acquires the factor \(\|\bv_{m+1}\|_1\) when the Arnoldi basis is Euclidean-orthonormal. For a column-conservative Markov generator, use uniformization to show that \(\|e^{t\bA}\|_1=1\), and explain why this does not imply Euclidean contractivity.








TipRemark



(Superlinear convergence of Krylov actions) Hochbruck and Lubich bound the error of the Krylov approximation in terms of the spectrum and the time step (Hochbruck and Lubich 1997). For a symmetric negative semidefinite matrix with eigenvalues in \([-4\rho,0]\), the error in approximating \(e^{\tau\bA}\bv\) from \(\mathcal{K}_m(\bA,\bv)\) is bounded by a constant multiple of \(e^{-m^2/(5\rho\tau)}\) when \(\sqrt{4\rho\tau}\leq m\leq2\rho\tau\). For \(m\geq2\rho\tau\) the bound decays superlinearly in \(m\). The relevant scale is therefore \(\rho\tau\), not \(\|\bA\|_2\) alone, and in the first regime the required dimension grows only like the square root of \(\rho\tau\). The same paper gives analogous bounds for non-Hermitian matrices whose numerical range lies in a known region of the left half-plane. The projected approximation also adapts to the spectrum seen from the starting vector rather than to the full matrix norm alone. Grid-independent convergence requires additional spectral or semigroup assumptions and should be checked for the discretization at hand.

NoteDefinition: Nonlinear Dynamics and Observables



Consider a discrete-time dynamical system \[
\begin{align}
\bx^{(k+1)} = \mathbf{F}(\bx^{(k)}), \qquad \bx \in \mathcal{M} \subseteq \fR^n.
\end{align}
\] Choose a function space \(\mathcal F\) invariant under composition with \(\mathbf F\). An observable is a scalar function \(g: \mathcal{M} \to \fR\) belonging to \(\mathcal F\). For an \(L^2\) space, the measure and assumptions ensuring that composition is well defined must be specified. The Koopman operator \(\mathcal{K}: \mathcal{F} \to \mathcal{F}\) is defined by \[
\begin{align}
(\mathcal{K}g)(\bx) = g(\mathbf{F}(\bx)).
\end{align}
\] The operator \(\mathcal{K}\) is linear on \(\mathcal{F}\), even when the underlying map \(\mathbf{F}\) is nonlinear.








NoteProposition: Linearity of the Koopman Operator



The Koopman operator \(\mathcal{K}\) satisfies \[
\begin{align}
\mathcal{K}(\alpha g_1 + \beta g_2) = \alpha \mathcal{K} g_1 + \beta \mathcal{K} g_2
\end{align}
\] for all scalars \(\alpha, \beta \in \fR\) and observables \(g_1, g_2 \in \mathcal{F}\).








WarningExercise



Evaluate both sides at an arbitrary state and expand \((\alpha g_1+\beta g_2)(\mathbf F(\bx))\). Which operation is linear here, and which map may remain nonlinear?








WarningExercise



For the state update \(F(x)=x^3\), compute the images of \(g_1(x)=x\) and \(g_2(x)=x^3\). Show that \(\operatorname{span}\{x,x^3\}\) is not invariant under composition with \(F\). Which further monomials appear if you repeatedly add the missing images? Explain why linearity of the operator does not imply that these two observables give an exact matrix model.








NoteDefinition: Koopman Eigenfunctions and Eigenvalues



A nonzero function \(\varphi \in \mathcal{F}\) is a Koopman eigenfunction with eigenvalue \(\mu \in \fC\) if \[
\begin{align}
\mathcal{K}\varphi = \mu \varphi.
\end{align}
\] Along system trajectories, Koopman eigenfunctions evolve linearly: \[
\begin{align}
\varphi(\bx^{(k)}) = \mu^k \varphi(\bx^{(0)}).
\end{align}
\] A sufficiently rich collection of Koopman eigenfunctions can provide coordinates in which part of the nonlinear dynamics evolves linearly. Such a collection need not exist globally, and finite dictionaries generally give only a projected approximation.

34.4 Continuous Generator Estimation






TipRemark



(Discrete-to-continuous inversion) Given an EDMD estimate of the discrete Koopman operator \(\widehat{\bK}_h \approx \exp(h\mathcal{A})\) over a sampling interval \(h\), the continuous generator is recovered via the principal matrix logarithm: \[
\begin{align}
\widehat{\bA} = \frac{1}{h} \operatorname{logm}(\widehat{\bK}_h).
\end{align}
\] If \(\widehat{\bK}_h\) is real and has no eigenvalues on the closed negative real axis \((-\infty,0]\), then its principal matrix logarithm exists and is real. A matrix with eigenvalues on this branch cut may possess other logarithms, but it does not possess the principal logarithm used here.








NoteTheorem: Sensitivity of Recovered Decay Rates



Let \(\lambda_i &lt; 0\) be a continuous eigenvalue of the generator \(\mathcal{A}\), corresponding to a discrete Koopman eigenvalue \(\mu_i = \exp(h \lambda_i) \in (0, 1)\). The relation between discrete and continuous eigenvalues is \[
\begin{align}
\lambda_i = \frac{1}{h} \ln \mu_i.
\end{align}
\] The sensitivity of the continuous eigenvalue with respect to perturbations in the discrete eigenvalue is \[
\begin{align}
\left|\frac{d\lambda_i}{d\mu_i}\right| = \frac{1}{h \mu_i} = \frac{1}{h \exp(h \lambda_i)}.
\end{align}
\] For fast decaying modes where \(-\lambda_i \gg 1\), \(\mu_i \to 0\) exponentially fast. Consequently, the noise amplification factor diverges: \[
\begin{align}
\lim_{-\lambda_i \to \infty} \left|\frac{d\lambda_i}{d\mu_i}\right| = \infty.
\end{align}
\] A tiny statistical fluctuation \(\Delta \mu_i\) in data-driven EDMD induces an exponentially magnified error \(\Delta \lambda_i\) in the recovered rate.








TipProof





The scalar decay relation is \(\mu=e^{h\lambda}\) with \(h&gt;0\). For a positive multiplier it can be inverted as \(\lambda=h^{-1}\ln\mu\). Differentiation gives \[
\begin{align}
\frac{d\lambda}{d\mu}=\frac1{h\mu}.
\end{align}
\] Substitute the exponential expression for \(\mu\): \[
\begin{align}
\left|\frac{d\lambda}{d\mu}\right|=\frac{e^{-h\lambda}}h.
\end{align}
\] When the negative decay rate becomes increasingly large in magnitude at fixed \(h\), \(-h\lambda\to+\infty\), so this derivative grows without bound.
The exact perturbation calculation also shows the range of the linear estimate. If \(\mu+\Delta\mu&gt;0\), then \[
\begin{align}
\Delta\lambda
=\frac1h[\ln(\mu+\Delta\mu)-\ln\mu]
=\frac1h\ln\left(1+\frac{\Delta\mu}{\mu}\right).
\end{align}
\] For \(|\Delta\mu|/\mu\) small, Taylor’s formula \(\ln(1+s)=s+O(s^2)\) gives \[
\begin{align}
\Delta\lambda=\frac{\Delta\mu}{h\mu}
+O\left(\frac{(\Delta\mu)^2}{h\mu^2}\right).
\end{align}
\] Thus the derivative describes amplification of small absolute multiplier errors. It is not a valid linear approximation when the error is comparable to the multiplier. If the perturbed multiplier is nonpositive, the real scalar logarithm is unavailable, which is a separate obstruction.









WarningExercise



Differentiate \(h^{-1}\ln\mu\) and substitute \(\mu=e^{h\lambda}\) to obtain the stated amplification factor. Then compare the exact change \(h^{-1}\ln(1+\Delta\mu/\mu)\) with its linear approximation. What happens as \(\Delta\mu\downarrow-\mu\)? Distinguish this loss of a positive multiplier from a large but locally predictable perturbation.
